      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>• oDOU GAL I:S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOU R ONCE TRIED ALWAYS USED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE.</p>
        <p>You may remember that in the Spring I gave you as my opinion that three-quarter coats would be much in vogue. That was the state of things for some time, but really this kind of coat was nob universal. But that we have the three-quarter coat with us is an accomplished fact, and therefore I will give you an example which could be carried out in castor-coloured cloth, in blue of the marine shade, or in a good forest green. The revers fold below the throat, and after that are pushed to one side in two stiff tabs. Stitotings of steel-grey silk pipe the edges. There might be a high storm- collar cut in scallops and made in one with the shoulders. Another coat which symbolises the vogue that we ?light to Bee in the coming by-and- by a guod dss,l is the long-trained coat of the spring curtailed to the three•quarter length.</p>
        <p>Events in the milltnery world go to prove that tulle and gauze are not going to be dethroned; they are even to be allowed to mix with fur and other materials as substantial. Crepe de Chine, too, is being used for hat-strings and to make large, soft-looking choux, and as this material has more substance about it than the really flimsy mous- seline de soie, it should have a trial. Strange flowers still prevail, and we have lilies, edelweiss, gardenias, and as well the old-fashioned cottage- garden flowers, such as dahlias and hollyhocks. The newest rosette is composed of velvet, literally wound round and round until it achieves a huge size, and looks as exactly like a cabbage as it is possible for anything in millinery to do.</p>
        <p>It is a fat t.aely recognised that ear.l are ever useful to their fortunate possessors, hut except in the matter of trimming; I cannot say that any great variety exists for one to choose from. The latest style is to have the aides of the cape cut rather short, in fact to just below the elbows of the wearer. In float the garment descends to just below the waist. One I saw the other day had a artistic and pretty trimming. The chief design was rows of black fur. The whole of the front of the cipe whore the hocks were placed was lined witb fur, which continued round to the back but gradually rose to nearly opposite the shoulders. Within a couple of inches was another row, and this ran parallel all the way round the cape. The garment had a storm collar, and this was also trimmed with fur.</p>
        <p>In all the latest fashions it will bc noticed that there is an immense amount al labeur absorbed in the millinery, and it may Ls iEwested with much beauty by the careful selectioa of Ow colours. I saw yesterday a hs,t with a crown of hyacinth blue velvet, much stitchrd, and the brim formed of little plaitings of mature, tuiquoise blue, and hyacinth blue velvet, a pair et irridescent blue, green, and mauve wings wavinc: loosely at one side, held by a diamond buckle—Parisian diamond, of course.</p>
        <p>The hideous coverings so seen on mantel. shelves has tempted me to speatz of suttable ways of levering that article of ftuniturs and ornament. The most satisfaaory as well as the simplest way of draping a manteisheif is carried cot with a straight strip of material I.lf.fi(liently long and wide to hang well over the she if in tront and at the ends, this being secured by means of a few tacks along the hl-ic edge to a board an inch (or more if desired). ionger Wan the shelf ou which it rests. This board should have the front corners rounded off slightly, and, unless the drapery is of thick stuff indeed, ought to be covered tightly with coarse flannel or baize. It, should be made of thoroughly seasoned timbr, or the heat of the fire will cause it to warp. The drapery should be of some soft but fairly substantial fabric that hange well.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNUt II</p>
