      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The visit of the Grand Duke Michael, present heir to the Russian throne, to Balmoral, would scum to lend weight to the rumour that his betrothal to Princess Margaret of Connaught is already an accomplidaed fact. Princess Margaret who will be eighteen in January next is a vi ry charming, fascinating girl, and extremely like her nictner—which is high praise indeed. The Grand Duke Michael is a bright, clever young man. and wr-11 fitted to fill the exalted position that may one day fall to his lot, should the Czarina fail to present her husband with a son and heir. _ .</p>
        <p>According to an authority on the subj et there are severity-one marriageable princesses of the blood Royal, in Europe, while the number of princes of an age to nter into the holy bonds of matrimony is but ferte- seven ! What will become of the surplus princesses, is a matter that has evidently caused the eminent statisti- cian much food for thought. As a matter of fact tl.ere ' are only two courses open to these unt.rtunate damsels.. They must either coetract morganatic marriages, or become St. Catherines. The opening meeting of the Church Congress was de- voted to women and girls, a sort of gigantic "Mother's meeting" which filled even the huge Albert Ball. The Bishop of Loncic.n delivered an address upon husbands and wives, a subject which naturally gave an eppor. u- nity for many ii tie pleasantries. Be admitted that hus- bands were stupid, and a great nuisance in the house, but they had to be put up with,and he advised wives to cultivate unfailing cheerfulness, as the sure-t guarantee of peace in the home. This is not always eagy, and there are some people to whom the other persons cheer- fulness is sometimes more irritating than the display of a little ill temper, but on the whole there is no doubt that the Bit h- p'e advice is most excellent. Dr Creighton however seemed to regard it Limself as a counsel of per- fection, for he related the story of the old Scotchwoman upon whom a friend called to congratulate her on her daughter's marriage. " Yes " said the old lady, " she's cc mfortehly settled, but there's just one thing about it —she ea.e'e endure her husband, but there's eye a some- thirg." While the Maine% have been discoursing at the Church Congress, the English Claw( h Union has been discrv•sing Matters as, from its own particsiler point of view, it is coesidered they aught to be. Neither gathering seems to hold out any pre spect of an early termirrition to the controversy between the Ritualists, and the anti- Ritualis!s. Perhaps a Lint might be taken from a Lon- don rector who recently edvr rtisecl in one of the church papers for a curate wizb "Views Broad, High Church, Evangelical, Catholic." No one could be mere accommo- dating than a cleric who contained in himkelf such cone- prehensive;and unduly Ciffe ring sentiments. Lord Hali- fax said in his address to the English Church Union that he does not desire separation from the state, but he wants absolute fie edom, and since the Lamhe th decisic n against incense and processional lights, he seems more than ever in revolt against the Bishops. But Lord Hali- fax cannot have it both ways. and he cannot see that if the Church of England is not to be governed by the State, and the Bishops, the only alternative is Its discs- tatlishment. Tlitre has be n a discussion lately as to whether the proper ale for a woman doctor is not" doctress." We have actor and "actress," acd why not the same in the medical profession. A new difficulty is created by the recent appointment of a lady to a Nonconformist pulpit in the Congregational Church at Brotherton. One can- not but marvel whether the parishioners will speak of her as the " minister "or the " ministress." It is no new thing fur ach rgyman's wife to run her husbands' parish,but it is quite an innovation for a woman to take charge of a church altogether. According to the" Hospital," nurses like war just as much as the youngest sabaltern who is eager fir pro- motion. In an interview with the matron of the Guard's qespital, that lady is reported to have said that active seilkviee has the same" attraction for a woman, ae for a man." To tkis she added, " I've seen a good many veers' service in South Africa, in Egypt, and on the Wet Coast of Africaond I only hope it will ceme te my turn to go to South Africa again this time, if there be war." There are seventy nurses on the staff each of whom is connected with the Army Hospital Corps and the views of Miss Gray, the matron, appear to be shared by every one of them. With war in the air, one invariably sees red in the world of fashion, and the present occasion is no excep- tion to the rule. The shade approved by all the leading builders of gowns is a deep toned grenat, known to the initiated as " automobile." It is undtubtedly a becom- ing shade but its vogue is not likely to be a very long one, and the limp canious would therefore do well t) avoid it. The many picturesque items in the uniforms of the neer, ere being seized upon by the modlstes too, and adapted to the needs of fashionable women. The same idea prevailed in Napoleon's day. There were bodices nee soldier's tunics, ard skirts trimmed en suite. Then what a vogue garibildis' scarlet shirt gave to blouse. A garihaldi was first a red flannel or cloth jacket or shirt. Then the colour changed, and the ahap e remained, next the [quince altered, and still the name remained, At last Garibaldi himself havirg eone out of fashion, tnese shirts were no longer nemed after him, awl the shirt and blouse as we know them new came into vogue.</p>
        <p>W. have heard a good deal of "death in the lamp." but , aecr-rding to a correspondent of a London p4per, the use of oil as an illuminant is fraught not only ‘‘ith peril to inclvictuals but to the whole world, which is being "drained to death by the rstraction of oil from the earth." When sufficient cil has been taken out the cc:Ater says. there will be a collapse, the earth will fall in and the subsrquent proceecling,s will interest us no more. The subj-Tt is one which may concern the Prophet Baxter, but the danger, like that of the exhaur- lion of the coal surplies, seem to be a liti ii too rrmate to trouble the present generation. It was stated in a ease bran i rrcently at Lambeth County Court (London) that certain ornamental screens were made for 91c1. each thc cost of material being 6d., bet the retail price of the scre.,n to the public was 24. 'IIAI. each. That seems to be a pretty fair profit, hut the els,. is typical rf many. For example everyone has foetid that fish is dearer at the seaside than it is a inland towns, yet it is certain that only a small share of the high prices charged gOPS to the men who c'ually catch the fish. The majority rf farmers and fruit growers are in much the same positir n, and it is not the public who gain by the small amount which they receive for their produce. The foreigner '-ho at a certain Southern waterinq place was charged 64. a lb. for apples and plums during the fruit season this year, might s ppose that fruit growing was a profitable business, but he wouli b, quite as much mistaken as if he were to be- lieve that dairy farming is lucrative tar cause he lad to pay 14i. each for newly laid eggs all through the summer. MADGE.</p>
        <p>BANK QUAY BitETISII INFANTS' SCHOLLS.</p>
        <p>CONCERT IN AID OF - THE CHILDRENS' TREAT. A concert, the proceeds of which .'ere in aid of the day school childrens' treat, was held in the new schools. Thewlis-street, on Monday evening. There was an exceedingly largo attendance, every available seat being occupied. Alderman H. Roberts presided, and in the course of his introductory remarks said that when he received the invitation to take the chair, he had no diffi- culty whatever in complying with the re quest for many reasons. One reason was, because the request was made by a lady, and he found it was exceedingly difficult to refuse a request made by a lady. (Laughter.) When that re quest was made by one who was engaged in such a useful and noble occupation—that of teaching the young, he felt doubly bound to comply with it. He was told that the object of that concert was to proeide funds for a treat for the children who attended the school. The object was an exceedingly goed one, and he thought that judging from the attendance there would be ma difficulty in raising the money. He hoped the treat would be a satisfactory one to those who gave it and to those who had to receive it and that the occasion would be one that weu!cl be re- peated annually, successfully for very many years. He was glad to have theeopportunity of being present and hoped that would only be the predecessor of many other occasions on which he should have the plea ure of meet- ing his friends in that very excellent building. (Ap- plause.) The entertainment was then proceeded with, the first item on the programme being a musical drill by the school children under the direction of Miss Cowap. Later on the scholars gave a "Lullaby Song " in a very creditable manner, and Waster Harry Glover sang "A Flower from my angel mother's greve." Several well-known local artistes appeared and their contributions were well received. Messrs. Pearson and W. H. Paeton were loudly applauded for a violin and piano duet, and Mr. Pe arson contributed several violin solos " Cavatina," " Moto Perpe,uose." and "Dances from Henry VII." in his best style. Mr. Levi Richard- son, tenor, sang" Where'er you walk" and" 0 promise me "in a manner which called forth the warm applause of the aedier.o,. Mrs. Pearson was announced to sing, but could not appear owing to indisposition. Her place was taken by M:ss Richy, who was very successful with her Contributions, "The Children's Home "and " The Zuyder Zee." Messrs. Davenport and G. Clay sang a couple of duets" Love and War " and " Ths moon loath raised " with good effect. Mr. Whittaker gave two whistling solos "Should he upbraid " and " Ave Maria" in a praiseworthy manner,being recalled for tee former. The humorist was Mr. A. Ground., who met ie iih great success. , His first contribution was a song entit'ed "I didn't get a wink all night" for which he was vigorously encored. He concluded the entertainment with his ven- triloquial sketch "Muse and Mimicry "which provsked much amusement. In proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman and artistes, Mr. Edward Wallington, head- master of eeople's College, said it was exceedingly kind of Alderman Roberts to come and preside over the enter. tainment seeing that he bad so many engagement', both of a public and psivate character. It was only another Prolf of the interest he took in everything that was happening in the town, and the valuable services tie had rendered to the town on many occasions, bad led' to the honourable position be held in the Town Council, and also to the honour that was so soon to be conferred upon him. He (Mr. Wallington) was also asked to propose a vote of thanks to those ladies and gentlemen who had contributed to the entertainment by their musical per- foimances. They most not forget the little children who had done their part so well. He hoped soon they would enjoy the treat that was to be provided for them) out of the proceeds of that concert. (Applause.)—Mr. Monks seconded, and eluring the course of his observations expressed his pleasure that the Council were about to appoint Alderman Roberts as Mayor. He hoped that when the town put upon him he responsibility of chief magi.- ' trate that he would be able to prevent a recurrence of ' the scene that took place in the Council not long ago. He (the speaker) was sure that with the tact that Ise exhibited, be would fi'l the position worthily during the coming year. (Applause.) The vote of thanks was carried unanimously, and Alderman Roberts briefly re- sponded. He said ti at he was pleased to Pee that People's Cellege, his old school, maintained its p'ace as the first school of its kind in Lancashire. He owed a debt of gratitude to Peeple's College that he could never re- pay and whatever usefdre se there had been in his career was due in a great measure to the tuition he received there. (App!ause.)--The proceedings were brought to a close by the singing " God save the Queen."</p>
      </div>
