        <head>. , t„ • -.... . -- --___-._ ---- ---.- --,....- ' – • ' – ~, • • ''''''l • -,,,,,,, – • 1 4-;':'''. '.'. •64' .4iff't _;- ~' – -',-•,:•,..-'-- • •:,-;i,',.): -_4,:--; ' ---.'-,A ,-• K '''';-• 7.,:i. -'''" '' .- ". – fii.; \, A it `• ',--'.'4' A.... --, -Z. .-. •.,,,,;41 – • -....4-`..: ' ; -„,',,,at'5...:` !, -. • , '_ • , f ..,,,,.....-. --,,e-,.... '-•••• . • ~-. ~ • – • – .</head>
        <p>. , t- „ • -.... . -- --___-._ ---- ---.- --,....- ' - • ' - ~, • • ''''''l • -,,,,,,, - • 1 4-;':'''. '.'. •64' .4iff't _;- ~' - -',-•,:•,..-'-- • •:,-;i,',.): -_4,:--; ' ---.'-,A ,-• K '''';-• 7.,:i. -'''" '' .- ". - fii.; \, A it `• ',--'.'4' A.... --, -Z. .-. •.,,,,;41 - • -....4-`..: ' ; -„,',,,at'5...:` !, -. • , '_ • , f ..,,,,.....-. --,,e-,.... '-•••• . • ~-. ~ • - • - .</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES,</p>
        <p>ETC.</p>
        <p>NIACMILLA.N AND CO.—ID "Miranda of the clay " (6e), Mr. A. E. N. Mason has produced a novel waich is a very fitting successor to his earlier works, "The Courtship of Morrice Buckler," and " The Philanderers," both of which have attained a well-merited popularity. " Miranda of the Balcouy" resembles neither to any great extent, and yet does not greatly diffsr from either. Mr. Mason has a style of his own—not unlike Mr. Anthony Hope's— and he writes with an easy and dexterous pen His characters are net very profound, like those of Meredith or even Crawford, but they are true to life, true to teemselves, and invariably interesting to the reader. Tee principals in the novel under notice are Miranda Warriner, and Luke Charnock, the former a beautiful girl who loses tier husband after a brief but loveless wedded life, and the latter a bachelor of that unsentimental and practical type to be found among men who have worked their way to a position of some consequence over very stony paths. We do not wish the word unsen- timental to be construed into harshness or lack of feeling, because Lake Charnock was neither one thing nor the other, but simply a man—a gentle- man—whe had been tried by experience and not found wanting or weak. The plat, such as it is, becomes base!: on the early return to life of Miranda's husband, Ralph Warriner, a captain in the army and no credit to the service, who dis- appears after the committal of some misdeed, but eventually arranges matters so that his death shall seem eenclusivelv proved to his relatives and thei world at large. In the meantime he pursues the I profitable avocation of gun-running between this country and Tangier until the turn of circumstances. discloses his identity. This discovery complicates matters for the supposed widow and her lover, Charnock. The latter proves his devotion by hunt- ing up the missing Ralph Warriner,whose misdeeds result in his being sold as a slave, from which un- happy lot his wife's lover kindly rescues him. Events then hasten rapidly to a close, and the sequel accords with the desire of the reader. The interest of the story is well-sustained from first to last by a plenteousness of incident, and a clever dialogue, added to a plot which is not sufficiently involved to be at all puzzling.—" Stalky &amp; Co." (65.) is the last to hand of the new issue by Messrs. Macmillan of Kipling's prose works, The story was so recently in the pages of one of our most popular periodicals that it is hardly necessary to say much about it. Those who have read "Stalky and Co." —and are of the people who can appreciate Kipling —know well that there is no sample of light litera- tura extant which they would rather have on their bookshelves than this delightful story of three schoolboys—for that is what Stalky, Beetle, and McTurk are. Their doings, and misdoines, their sayings and soliloquies, are unique and inimitable. Fun at anybody's and everybody's expense is their aim. and one's sides ache with laughter before one is half way through the book. We owe Kipling more than we can esti- mate for such a work as " Stalky and C 0.," with its healthy humour and marvellous realism.— Phe present peculiar pasition of the Established church in its relatioaship to the country and to its own members makes the re-issue of the Eon. Arthur Elliot's book "The State and the Church," (23 61), particularly appropriate. The subject is dealt with from the point of view of the citizen, the volume forming one of a series, the object of which is to present a general view of the rights and res- ponsibilities of the English citizen, and explain the legal position and working of the great institutions which constitute our political system. The book as items; to do with the differences within or ! without the state church, and the author applies himself solely to answering the questions : " What is the position of the State Churches of England and Scotland before the law ?" " What is involved in the expression, Established Church "? The church crisis of the last year or two has caused these questions to be revived in many quarters, and tha lion. Arthur Elliot's treatise se ould be read with interest.—ln Macmillan's sixpenny series "The Forest Lovers," by Maurice Hewlett. has recently been issued. It is a romance of a high order from ' the standpoint of general interest, and the fortunes of the hero and heroine will be followed with eager- ness by those not already acquainted with them. Pro4per is Gal and laoult la Desirous are somewhat out of the ordinary run of the characters who usually occupy the places of honour in a work or fiction.—" The First Violin," by Jessie Fothergill, and published in the same series, has also left the I press. CASSELL AND Co.—These publishers are now ) issuing their" Gazetteer of Great Britain and Ire- and," in 61. weskly parts, thus affording a valuable opportunity of acquiring this unique publication it a low price. Tee Gazetteer includes numerous illustrations and sixty maps—Anew magazine for the Sabbath " Sunday Chimes,' has been started in penny numbers. It much resembles the "Quiver " in character, and containes some excellent articles and stories by eminent contributors.—" Cassette Magazine " seems to improve monthly, and is cer- tainly a credit to its popular editor, Mr. Max Pem- berton. Its contents are always judiciously varied I and of the bast quality. In this rconth's number "The British Embassy in Washington," well illus- trated, and written by Elizabeth L Banks holds a prominent, place, while among the other articles of a special nature are " Flashlight Photography" by F. M. Holmes ; "Broken Bones," an interview With Prof. Atkinson; and "What was Shakespeare like,?" by John Munro. The six complete stories will be- guile many a half hour pleasantly away, while the serial by Guy Boothby. "A Maker of Nations," is promising to be one of the best of this popular novelist's works.—The" Quiver" will commence a new volume next-mouth, and some attractive new features are promised.—The Sixpenny weekly edi- tion of " Through the Dark Continent," by 11. M. Stanley is now complete, the eighteenth and last number having just been issued. NEWNES AND Co.—" The Strand Magazine" for October is an excellent number, and in addition to ! one or two series of stories which have claimed in- terest for some time, contains the opening chapters ! of a tale in three parts by A. Conan Doyle, entitled "The Croxley Master." Other items of the custom- ary character and quite up to the standard of the ! magazine are plentiful.—A firm place in the love of the literary public seems to have been secured by" The Wide World Magazine," which has fol- leseed up De Rougemont's remarkable narrative with the more authentic but equally interesting ! story of Mr. Charles Neufeldt's captivity in Omdur- man. Among the short stories of adventure are " Buried A live in an Avalanche " ; i• Lost in VeSaVillS"; "Some Stanley Relics"; and "The:Terrible ! Adventure of Emil Hahl, the titles of which speak eloquently for themselves.—ln the October "Captain" is told the story of Phil May's success. !C. B. Fry gives" Hints to, School Captains "•, and , R. S. Warren Bell tells" Tales of Greyheuse," No. 1 'being entitled "Sir Billy." WARD, LOCK, AND Co.—We have never yet found the" Windsor Magazine " behindhand or lacking in enterprise, and this montli it publishes a very read- able and well-illustrated article on the origin and history of the America Cup by G. F. Lorimer. Pictures of the" Shamrock "rued " Columbia " are included. W.T. Stead writes pleasantly on "The Cape to Cairo Railway," and other articles are" A Talk with Lucas Malet" ; " Dellis Mill and its Memories of Gladstone" ; "The Great Meteor Shower of 1899." etc. Stories by lan Maclaren, Fred Vshishaw, Rosalind Chambers, E. Nesbit, G. R. Falconer, and J. Hickory Wood help to make up a very attractive number. SMITH, ELDER AND CO. —The October "Cornhill" has some pleasing contributions to its pages. R. Barry O'Brien writes a racy article on Curran,which will be found to be one of the most attractive items in the magazine. The author alludes to Curran in the following terms :—" Curran is remembered not ae a judge, but as a good advocate,a staunch patriot a brilliant wit, and a brave man. A Protestant, he was ever the friend of Catholic liberty. A constitu- tional lawyer, he defended the United Irish rebels with zeal and courage. A charming companion,full of fun and frolic, bubbling over with joyousness and mirth, the very soul of good humour and good fellowship, he was the delight of every society in which he moved. A true friend, a chivalrous foe, the ehampion of the oppressed, the enemy of the little tyrants who overran hislcountry, he was admired y the profession which he adornesLand be-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>3 -4,</head>
