      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>EXAMINER- FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER- FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13, 1899.</p>
        <p>loved by the people whom be tried to serve." "Colonial Memories," by Lady Broome ;" At the Reporters' Table," by Michael M'asnagh ; and "Captain Cook's First Log in the Royal Navy," by Profossor Morris, also form features of interest in his capital number. ARCHIBALD CONSTABLE ANT, CO —We heartily welcome "The Prince's Story Book" (6s ), edited by George Leurence Cromme. It is a handsomely bound, well-printed volume of historical stories collected out of English literature in illustration of the reigns of English monarchs from the time of the conquest to the reign of Her Nlajesty. Two previous volume's of similar character, viz : "The King's Story Book," and "The Queen's Story Book" have been isseed,and with this tkird collection, the history of the country is comprehensively covered. It will be seen at once that this class of literature has much in its favour, for not only do the stories interest readers young and old, but they possess the merit t be- ing true, and have the additional usefulness of improving one's acquaintence with the most romantic events in English history. The amount of romance that history contains is only to be real- ized by the perusal of a work of this type. Here we have it presented to us in the most acceptable form, for each story is unaltered just as it appears in the novel from which it is extracted. Another consideration is that the stories are also representa- tive examples of English literature, "cameos from writers of acknowledged pre-eminenee," and, furthermore if it becomes necessary to refer to some event in history,what better reference volume could we have than one of these edited by Mr. Gomme. Toe idea which has been responsible for these publications is an admirable one, and we have no doubt whatever that they will be popular with all classes of readers. " The Prince's Story book" opens with a selection from Lord Lytton's " Harold" entitled "The War Princes of the North " and describes that stirring event,the battle of Stanford bridge, in September 1066. Mest of the succeeded reigns are represented by a story of their time, and the last of the lot illustrates the charac- ter of toe times of our own greet Q teen. Mr. H. S. Banks is the illustrator of the work, and he has piodneed some excellent and faithful pictorial representations of the stirring incidents which are related. WITHERBY AND CO.—We hasten to commend the 88th number of" Lean'a Royal Navy List," which is truly described "A complete encyclopxlia of our first line of defence." There is no more authentic record of naval history extant, and every care is used to bring each new issue up to date: It is worth mentioning that the work deals with the career of some 10,000 naval officers. Attention is specially directed by the proprietors to the records of the War and Meritorious Services of Officers of the Royal Navy (only to be found in this work), and of the Royal Marines and Royal Naval Reserve; also to the details of civil appoint- ments held by officers in the retire 1 lists. In the list of ships will be found recorded the victories of famous battleships bearing the same name, form- ing an interesting and unique record of the war services of the ships. T. NELSON AND SONS, EDINBURGFL—These pub- lishers announce the issue of a new library of stan- dard works in a unique form, the special feature bsing that the volumes are of pocket size, printed on large type, on India paper. Each volume measures only 6* inches by 4 inches, and is just over half a inch in thickness, though containing 800, or more, pages. Yet there is no transparency of the paper. It is quite opaque,and the type looks exceedingly clear and neat. In fact, neatness ie the predominant feature of the "New Century " library We have a copy of" Pickwick Papers "to hand, this being the first volume of the new issue. It is most tastefully bound, in addition to fulfilling all that the enterprising publishers claim for it. The price is 2s.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. This Board met in the Beard Room at the Workhouse on Wr-dnesday morning. Mr. A. J. Jolley presided and there wra also present : Mrs Clay, Mrs Rielsten. Rev W H DrammonO, Rev T R Matthews, Rev J U Riddette, Dr Patterson, Messrs William Hickman, A Jollev, G Pilling, T Fl Ashcroft. H HibbrF, J Billinge, TB S Davies, It Forshavr, J W Knowles, William Houghton, J Clarke, A Brooks, with J C Sutton (clerk). S Sutton (assistant c!erk), C U Ward (w.)rkhouse master), A Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools), and W Owen (architect). PIDGATE SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the ahoy,: comalittee it wag resolved that Mi-s E M. Gallowly be appointed assistant school- mistress in place of 1111. is Hazal, at a salary of £3O with apartments, rations, &amp; The minutes were carried. SUGGESTIONS BY THE REV. J. q. RIDDETTE. Ths Rev. J. H. Rionfirm asked if the committee had considered the desirability of issuing a pamphlet, simply worded, illustrated with photographic blocks, so that they might be able to put information into the hands of the people who came before them whose children it was desirable to get into the Padgate Schools ? He should alsoliike to know whether:they were required by an order of the Local Government Board to send children to the workhouse for quarantine purposes, which was a serious hindrance in the way of filling up the vacanci,s in the Padgate Schools. fie had tried to persuade the people in his district that the children would not be brought into contact with workhouse associations, but as long as the present rule maintained one's ground was cut away frcun him. The CHAIRMAN remarked that the matter had not been formally considered by the committee as a committee, but he had mentioned it 'incidentally to one or two of the memters, and it was his intention to bring it before the :committee at an early date. With regard to the matter of the quarantine, they had no option as a Board. An order on toe point existed, and if Mr. Riddette's suggestion was carried out it would involve the erection of additional buildings at Padgate. The Board would not feel disposed to do that at present. At the same time it was a matter that required careful consideration. It was desirable that the childr.n should be kept as much as possible from the associations of a house like this, although the House Committee did their best in the matter. Mr. DAVIES : The children don't mix much with the inmates of the workhouse. The Rev. J. H. Rtunerrn : But you have to consider the prejudice that exists against,the workhouse. The subkct then dropped. COMMUNICATIONS FROM THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said he had received a communication from the Local Government Board asking for particulars with reference to the remaining part of the £25,000 pro- posal to be borrowed on the new infirmary. He had replied to the communication. The Local Government Board had also wriVen for a list of the allowances made by the Board up to the present time under the Poor Law Officers' Superannuation Act of 1898. He had written in reply that no inch allowances were made by the Board. THE APPROACHING POOR LAW CONFEHENCE. Mr. PILLING moved that the Chairman, the two vice- Chairmen, with the Clerk be appointed to attend the Poor Law Conference at Manchester on the 27th and 28th of this month. It any other members of the Board were desirous of attending at their own expense they could be recogniied as representatives if the Board if they gave in their names before the 20th inst. Mr. HICKMAN said he would like to be present to hear their Chairman read his paper. Mr. BILLINGS said it was rather a pity the ratepayers could not send the whole Board as a deputation. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN : The ratepayers might not think so. (Laughter). The resolution was then carried. A NEW TENDER ACCEPTED.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC read a communication from Messrs. Skel- ton's, Limited, which read " Having misinterpreted your specification on one particular point we beg your permission to withdraw our tender." The CHAIRMAN said that on the advice of the Clerk he would move a reselution that Messrs Skelton's be allowed to withdraw their tender. He did not think it was 'expedient to go into the reasons why Messrs. Skelton's withdrew their tender. Mr. Billinge seconded. Mrs. CLAY withdrew from the meeting at this stao of the proceedings. The Rev. W. El. DRUMMOND said he felt very strongly on the subject. They were now getting very close on the winter. He thought it would be better to do the work in the summer, and it would be wiser to have fresh tenders next February. He would move an amend- ment to that effect. The CHAIRMAN hoped it would not be hastily carried. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE asked how it came about that Messrs. Skelton's Limited had mis-interpreted the speci- fication ? Whose fault was it? The CHAIRMAN: They have blundered; but we re- lease them from any remonsib lity. Mr. Oveme said he was ordered to accept the estimate if it was satisfactory. Be did not accept it formally but wrote to Messrs. Skelton's that he had orders to accept their tender providing it was satisfactory to them when they hal examined the work. They sent a representative to the institution and found out that they had omitted one grate and other minor matters which made a difference of £ll in the contract. He (Mr. Owen) pointed out to them that they must go on with it at the price stated or withdraw. It was entirely their mistake. Mr. Asmcnorr asked for the next lowest tender. Mr. BILLINHE thought it would be advisable for the Board to aecept a tender now. He did not think it would be possible for them to get the stoves any cheaper next February. The prices of material were going up. The CHAIRMAN 94id as Chairman of the souse Committee he would like to say a word on the sub- ject. He did not wish to rake up past things but it was only fair to state that if the first recom- mendation of the House Committee had been accepted they would have got the work done for £2O less and there would have bean a saving of £5 a month in fuel. The object of the House Committee in putting the stoves in was to save the extremely extra- vagant consumption of coal that went on in the work- house. slle thought he would be well within the mark when he said that they would effect a saving of £5 a month and if they put it at that figure the saving would have been £2O. Mr. Billinge was perfectly right in his I anticipation that the prices were likely to go up higher, lsecause material was increasing in price. He thought it would be wise for the Board to accept one of the tenders submitted. Mr. KNOWLES rose to a point of order and called at- tention to the fact that the Chairman had said that the work could have been done for much less. He wished Ito point out that it was not this job at all for which a tender was previously received but something in the contra.etor's own mind at that time. If the Chairman would adhere strictly to the resolution and not waste the time of the Board unnecessarily it would he better. - The CHAIRMAN: "Order, order.' Mr. KN )WLES It.is not a question of order. The CHAIRMAN said he had pointed out what seemed to him strong reasons for not accepting the amendment. He thought he was quite within his right in saying so. His position as Chairman of the Board did net deprive him of his right of expressing his opinions. He should not accept the position if it did, and he did dot think he would be worthy of their confidence if he refrained from expressing his opinions. The amendment was then put to the meeting and seven voted foreit. The CHAIRMAN, without asking if there were any to the contrary. said it was lost. The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND thought it would be well when the Chairman put the resolution in that way if he would explain that any persons who abstained from voting were voting against it. Sometimes people at- stained from voting and did not know they were voting against. The CHAIRMAN said he had acted according to the standing orders. The standing orders provided that every member who kept his seat voted: The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said that was not always understood. Mr. OWEN then read out the other tenders, which were opened at the last meetine when Messrs. Skelton's was accepted. They were as follow :—Messrs Williams and Clay, £114; Mr. J. S. Garland,£lls •. Mr. J. Dolan, £137 55.; Messrs. Ca'dwelle. Limited, £l4O. Mr. ASHCROFT moved that the tender of Messrs. Wile liams and Clay be accepted. Mr. HIBBERT seconded. Mr HICKMAN asked if Messrs. Williams and Clay would be likely to accept it at the price stited, or whether the rise in the price of iron would effect it. Mr. OWEN thought not. Mr. HICKMAN said there had been two advances in iron quite recently. Mr. KNOWLES asked whether in case they accepted the tender of Messrs. Williams and Clay it would effect Mrs. Clay's position on the Board. The next tender to it was ro little different in price, he did not know whether it ought to be accepted. The CLERK said that if Mrs. Clay had an interest in the firm which offered the tender, she would be dis- qualified from acting as Guardian. If she had not a personal or direct interest in the profits of the firm she Was not effected. Mr. KNOWLES said the opinion of the "Justice of the Peace" was different. The CHAIRMAN said the Clerk was their justice ot the peace for the time being. (Laughter.) Mr. KNOWLES moved that the tender of Mr. Garland be accepted, but on the suegestion of Mr. Riddette he withdrew it and the tender of Messrs. Williams and Clay was accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BAD FOR A GARDENER.</head>
        <p>BAD FOR A GARDENER.</p>
        <p>A gardener has no use for rheumatism. In his calling he wants muscles that will work like a perfectly-ad- justed and well-oiled clock. A man may do some clear- headed: thinking even when disease has put the " der- bies" on his legs and arms ; and authors have dictated a lot of good stuff under those conditions. But those of us who have got to move about and earn our bread and cheese with hammers, shovels, hoEs, and pruning knives must keep the machine in as good form as we can. "Everybody knows that," say you. Yes, but does everybody know how t 3 stave off influenza, rheumatism, and other ailments which lay people on the shelf, while the rent goes on and debts pile up? Do they know, I say? No, they don't. If they only did, and acted on the knowledge, we shouldn't have life's music spoiled with groans and sighs and vvailings. Here's another lesson on the subject :—Please really° it as patiently and at- tentively as its importance deserves. The gardener above referred to is Andrew Jolley. He works at Stalybridg,e, and was working there when his trouble came upon him. Now, it is in the nature of things that a gardener's life should be an healthy one. His bu4iness is out of doors, and so far as I can say, he is not obliged to expose himself to very bad weather. as 'bus drivers are and many others. He has the benefit of the sun and the fresh air, and the clean, wholesome seciety of the grassss, fruits, and flowers. All the same, Andrew—in April, 1898, it was—had a bad attack of influenza, so miserably carnmon in the spring and autumn. lie got over it after a bit, but he was n)t his old self again. It left him a mean legacy of weakness and debility. His appetite held off and the food be ate lay dead and undigested in his stomach. He complained of aching pains across the chest rnd around the body, and was hardly better off than when down with the influenza. Then rheumatism took up the story and ground the poor fellow down lower than ever. It established itself in his shoulders and gave him so much pain that he had scarcely an hour's rest straight away day or night. The doctor attended him faithfully, but in spite of all the medicinca, rubbing oils, and embrocations, he went on growing worse awl worse. It was a bee look out for Andrew, and he stood to lose hle place if the ail merit ha.ng en too long. For gardens that require attention can't wait for gardeners to get well. Weeds are rank and strceg, albeit men may be frail and feeble.</p>
        <p>Finding himself so better for what had ben done, Andraw went to the Convalescent Home at Bridlington, where he stayed six weeks. The change and rest were good for him, bet on returxing he soon relapsed into his former state. In two words ho was as bad as ever.</p>
        <p>In a short account of his illness, furnished by his L; mother, the lady says :—" We were in despair what to do for Andrew. Everything the doctors could do,and all we ourselves could think of, had been done; yet here he wac, with the full bloom of summer upon us, in no condition to do a hani's turn of work. In June, 189 C, a friend who Came along said the right medicine to cure Andrew Wa3 Mother Seigel's Syrup. He told of what it had done in cases he knew of—whE r e nothing else had any effect whatever. This friend was so full of faith and confilence in the Syrup that Andrew said he would try it. lam glad to tell you that the re- sult was beyond all we would have believed possible • It seemed to go to the spot. One bottle stirred up his appetite and made his victuals dirst and agree with him. In a few days h? was stnnger for what he ate and the rheumatism was easier, and in less than a month it was entirelv gone.</p>
        <p>"Soon oftervrards he went back to St klybridge to work, and pre :wady wrote us that Mother Seigel's Syrup had completely cured him, and that he was in the best of health. You are at liberty to publish what I item said and refer anyone to me. Please accent our thanks."—(Signed) (Mrs.) MARIA JOLLEY, Mears Ashby, Northampton, January 26th, 1899. The foregoing facts are youche 1 for by Mr. J. H. Simcoe, Postnaaater at Mears Ashby, and by others. But how to ward off rheumatism and other ailments— that was the question we startad with. Keep the diges- tion in order, the bowels free, and the bloo3 pure. The secret of health and long life i 3 in those three things</p>
        <p>And the agent to do the work is Mother B,!lgel's Syrup."</p>
        <p>THE GLUW-SPEY AND STRATHMILL DISTILLERIES Situated in the fiaest Whisky-producing district of Scotland are the property of W. and A. Gilbey, and the hisdcv made from Hon', -grown Barley only. "Glen-Spey." 3s. 6d nd —Strathnsill." 3s. Si. sold by W. ant A. a-ILB EY gents in every towa NEW AUTUMN MILLINERY: Choice Selection of FURS. The latest in Jackets. and Waterproofs.</p>
        <p>Bionsas, Dress Material, tcr... now sizowitig—W. T. WILLIAMS, Smalley-street, and Legit-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FALL FROM A 'BUS. SAD FATALITY TO A PENKETII LA.DY,</head>
        <p>FALL FROM A 'BUS. SAD FATALITY TO A PENKETII LA.DY,</p>
        <p>THE CONDUCTOit</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse conducted an inquiry at the Con- servative Club, Penketh, on Tuesday evening, res- pecting the death of Mrs Mainley, wile of Mr. John Mainley, painter and decorathr, Penketh, who died on Sunday morning from injuries ustained by fall- ing from a 'bus under circuaistancee previously reported.—Mr. Browne, solicitor, Warrington appeared for the relatives of the ueceased. and Mr. Walter Cobbett, solicitor, Manchester, for Mr, Edward Connhr, the owner of the 'bus. The Corr.ner read the police report ot the accident, which seated that the deceased lade' went to Warring- ton by the nine o'clock 'bus and returned at eleven o'clock on ttorday, the 30'h alt. The 'bus was in charge of Joseph Mollinson, 27, Golborne-street, War- rington, driver, and John Clarke, 44. Elizabeth-street, Warrington, gu ad. On arriving at Brooktield-terrare at 11-30 the 'bus stopped to allow passengers to get off, a passenger leaving at the other side of the Liberal Club. The deceased was heard to say," I Clink I will get off here to save him stopping again." She procesNded t) gat eft being seated at the top of the 'bus, and she was heard to call for the driver to wait a minute : I' will get off here. Mrs. Main!oy was about tr get off but the driver took no notice. There was a crash o something falling, and the passengers saw the deceasedt lying on the ground. She was unconscious, She was carried into her own house, and a doctor sent for, who attended her until her death, which took place on Sun- day morning last. Jnhn Mainley, master painter. Brookfie'd-terrace, Penketh was then called and said that the deceased was his wife, and she was 62 years of age last birthday. On the 30th ult. she went to Warrington by the Warrington 'bus, and returned by it. Be saw her lying on the ground after the accident. At that time the 'bus had been pulled uo. She was bleeding. Be obtained assist ance and carried her into the house. He at onco sent for Dr. Murray. She died last Sunday morning. She had gone by the same 'bus for years to Warrington, and had returned 'hy it. The driver and guard knew her habits, and wh.re she had to be put down. She never spoke after the acci&amp;nt, as she was unconscious the whole of the time. The driver was on the 'bus when he saw him after the accident. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browns: He called in Dr. Lanzdale and Dr. Bowden, as well as Dr. Murray, to see his wife. The driver MAison called upon him after the aecident, and witness told him he had " made a bad j 31.1) of it," and the reply was" She had plenty of time to get off." Wit- ness then remarked "That is a very poor compliment to me." _ _ .</p>
        <p>The Coroner : He didn't go into details as to how the accident happened ?</p>
        <p>Witness : NO, only that.</p>
        <p>_ By Mr. Cobbett : The last stop was opposite the butcher's. His wile slipped off the pavement when in Warrington, but that was owing to its greasy condition. She was not subject to attacks of giddiness, and she had never fainted to his knowledge during their married life.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: lam going to assume that she fell off the 'bus and died in consequence of the fall. Joseph Mollinson, the driver of the'bus, stated that he was in the employ of Mr. Edward Connor, of Warring- ton. He had been a driver and conductor for two years. The deceased went to Warriogten with him on the morning in question andfretuined to Penketh with him. She sat on the top of the 'bus. When near to Mr.Main- ley's he stoppfd the 'bus to put down a passeneer named Wilkinson, who had some steel and tools with him. He then drove on by order of the conductor,who was on the parapet. He could see him. The conductor looked each way and said" All right," and witness went on. From where he sat he could see everyone on the top of the 'bus. He could see the people behind him. When he started off he did not Fee Mrs. Mainley, and he could not tell where she was. He took the conductor's word that everything was right and drove on. He never started the bus until he had received the order from the conductor to do so. After he had started someone com- menced to call cut" Stop, stop," and he did so. He had then proceeded three or four yards. It was ascer- tained that someone was on the floor. He could not say how the deceased got there. There were steps oa one side of the 'bus only. By Mr. Browne: He did not hear Mrs. Mainley say " Wait a minute. I want to get off." No one called out that she was on the ground. The conductor gave him the signal to start by saying " Right." He had paid a visit to Mr Mainley since the accident, and had told him how sorry he was it had happened. He told him there was plenty of time for her to get off. They could not " stand there all the afternoon." The Coriner : He means to say that as the 'bus had been standing so long there was ample time for all to get off. That is what I take it he wishes to convey. John Clarke, a youth, said he was in the service of Mr. Connor as conductor of the 'bus in question. It ran between Warrington and Penketh. He had been the conductor about three months; altogether he had discharged similar duties for two years and six months. He knew the deceased lady, who went to Warrington on the 'bus once or twice a week. The 'bus had been stopped about four minutes when the accident occurred. He assisted ) get the steel off the front part of the 'bus. He said " Right, driver," and he started. He was then at the side isf the 'bus in the road. The Coroner : Could you see that everything was right from that place P—Witness : No, sir. The Coroner: Were you in such a position that you could see that everything was right, and that it was right for the driver to go on ? I think it was right, sir, At the time you give the signal " all right" to the driver, if there had been a person half way down the stairs you could not have seen her P—No, sir. If you had gone to the back of the'bus before you gave the signal, you would have been able to tell whether anyone was going up or down, would you not ?—Yes, sir. You don't blame the driver for starting when he got the signal ?—No, sir. What do you say about yourself? Do you say you ought to have gone to the back of the 'bus, or aid you do right in giving the signal where you were ?—I ought to have gone to the back of the 'bus. Did you know Mrs. Mainley had fallen off the steps? —I was going to get on at the letck when I saw she was falling off the third step backwards. If you bad given the signal from the hack of the 'bus you would have seen her on the steps, would you not? —Yes sir. By Mr. Cobbett : He did not hear anyone call out that she was going to get off. Toe 'bus waited long enough for nearly everyone to get off, if they wished to do so. By the Jury: Only Mr. Wilkinson got off at the time of the accident. The bundle (the steel) was fast in the brake and could not be got off properly. The deceased fell when she was on the third step from the bottom. The Ceroner at this stage said be thought the jury had heard sufficient evidence. The case was quite clear from what they had heard the driver and conductor say. They had been very honest is the manner in which they had told their story. They had not tried to disguise or keep back anything. There could be no criminal negligence. There might be negligence of a civil character, but that was not for the jury to thrash out. That was for Mr. Browne and Mr. Cobbett to thrash out in another Court. Alice Baker, called by Mr. Browne with the permis- sion ef the Coroner, said she was the wife of Thomas Baker and lived at Penkotb. She was on the 'bus on the morning in question. She heard Mrs. Mainley ask the driver to stop, as she was going to get off. The 'bus had been stopped for Mr. Wilkinson to get off. She was not prepared to say the driver heard her. The Coroner : There would be a rattling of the steel. It would be to the driver's advantage to put Mrs Mainley down rather than stop again. By Mr. Browne: She admitted it was quite possible the driver did not hear the request to stop. She did not think the 'bus was stopped for four minutes. Henry Wilkinson said the 'bus stopped near his house. It stepped two minutes before it started again. No one got off the 'bus at the time except himself. H e had three rods tied together, and it took him about minute to get them off the 'bus. There was time for thea deceased to get down if sbe rose to do so at the same time he did. There was no indication that she wanted to get off the 'bus when he got off. Witness added that the 'bus was without a bell, and that there was no whistle blown. -</p>
        <p>_ The Coroner then briefly summed up the evidence, re- marking that the case was quite clear and that the jury would be able to form an opinion without much consi- deration. Their only duty was to see whether there had been any criminal negligence. There could be only two persons involved—the driver and the conductor. The driver was entitled to go on when he had the signal from the conduc'or that Everything was right. The conductor said " All right," and he went on. Mrs.Ba.ker was not in a position to say he heard the request of the deceased to the driver to stop, and one would scarcely think, looking at the matter from his own point of vie w, that he would be se foolish as to go on. Good drivers dk d not like to pull up their horses more than they could help, as it was not good for the animals. Even if the conductor was put on his trial for manslaughter, and the jury were sitting as a grand jury, they would cut the bill. The matter had to be thrashed out in another court, and it was better to let that be the case. That was his view of the matter. The jury, if they wished, could add a Pres ,ntment to their verdict. The jury were left by themselves to consider their verdict, and in about 20 minut,s, when the Coroner, witnesses and others were admitted. The Foreman handed to the Corner a written verdict. It was to the effect that the death was accidental, bute they added the following presentment :—" That th accident would not have happened if the guard had not unfortunately, given the driver orders to start before he had taken his proper position at the end of the 'bus." The Coroner also added that the jury desired to ex- press their sympathy with Mr. Mainley and his family in their sad bereavement.</p>
