        <p>THE CO3r OF THE NEW INFIRMARY. MORE CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM OWEN read the minutes of he Infirmary Committee, as foil iws :—At a meeting of the Committee on Friday, the 6;11 o,:tober, at the Boardroom of the Workhouse, after discussion it waB. decided to recom- mend the Board of Guardians to accept the estimate of Mr. C. W. Davenport for the new boiler and engine house and chimney, amounting to £l,BBO, after making deductions from it for sundry items pointed out by the ' architect, £320 24 91. leaving the net tender to be accepted at £1,559 7s 31 The architect gave the follow- ing figures to show what estimates had already been accepted :—C. W. Davenport. building, £23,266 13i ; , Knelt and Cochrane, heating and hot water, £1,750; Warrington Electric Light and Supply Company, £2,778 ; Merryweather and Company, fire hydrants, £76 24 3d ; Messrs Tinkers. boilers, £1,125 ; C W Davenport, boiler-house. Sze., £1,559 17.1 33 ; Apple- ton water main, £95 ; total, £30,650 12s 114 ; architect, engineer, and clerk of works, £1,532 108 ; grand total, £32,183 24 11d- In addition there will be steam pipes and connections, and the subsidiary mains carrying Winnick water to buildirgs. It was re- solved to recommend the following members to form a sub-c Ammittee for the purpose of furnishing the new in- firmary and administrative block, with authority to visit some institutionsiof a similar character of the most modern type with a view to decide on what is the most desirable I—Mrs. Cliy, Mrs Elelsten, Rev. W H Drum- mond, Messrs. G Piling, A J Jolley, and A Jolley.—The Clerk intim Eted that the outlay of, say,£l,ooo for furni- ture and £5OO for roads and laving out of lands can be met out of revenue without risk of increase of the ordinary sixpenny Union Rate. He further advised that if the outlaye included in the contreee did exceed by only a few hundred pounds, the £32,000 loans, such ex- cess may be similarly provided out of revenue. It was recommended that the Clerk be instructed to proceed as he may think advisable to borrow the remainder of the loans of £32,000 upon the most reasonable terms obtain- able.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM EIOURKTON, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the committee, said the committee hoped to be able to do the furnishineout of the current revenue. The committ?.e very vigilantly looked over the items for the engine and boiler houses and coach house and struck out several items which had considerably re- ductd the estimate. With reference to the appointment of a sub-committee it was thought time that one was appointed to consider the question of furnishing the new 'nil rmar</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING secondei the adoption of the minutes. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Thenley. J. H. RIDDETTE said it seemed sail the Infir- mary Committee had recognise] the custom of taking little excursions at the Union's expense. It was proposed to expend £l,OOO on furnishing. The very lowest estimate for the Infirmary was going to cogt the ratepayers at the least between £34,000, and £35,000. A great deal of that amount would have to be paid by the Warrinton ratepayers and be thought there were firms in Warrington of sufficient importance and ability and experience who were able to do what was necessary in furnishing the hospital without going out- side. (Hear, hear.) lie saw no reason for the appoint- ment of a sub-3ommittee. He was sorry his colleague the Rev. W. H. Drummond, was appointed to serve on the sub committee, but evil communication corrupted good manners, (Laughter.) He understood that Mr. Drummond had a very poor notion of sub-committee in the past.</p>
        <p>Mr. FORSH&amp;W thought it wag very necessary in order- ing furniture for a small sub-committee to be formed.</p>
        <p>the CHAIRMAN &amp;aid there cluld be no discu4sion on the aopointrnen: of a sab-committee. Thl appointment of a sub-committee rested entirely with a committee and there coald be no discasiion on it at th 3 Bard meet- ing. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. RIDHETTE laid if that was the case all he could do would be to move that the minutes be re- ferred back. The CHAIRMAN said he could not allow a discussion on that point. The CLERK said Mr. Riddette might move that the portion of the miant-s he ot4jected.to be not confirmed.</p>
        <p>- The Rev. J. H. RIDDErrE -sail he would then move an amendmlnt to that effect.</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN pointed out that the sub-committee was only appointed by the casting vote of the Chairman. The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said the purpose of the sub-committee was that it should draw out a plan for the furnishing of the new hospital and recommend it to the committee and afterwards to the Board. In order that the work of furnishing might be thoroughly well done the small sub-committee was necessary. It was the intention of the sub-committee to visit several modern hospitals at Mancivster and Liverpool in order to see how they were furnished and thus obtain some ideas as to what was nelessary for the new hospital. Mr. HICKMAN thought the appointment of a sub-com- mittee was a reflection on the capaoilitios of the other members of the committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND stid it was quite possible that twenty meetings of the sub-committee would be necessary before they had formulated a proper scheme. It was net work for a large committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. FORMANT said he had known many instances where deputations had saved more than they had cast the ratepayers. Mr. HorroirroN slid he would be glad to hear a sug- gestion from Mr. Riddette as t• 3 any better course to pursue. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE said they had an architect who could advise them.</p>
        <p>The CEIAIRMAN said the Board had already decided that the architect should not advise them as to the furnishing of the Infirmary. Mr. HISBERT asked how long it would be before the Infirmary was finished. The CIIkIRMAPT said he was unab'o to answer that question at present. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE ultimately withdrew his amendment and the minutes were passed. The Board decided to accept the contract of Messrs. Killick and Cochbran for £5lO for the steam piping required in connection with the boilers, etc. The Board then rose.</p>
