        <p>E LABEL TELLS THE PRICE. 'JO 10r*"*.VOOL,V*;3$1</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING VISIT OF THE ROYAL WELSH LADIES' CEIOIR.</p>
        <p>On Monday next, Warrington is to be favoured with a visit, for the first time, of the Royal Welsh Ladies' Choir, who will give a concert in Parr Hall the proceeds of which are to assist the funds of the St. John Ambulance Association. The good work which this association is doing is sufficient in itself to commend it to the support or the public of War- rington. The choir has gained the reputation of being the fiuest ladies choir in the world, and to Madame Clara Novallo Davies the celebrated pros fessor of singing in London belongs the credit of organising and training this body of vocalists. Madame Davies has achieved signal triumphs with many of her pupils. The choir are unJer the special patronage of Her Majesty the Queen, before whom they have aupeared by command. They have also sung freqaently at concerts with Madame Patti in Lon- don and the provinces, at the London Ballad Con- certs, itc., and gained the first prize for choral sing- ing at the World's Fair, Cnicago. At the close of the concert at Osborne Madame Davies was presen- ted to the Qaeen, who ha I qaite a long chat with her, and paid her many charming compliments about the singing, and the perfect control she has over the choir. Equally successful was the choir on the occasion of the beautiful serenade which they ten- dered Governor and Mrs. McKinley at the letters apartments in the Neil House, Colornbus. Under the direction of Madame Clara Novell° Davies, the young ladies sang five of their choicest selections, opening with the "Spanish Gipsy," on the rendi- tion of which they were awarded the first prize at the World's Pair, in 1893, and closing with the stately; "March of the Men of Harlech,” the war hymn of their native country. They also rendered the " Bells of Aberdovey," and with peculiar sweet- ness "Annie Laurie" and " Home, sweet home." As the last notes of the latter 'died away, Govenor McKinley stepped out of an interior room with an armful of beautiful white roes, which he proceeded to distribute one to each of the ladies as a special token from Mrs. McKinley. Both Mrs. McKinley and the Governor were delighted with the soulful music of the famous voices, and were profuse in their expression of appreciation of the pleasant visit. From such a distinguished choir. a musical treat may be safely anticipated by those who attend the concert on Monday evening. In addition to this attraction, two capable artistes in Miss Back- sheen Wood (solo violinst) and Mr. Emlyn Davies, A.It.C.W. (baritone) will contribute to the even- ing's enjoyment. The prices of admission are : Reserved chairs (body of hall and centre gallery), 35.; side galleries and second seats (body of hall) reserved, 23.; other seat, is. Money will not be taken at the doors till 7.30; but ticket holders will be admitted at least 15 minutes earlier.</p>
