      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ANOTHER BURNING FATA.LITY AT WARRINOTON</head>
        <p>ANOTHER BURNING FATA.LITY AT WARRINOTON</p>
        <p>A FATAL PRANK. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner,held an inquiry at the Police Station on Tuesday, into the circum- stances attending the death of Arthur Tingle, aged eight years, son of John Tingle, a puddler, residing at 210, Winevick-roao,who died in the Infirmary on Saturday from burns.—John Tingle, 210, Winwick- road, said deceased was Die son. Witness was not at home when the accident occurred.—Elizabeth Tingle, mother of the deceased, said that at 8.20 on Saturday morning, deceased was in the kitchen with his younger brother. He had no clothing on with the exception of his shirt. She had occasion to go upstairs, and she had not been up long, when she heard screams. On going downstairs she saw deceased was in flames.—Thomas Tingle, aged seven years, brother of deceased, said that os Saturday morning his brother got up the chimney to get some soot to black his (witness's) face, when his shirt caught fire, He screamed and his mother came and put the flames out.—Nuree Williams said deceased was admitted to the Infirmary at nine o'clock on Saturday morning suffering from burns all over his body. There was no chance of his re- cevery and he died at 130 the same day.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned. SHEPHERDRY AT WARRINGTON. The quarterly meeting of the Warrington district of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, A.U., was held at the Friend-in-Need Lodge, Waggon and Horses Inn. Warringtou, on Saturday, wider the presidency of Bro. r. S. Massey, who was supported by Bros. Thomas Miller (vice-chairman), James Parr (district treasurer), Peter Edgar (district se- cretary), William Eckersley (trustee), and the re- presentatives from the various lodges belonging to the district. A large number of brethren were present. The accounts for the quarter having been passed, the ordinary business was transacted and was followed by an interesting presentation to an esteemed member of the district,Bro. Hall.—P.P.C. S Bro. Lawrence Weir, the oldest member present in Shepherdry, in presersting Bro. Hall with a handsome timepiece, said he would rather have preferred that the duty of presentation should have fallen into more worthy hands. He was afraid he would not do the justice to the recipient as he deserved having only just received the news that he was expected to make the presentation, bat if Ithey were determined that such should be his lot, they must take the will for the deed. He took as much pride in this presentation as in any that had taken place, and he had taken part in some few since he had attended the district meetings. (Hear hear.) Numerous members had passed through the chairs of the district and received their reward and gratiGude for their services, and each one had endeavoured to fulfil those offices to the satisfaction of those who had confidence to elect them. Brothel Hall did not stand aside in that re- spect. Time had flown almost like the wind for it only seemed a few vr.veks ago since they were re- joicing at his elevation to the D.P.C's. position and that day they were met together to pay a tribute to his two year's work. The selection made at the time proved that he had not been selected because he held a more exalted position over his fellows or that there was a different ring in his name to that of other people, but because of his constant atterr'ance to the meetings. and the deroted attention he constantly paid to the work of the society. This inspired the members to have sufficieist confidence in trusting him with the destinies of the district, as ehairwan. They had been favoured with one of the most patient and forbearing men that it was possible to finri. in their midst, and many times he was sorry to sae many of them had trespassed upon that patient dia- position, which he trusted he would pardon them for. The position he (Mr Weir) was placed in that night was not a very enviable one as he had eulogy showered upon him from all sources and in view of that, he wished to spare him as much as possible. In asking him to receive that teetimonial as a mirk of esteem for his laboins, he could assure Bro. Hall that the wishes of the delegates and the district at large were that he might long be spared to enjoy it, and should he at any time be inclined to digress from the paths of Shepberdry, might this be the means to cause hi cr to buckle on his armour and to take np his former position. (Applause.) Bros. Haley, Parr, Kirk- nem, Lons,e, Aden's, Miller, and many other brethren spoke in high praise of Bro. Hall's con- duct during his term of office. Bro. Hall, who was , eery warmly received, thanked them all for the handsome testimonial,and several brethren for the kind remarks they had made. He had striven to do his duty, and it was pleasing to him to find that his conduct and work had met with the approbai. • tion of the members generally.</p>
      </div>
