        <p>SPECIAL ATTENTION PAID TO NEATNESS AND</p>
        <p>Elegance in PROSPECT USES, PROGRAMMES, &amp;c. PROGRAMMES FOR BAZAARS. PROGRAMMES FOR SALES OF WORK. PROGRAMMES FOR SOIREES. PROGRAMMES FOR CONCERTS. PROGRAMMES FOR FETES, &amp;C. Anctioneers' Sale Bills, Catalogues, Conditions of Sale. Circulars, Pamphlets, Price Lists, Programmes for Concerts, Soirees. Balls. Sze. ; Rules for Friendly. Building, and Co-operative Societies ; Cricket Club Rules, Time Sheets and Boeks, Note Headings,Memorandums,Work Notes,Bill Heads, Business Visiting and Monrning Cirds, Handbills, 8;:c. Solicitors, Merchmts, Manufacturers,Tradeamen, and others requiring Printing of any description are requested to give a trial.</p>
        <p>ALL ORDERS EXECUTED PUNCTUALLY, CORRECTLY,</p>
        <p>AND ECONOMICALLY</p>
        <p>WAR. is always horrible, and An Humane Work. it has been a bad business for 13: itish residents in the Transvaal up till now, though it may be worse hereafter. The flight of frightened people-- miners, labourers, shopkeepers, and thousands of other persons from the Transvaal, has been atten- ded by suffering and ruin. We cannot leave these people wholly to the care of the inhabitants of Cape Colony, who are doing the best they can for them under the circurasts.nces. It must be a matter a Imperial concern. When Parliament meets some grant ought to be made to assist these victims of Imperial policy. Grants have been made to the West Indies for persons who have lost their all under the effects of a great hurricane. War is a hurricane of another kind, and its approach has been marked by a flight as remarkable as any recorded in, ancient or modern history. It is too late to say that the residents in Johannesburg and other Transvaal towns should have remained and braved their fate. They lacked confidence in the tender mercies of the Boers in a time of war. They may have been mistaken. We hope it will turn out that they were, and that, apart from the politically pre- scribed, there will be no severities meted out to the British that remain, because they are unable to get away, even when the final oxodus comes, just before hostilities begin. But in any case, our duty at home is clear. We have all—Liberal and Unionist—espoused the cause of the Outlanders, and we cannot desert them now that they are the the victims of our policy and of their own fears. A Parliamentary grant ought to be one of the features of next week's work. Surely no objection could be raised to such an humane policy. Sir ALFRED MILNER has telegraphed to the Loma MAYOR of London inviting him to open a Mansion House Fund. There is no reason why he should wait to see .what the Government will do. Practical and timely sympathy is needed. The poor creatures who have abandoned their homes to seek a place of safety are the wives and children of men for whom we have risked so much, and it is our duty to succour them. On this point there can be no difference of opinion. If we were ready to make war for their political and com- mercial liberty,we ought to be eager to shew them by timely help that they are our kith and kin,and that our hearts are as large as our politics, pro• fessed to be generous and bold. A few days ought to evoke a handsome response,and the good people of Capetown ought to be cheered and supported in the noble work they have set themselves to do. The need for assistance is great, and it will be an earnest of our sincerity to give it promptly and liberally. War is a terrible thing, and there will be awful work before it is over. But the early victims, the frightened refugees, the homeless wanderers, are entitled to make the first and the most exacting claims on our regard.</p>
        <p>To the lay observer who Ecclesiastfcal looks on the present aspira- tions of ardent Churchmen with an intelligent desire to understand them, 1 there is something striking in the claim for more spiritual autonomy. Two Church systems have been at war ever since the foundation Of Christianity—the self-governed Church and the State-governed Church. The former has often ceased to be effective, and it has been persecuted and swallowed ip until the State and the Church were one. With varying fortunes, the two ideals</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>have existed for centuries, and toleration has allowed the Free Church ideal to become strong and to live and thrive. No new communities favour the State ideal, and so it slowly dies. To- day there is in the State Church of this country a movement towards the ideal of the Wesleyan, Con- gregational,Baptist,and other Churches. The hand of the State is irksome. Uniformity is almost as hard to the Iligh Churchman as it was to the old Presbyterian. There are longings and strivings for the external ideal—the ideal which every Churchman is thought to have repudiated once for all. Nonconformists must look on these cracks and fisssures in the old ideal with some interest. More autonony means government without the control of P Arliament, without reference to the Judicial Committee, and even without the Lam- beth judgment of the Archbishops. It is very much like a child's crying for the moon. A Church, which is united to the State cannot have any true spiritual autonomy, and the attempt to discover one is only of value as a symptom, a protest, a longing for a truer ideal, the discarded ideal of the Nonconformist. The autonomy desired must come from the State, or it will lack effective sanction. Parliament must part with some of its powers, and hand them over to the Church. But if it is to be invited to part with real supremacy, why should it not proceed to do so and grant Disestablishment ? That is the problem. The corapreheusionist can never hope to reconcile the strong Protestant and the strong Anglo-Roman, because the former wants more and the latter less. Parliamentary control with comprehension, as a theory, is beau- tiful; but it implies laxity in the administration of Acts of Parliament, in the interpretation of the Prayer Book, in the obedience to Bishops. Spiri- tual freedom implies disestablishment by which means alone true autonomy could be ob- tained. But it would be an autonomy of two Churches instead of one. Autonomy, as things are, is impossible. It is Parliament and the law which keeps the Church from splitting asunder. Of course, the autonomy desired will not come. Parliament will never assent to the supersession and degradation of its authority over the Church of the nation. It will agree to cut off the Church and to disendow it, and leave the Church to do what it likes. But that is an entirely different matter. The interest of the moment, however, for mere outsiders is not in the Bishop of London's pleas for unity and peace. It is in the confession that, after all these centuries of controversy, spiritual autonomy is the sine qua •non of an active, healthy, and progressive Church. The old ideal is dying, and dying hard ; but it be- gins to decay from within.</p>
        <p>Public interest in the race for the America Cup will hardly survive another fiasco. If no tangible ,result is achieved next time the conditions must be altered. It is difficult to understand why the time limit was changed. Sir Thomas Lipton's advisers must have been aware that the light airs so frequent off Sandy Hook would make it most difficult for the Shamrock to get over the course in five and a half hours. The three attempts have proved that it was equally difficult for the Columbia to do so, but that could not have been known before the attempts were made. We be- lieve that in the past rather more than seven hours were allowed. That time limit was reason- able, and had it been adhered to the last three races would have been brought to a definite con- clusion. If a fourth disappointment occurb, the Cup Committee must revert to the original con- ditions. It may well be that the Cup Committee felt justified in shortening the limit because modern yachts are so much faster than their pre- decessors. In that case an unfortunate but easily understandable error of judgment was committed. It is to be hoped that the error will be promptly recognised by an immediate extension, so that public interest in the race may be satisfied.</p>
        <p>IT is said that a revolution in Afghanistan would be hailed with heartfelt joy by Russian statesmen. Great Britain being engaged in a heavy task in South Africa, it is assumed that we should be powerless to prevent Russian interven- tion, and possibly a Russian occupation of Cabul. Hence these fearsome tales of the Ameer's lunacy, .the Ameer's ferocity, and the simmering hatred of his people. Yet the reports need not disturb us. If Russia has the chance to do us a good turn on the borders of India, of course she will take it. Spite- fulness and craft are venerated traditions in Russia. But there is no reason to suppose that we shall 'fall in with Russian aspirations, Given an Afghan revolution—which is extremely unlikely—and given war in South Africa, the situation does not imperil British interests. We have the flower of our Indian army still within reach of the Indian border. The home establishment will by no means be depleted when the Army Corps has been des- patched. The volunteers would be called out for home service, so that all trained fotces could be relieved, and the navy would threaten Russia's position in the Far East. Hampered as we might be in South Africa, we could at this moment do infinitely more harm to Russia than Russia could do to us. Russian statesmen are well aware that mar for the possession of Afghanistan before the ,completion of the Siberian-Manchurian line would mean the total overthrow of Russian interests in 'China within three months from the declaration of hostilities. They are not going to imperil their position there for the mere sake of annoying England and getting nearer to the borders of India.</p>
        <p>The Warrington District Nursing Association may now be said to be a firmly established insti- tution. The report which was presented at the annual meeting last week bears evidence of the good work which is being accomplished, and there is every reason to believe that the visits of the district nurses have been appreciated by the poor. " I'd have been lost but for you, nurse " is the testimony of grateful patients and it has an honest ring about it which cannot be mistaken. The majority of the patients nursed were housewives, and those who know any- thing of the conditions in which housewives of the very poor are placed when sick, can quite appre- ciate the statement of the Superintendent when she says that in many cases the ponr women are left to the care of children much too young to understand the importance of anything but play. The present nursing staff is quite inadequate for such a large town as Warrington, and there is a distinct need for more nurses, but as the present subsciptions will not allow an increase in the staff, the work must naturally be curtailed until the necessary funds. are forthcoming. In looking through the subscription list, we observe that the individual subscriptions only amounted to £l7O. This surely cannot he said to represent the interest of the upper and middle classes in such a deserving institution.</p>
        <p>PALETHORPR'S ROYAL CAM BRIDGE SAUSAGES</p>
        <p>AND MELTON PORK RES.</p>
