      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>GOOD NEWS FOR WESTON.</p>
        <p>Last Friday tTe-to-r-e -the Runcorn Nligi,trates a rate of 1s 3d in the £ was nal or tie Parizh of Woston,wn'ch is Co realise £789 13i 4d: The rate is a reduction of 31 in the 2 on the corresponding half of last year.</p>
        <p>SIR REDVERS HULLER SAILS WITH A RUNCORN CAPTAIN. Next Saturday morning, Sir Redvers Bullet, who is to command the British Forces in Smith Africa, sails troai Southampton for the Cape on the "Lianotter Castle." The vessel is captained by Mr. H. Rigby, of Runeorn, who is one of the most respected of the cmninanders of the "Castle Line." NEW ALDERMAN AND MAYOR FOR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the usual monthly meeting, held at the Town Hall on Tuesday afternoon, Councillor John Ingram Neil was placed on the aldermanic bench in place of Ilderne en John Widiamssn, who has just retired °win?, to business pressure. At a private sitting held immediately after the business was over, the Mayor (Alderman John War- ing) intimated that Aldermen Neil had consented to act as Mayor for the ensuing year. PARISH CHURCH WEDNESDAY TEAM AT CHESTER.</p>
        <p>On -W-e7i-ne-s-da-y--afternoon the Parish Church Wednes- day (rugby) team visited Chester College, and at the conclusion of a fast and exciting game won by 11 points to 3 points. The scorers tor the Parish were :—Sutton, Shallcross and Bill, and Nickion was responsible for converting one. Coasidering that the Runcorn team played with 14 men for the greater portion of the game, though Capper being injured, the win was a good t one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>BOY RUN DOWN BY A Cycusr.</head>
        <p>A BOY RUN DOWN BY A Cycusr.</p>
        <p>As Mr. J. Brown, assistant master' at the Victoria- road, Board Schools, was riding his bicycle down Savage's Bridge.he ran over a young biy and was pitchel bead-foremost from the machine. Luckily, he eacaped injury, and on regaining hi a fe-3t his first care was, not, as is generally the case with cyclists, his machine ; but, he solicitously ran to the by, and embracing him in- quired whether he was hurt. Happily the youngster was also found to have eacaped serious injury. Sr. PATRICK'S CHURCH, WIDNES. The special effort of the congregation of this church to raise tae sum of £l,OOO in aid of the building fund of the new presbytery terminated ()a M3nday evening by a soirée held in the Drill Hall. The stalls and parapher- nalia in connection with the basar were speedily removed during the day, and in the evening a goodly company assembled. Messrs. P Manning, J. Fishwick, P. Malone, snd M. Wailing officiatod as M.G.'s, and dancing was continued with zest to the strains of Messrs Gill and Gillon's band until an early hour. The catering, was in the hands of Mr. T. Laws, and gave every satis- faction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TSB RUNCORN HARRIERS AT AINTREE.</head>
        <p>TSB RUNCORN HARRIERS AT AINTREE.</p>
        <p>The opening run of the cross country season was held on Saturday, at Aintree, in beautiful weather, and there was a record muster of runners,representing the various clubs of Liverpool and district. Owing to the Runcorn Harriers being so poorly represented, they ran under the Farnworth Club. Five miles of beautiful country was covered, the mile break being very exciting. W. Ray succeeded in securing the second position for novices. A dispute for third place, between Pollitt, of West Cheshire, and S Flopkin,of Runcorn, was given in favour of the West Cheshire man. After no run was over tea was provided at the Sefton Arms Elotel. In the evening there was a distribution of prizes and Fmoking concert, when the successful competitors were awarLied their prizes.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL GATEIHRING AT TRH CO-OPERLTITH HALL, RuscoaN.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Amalgamated Union of Co- operative Employes a social gathering took place at the Runcorn Co-operative Hall, on Wednesday evening, Mr. IT. Saunders presided. An address was delivered by Mr. A. Hewitt, general secretary of the A U.C.E., M In- chester, and Mr. W. E. Dudley also spoke. In the course of the evening the following items were contributed ; refreshments were also provided Pianoforte selection. Mr S Beckett ; glee, " 0 who will o'er the downs," Wbeatsheaf Glee Party; song, "King of the deep," Mr G Ronald Johnson ; song, " Queen of the Earth," Mr A Shingler ; violin solo, Mr M Jams ; Comic sosg (by request), "It's another colour," Mr J L With- ington ; pianoforte selection, Mr S Beckett ; song, " Toe Flight of Ages," Mr G Ronald Johnson ; violin solo, Mr James; song. Mr C H Johnson ; song, " The old soldier," Mr A Shingler. The accompanist was Mr S Beckett.—Votes of thanks concluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CONVERSAZIONE AT TEIB NIERSEIT MISSION.</head>
        <p>CONVERSAZIONE AT TEIB NIERSEIT MISSION.</p>
        <p>A conversazione took place at the Mersey Mission to Seamen on Monday evening last, which was attended with a measure of success. The primary object of the gathering was for the sale of the fruit used in the decor- ation work at the Mission harvest home festival, but a secondary purpose was for a sacial gathering for the workers and friends of the mission. Mr. W. Shaw pre- sided and the attendance present was very moderate. The programme submitted was as follows :—Pianoforte solo, Miss Garvie ; song, "The Anchor's Weighed," Mr C Temple ; humorous song, "They are getting it up for me" (encored), Mr C Gornall ; song. " How to ash and how to have," Miss Temple ; duet, " Gipsy Countess." Miss F Temple and Mc C Temple ; song, " Margunite" (encore), Miss Garvie ; duet. " Very Suspicious," Miss Coward and Mr C Temple, this izien was most vocifer- ously encored and fairly brought down the house; humorous song, "A thousand yards in the rear," Mr Gornal ; song, " I dreamt I ,dwelt in Marble Halls " (nr.cored), Mies Garvie. The efficient accompanist was Miss Garvie, and the secretarial duties were ably carried out by Miss Bowyer. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVIIIIENT CLASS--FRISIT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>BANQUET.</head>
        <p>BANQUET.</p>
        <p>The inaugural meeting of the winter session of the Runcorn Brunswick-street Mutual Improvement Class took place last Wednesday evening at the Brunswick Schoolroom, when a large attendance of members assembled under the presidency of Mr T Simister, who was supported by the Rev Humphrey Morrison and Mr D Bisbrown. In the course of the evening Mr Morrison delivered an appropriate address upon the"advantagas of of improvement classes and the benefits derived from a study of good literature." Soon adiresses were als ) delivered by Messrs D Bisbrovyn, W Millington-Lester, G Hayes, J Douglas, and F Littler. Songs were contributed in appreciative style by Misseq. Raid- ing, Siinkin, and Unsworth, a recitation from Mr Frank Littler was well received. Altogether the procaedings proved a distinct SqCC.S4. Mr. J. J. Pickthall the secretary was highly complimented upon the complation of so good a programme and it was generally expressed that he should meet with some reward at the sessions close.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN INSTIT UTE SCROOLI. --PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening toe prizes to the students attending the evening classes at the Runcorn Institute were distributed by the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood. (vicar). It had been announced that Mr C. S. Roundell was to distribute the prizes, but from a letter which was read it appeared he was unable to attend and do so owing to a bad cold confining him indoors. Thus the duty devolved upon the worthy Canon, and right well he performed it. The chairman for the evenince was Mr Francis Wiswall (Chairman of the Runcorn Urban District Council), and he was supported by the Rev C W L Christien, Messrs John Wilson (chairman of the Techni- cal Committee), William Handley, E Imison, 0.0,. A R Norman, J W Lightburn, T A Lawyers - son (headmaster), S Pope and others.—ln the course of an interesting speech the Rev. Canon Wood emphasised the importance of a sound liberal and general education as a preliminary to taking up tech. nical education. He urged that what was wanted in this country was such an education that would adapt men and women to become a thoroughly intelligent and good people capable of turning their minds to anything that might be presented to them. They should endeavour to make them good workmen, and having done that,pat good tools into their hands. The means for bringiug about such a state lay in such institutions as that they were so interested in that night. A.t the conclusion a hearty vote of thanks was scoot led Csnnon Wood for his seevicPs,propesed by Mr. J. Wilson and seeonde I by Mr. W. Handley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FRODSHAII.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAII.</p>
        <p>CHANGE RINGING.—On Saturday evening the Dares- bury company of change-ringers paid a visit to the above church and rang on the bells two peals of mino:, consisting of 720 changes each, viz., college single and plain bob ; time, 27 and 26 minutes respecr,ively• Tha ringers stood as follows :—P Johnson, let; F Jones, 2nd; P Hamblen, 3rd; J Priestley, 4th ; E o Prez, sth ; T Houghton (conductor), 6th. Weight oi tenor, 18cwt. A RUNCORN TAILOR'S VISIT.—Oft Wednesday morning, at the Frodsham Police Court, before Mr: A. Thomas, Jame 3 Anderson, tailor of Runcorn, was charged in custody with being" found drunk" the previous night. —P.C. Wagg, whn proved the case, stated he founi the prisoner drunk in the station, at Frodsham. He was on the platform stud refused to leave. He locked him up.— Fined 53.---The money was paid.</p>
        <p>a..-...-.. 4.01..... THE ILLNESS OR MR. ROBERT GARNETT.</p>
        <p>"The Warrington Wesleyan Church Record" con- tains the following allusion to the illness of Mr. Garnett :—"A wide circle of friends scattered over the country have heard with deep regret of the sudden illness of Mr. Robert Garnett. His public position in the country and long and close connec- tion with our church have arousAd unusual interest and sympathy on his behalf. We are thankful to say that the latest news is favourable, and we may look forward to his speedy recovery." SANKEY HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>About 16 members of the above club turned up last Saturday at their headquarters. the Sloop Hotel. Sankey Bridzes, for the opening run of the season, The pack going out at an easy pace travelled over about 5 miles of light country in the direction of Burtonwood, Bold Estate, and Sankey. Captain G. Etstwood acted as pace, with sub-captain J: Potts as whip. The weather was splendid for cross-country running and was thor- oughly enjoyed by all. To-day, captain v. sub-captains' team from headquarters, and it is expected there will be a much larger turnout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13, 1899.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Council was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. Present : tlr J Knowles (airrnati), Admita.l Jones Parry, *MRS J Mc- Intyre, T Lewis, J Stokes, E Clarke. P Leather, W Bradford, T Baxter, E Bretherton, C E Linaker, J Mose. loy-Williams, r Wright, J B Chadwick, II Mawdsley, S Woolward, P Gleave, J Spruce, T A.ntwiss, with G F Ashton (clekk), W E ilouti deputy clerk), W Diggle and J Swinton (surveyors), James Farrington (inspector) and Dr J Adams (medical officer.) FRODSEAM WATER, SUPPLY £3,400. _</p>
        <p>Mr. NoelY.-K-73-rs-h-aw, assistaut secretary of the Local Government Board, wrote stating that he was directed by the Local Government Board "to acknowledge the re- ceipt of yaw letter of the 6:n inst., mad to forward to you herewith their formal sanctisons to the borrowing by the Rural District Council of Runcorn, of the sums of £3,000 and £lOB for w irk, of water supply for the Lordsoip of Frodsham, the latter sum being in respect of machinery. The sanctions are eivan on the under- standing that duplicate pumping machinery will be pro- vidA when the soherne is in fad workia4 order.',</p>
        <p>of the healthy state the doctor ieportel Kingsley to be in.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : It is like you ; to bring very startling reports bac. (Laughter.) Mr. Woodward was understood to say that he would supply the doctor with names and addresses of some cases, but whether of measles did not appear perfectly clear. He further addeu he was not responsible for the reparta brought to him ;if they lacked corroboration it was a matter of congratulation.</p>
        <p>TRH WATER AT DUTTON LODGE.</p>
        <p>At the r.quest of Mr. Jarn,33 Farrington it was decided that sanapks of the water at Dutton Lodge be taken for analysis before any steps be taken for opening out the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>THB I'EVBII HOSPIBAL.</head>
        <p>THB I'EVBII HOSPIBAL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the L scat Qovarnrnent Board adverting t 3 the letter of the Rural District Coun- cil with reference to the provision of Hospital accomo- dation for the Runcern Rural D:s:ricteand iaq iiriag Cite present position of the matter. The Clerk said it was a similar letter to the one he re- plied to in July last, when the Council iastrueted him to say they were unable to obtain a suitable site. The Chairman observed they were still in that position, and supposed they would have to forward a like reply. Mr. tdoseley Williams referred to toe question as one he believed he and the members of the Committee appointed to consider it would never be able to bring other gentlemen present to look upon in the same light. It was one they had been tackling for the lestfive ye ire, and he thought it was high time that they stated their reasons to the Local Government Board in a very plain way, and let the faecal Government Board or the County Council do what they liked. He proposed that the answer to the Local Government Board state that they were unable te find a site and for the present they were unable to do anything in the matter. Admiral Jones Parry seconded. Mr. J. Mclntyre moved an amendment, that the old committee be reappointed to reconsider the matter. Mr. Thomas Wright seconded the amendment. Mr. S. Woodward in support of the original proposi- tion wished to say that he could not understand the ac- tion of the Local:Government Board or its representative in their district with reference to the sites already sug- gested as fit for infcetious diseases hospitals. They were pretty much face to face with a state of affeirs which was not carried out throughout the country. They were to select a site something likes mile Of thereabouts from any habitable buildings, a thing that didscarcely obtain in other places, and he was at a loss to understand the position of the Local Government Board on the qua,- tion. He instanced, it was only the other week, he was within the district of an urban authority and there saw a fever hospital which was constructed of corrueated iron, part of the plate being of brick, and he should judge it had at one time served as an old farm house. Besides all that it was within a stones throw of—he would not like to tell--how many dwellings,ancl its back premises he described as more suitable for piggeries. In addition it was also within a short dietance of a ceme- tery. He could not understand why they in their dis- trict should be so tied down by those limitatioes of one mile, when they had plenty of room and space quite far enough from any habltation, in the face of what he described. On the motion being put, Mr. Williams' propeeition was carried by a large majority. RE-APP3INTMENT OF INSPECTOR. The Lecal Government Board wrote sanctioning the re-appointment of Mr. J. Farrington, as Inspector of Nuisances for the Rural District Council of Runcorn from the 30th of September, 1899, to the 29th of Sep- tember, 1900, at a salary of £l9O.</p>
        <p>DANGEROUS STEPS ON THE HIGH LEVEL BRIDGE, LATCH/ORD.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Ryan, of 1, Enfield Villas, Falrfield-:oad, Latchford Without, wrote to the Clerk of the Council drawing attention to the steps from the road leadint, up to the High Level Bridge. On Monday last whilst'des- °ending them he was pitched forward, and might have been hurt had it nct been that he was lucky enough to fall sitting upon some of the steps lower down. Mr. Ryan also expressed the hope that he (the Clerk) would see to the steps being put in order when made aware of their dangerous state. The Clerk having read Mr. Ryan's letter, stated that a copy had been forwarded to Mr. Swinton. Mr. Swinton c )nsidered that the Rural District Council was responsible for the place. Mr. Linaker advised caution in maters of that nature and advocated the aacertainina of the Council's true position beforeny steps were taken. After some further disaus‘ion it was decided that the Clerk ascertain the true position of affairs and report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HALTON WATER ANALYSIS.</head>
        <p>THE HALTON WATER ANALYSIS.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Marshall, secretary to tee Runcorn Urban Dis- trict Council, wrote, under date 2nd October,as follows: —" Dear sir,—Had your letter with regard to the water supply at Halton, coins to hand a day earlier it could have been at once laid before the Water Commit- tee. I will, however, bring it before them at their next; meeting. You will notice that the free ammonia is given as '0097. The last time the analysis gave this result Mr. Carter Bell made the following remark : " This is a first-class water of a high degree of purity, but contains too much chloride of sodium or common salt" I name this so that you may be reassured as t 3 there being no sewage contamination. It is not long since the reservoir was cleaned out. Although you do not say so, some dis3elouration of the wt.ter will prob- ably have been noticed- This would be caused by corrosion in the pipes. However this is a matter on which our water engineer will report." Mr. Thomas Wright said it was something like nine years since the matter of the Halton Water had cont s up before them,and,at that time, after analysing the water, their Medical Officer had stated it was absolutely ueees- , sary that the reservoir should be cleanel out annually or at least every two years. As a matter of fact, it as five years since it was cleaned. He thought that the Urban District Council of Renown should be compelled te clean out the reservoir of:ener, and that their inspector or other officer should be present and see it done. The Clerk remarked that the matter had not yet bee laid before the Runcorn Urban District Council. The latter portion of the letter was simply a comment by their secretary. It was, therefore, decided to await a reply fr)na the Runcorn Urban District Council.</p>
        <p>THE SILVERS POLLUTION COMMITTEE AND TFIE MEDICAL</p>
        <p>OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mclntyre stated, as chairman of the Rivers Pollution Committee, they had not much of a report to give. The members of the Committee in coesideraWe number had met that morning at the appointed hour, half-past ten, and sat looking at each other awaiting their Medical Officer. They could do nothing without the Doctor's presence. If he was to give a report at all, he should like to say that the time of those who had been waiting at that meeting was almost as precious as that of the Medical Officer, and he hoped their Medical Officer would mend his manners in the future, and not keen the members of the Committee waiting so long after the time appointed. (Elear, hear.) Owing to his absence there is no report to salon:lit. Dr. Adams said he had sent a telegram to the Clerk explaining his absence. The Clerk That was not received until after II o'clock.</p>
        <p>The : Oh! It was sent from my house before 10 o'clock.—Dr. Adams then explained the condition of affairs. He had received two instalments of water analysis, and had communicated with Dr. Vacher (the Medical Officer of the County). He took it that the object of the committee's sitting that morning was to reply to the letter dated 2tr.l October, recording the fact that the County Council Committee had met on the 20th ult., and were desirous of knowing what steps the Rural District Council had taken in the matter, and explained that that information had already been communicated to the Medical Officer of the County Council. Replying to the complaint of ths 'Riven Pollution Committoe he would like to point out that four days out of the last four- teen had been devoted by him entirely to sanitary work, bath morning and evening, and timit the meeting of the committee at 10.30 made it difficult for him to attend vrithaut serious interference with other duties</p>
        <p>Mr. Mclntyre considered the doctor's explanation was not sufficient. The doctor added in addition to what he had steel, he had been unfortunate ennigh to lame himself by in- juring his knee. The matter dranped.</p>
        <p>ILLNESS OF MR. .10IINT WEIR, OF WALTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mclntyre said before the business of the Coun oil proceeded any further, he should like to move a reSo- intim; that a letter of sympathy be sent te sivery old member of that Council—a member who had been ab- sent for a very lone time owing to protracted illness. He referred to Mr. John Weir, of Walton. fie thought he would be right in saying that Mr. Weir was one of the oldest members of that Council, and he thought that a letter of sympathy coining from them would cheer him up considerably. (Hear, hear.) Especially if it went from them as an expression of tha deep sympathy they entertained for him, and wishing him a speedy recovery. (Hear, hear.) Mr. J. Stokes seconded. Mr. Woodward sympathised with the prooosition,and spoke of the lengthy connection of Mr. Weir with the Council, during which time he had been art efficient and able member of the Council, and, one who had devote :l a c.)nsiderable amount of his time to the affairs of the Rural Sanitary Authority and the Union. Mr. Stokes supplimented Mr. Woodward's remarks and referred to Mr. Weir's work as that of a mast useful member. Oa putting the motion it was irianitnoosly agreed to. TRE MEASLES AT KINGSLEY.</p>
        <p>Dr. Aflame reported that hi hal int-) the complaint of measles at Kingsley and h3,d en' Mr. Rey- nolds. He believed there wa3 not a case of meaele3 in Kingsrov. Mr. Woodward said he was exceedingly glad to hear</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN IN A liEttRY EIDIOUR.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN IN A liEttRY EIDIOUR.</p>
        <p>BY THE "SCORCHER."</p>
        <p>Last week, I expressed the fear' that Tyldesley might defeat Rancorn—not through any merit dis- played on their part—but, by the "Linnets," in their might. holding the " Boagers " too cheaply ; there, and there only, lay the danger! On Saturday however, as the teams came within the enclosure at Inv ell Luis—the" Linnets" as spry and chirpy as ever, and the "Bangers "more after the manner of 'pinny numbers "—my fears took wing, and I knew the " wearers o' th' green " would be victors. This was doubly evident when the start—coming as it did with Tyldesley one short of full complement—took place. Their " presto " eight points were registered in as many minutes or less. The game was then won, and Tyldesley had no more say in the decision. Soon it developed into a pantomime and the crowd was kept in one continual roar Of merriment by the merry way the " redtails " romped around their opponents. It was funny, vary funny—bat then it always is for the winners—to sae Eloughton do- ing just woat he liked with his opponents, and the whole team following "in his steps.' Butterworth was quite at home, and looked comfortable all through the progress of the game. Warder, Wilkin- son and Jones were equally fortunate. Jolley and Faulkaar—(espetially the latter)—were ever a source of menace to the Tyldesleyites, and had but to set the ball in motion to tax the utmost resources of the visiting team. Indeed, at times, the " tune " Tyldesley had to "Step to" was none to their liking. In the Runcorn forward line, an utter contempt for their opponents was visible, and they simply played as if enjoying a practice against their own " A" team—though I rather fancy the "A "team would have given them a better game— far truly enough the Bongers "could play none." Farmer was particularly sarcastic, and often stood for the opposing team to try and bring him down, or cooly tucking the ball beneath his brawny arm strode con- temptuously through their midst. Always, of course, to an accompaniment of laughter from the crowd. Gayter, who bobbed about everywhere like an india rubber doll, always seemed to b 3 enjoying the joke ---!eastvrays he came up smiling every time. Dar- lington, Lightfoot, Walker,and the others all seemed of the same opinion that the game was a joke In- deed,the only man in the Runcorn team that seemed to be really serious was Tomlinson,and he appeared to be too busy tickling up the affections of his rivals to enter into the humour of the situa- tion; if he evinced any humour at all, it was of a grim nature. To say that Tyl- desley played at all would hardly be recording fact, for they gave a most disappointing exhibition of football and did not come within measurable distance of their form of the previous week. To say, that Runcorn beat them on their merits! As was the case last week, would also be obviously incorrect. But the truth lies nearer the statement " that Runcorn fairly thrashed them and easily too," and, it is quite evideat,that if Tyldesley expect to move from beneath the weight of the "good spoon for boys" they will have to change much. _ _</p>
        <p>Stockport at Stockport is the next mate' in the " Lsague," and the qtruggle between these teams— Runcorn and Stockport—is generally called that for the; championehip of Cheshire- Naturally no- onethinks of anybody else having any pretensions to it; and, if you believe the, " radtails " they go so far as to say its hardly a matter for dispute—they are the chamoione, and alwayi i1f1.90 been. Last season Runcorn beat Stockport en their own ground by 8 points to nil, and at Eltunora by 13 to nil. Thil year they ought to do able it, or be ashamed of themselves.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES, [By A. CORRE3PONDENT.I The subject of the Halton water, to which I re- ferred last week, Wl9 incidentally mentioned before the Rural Distric-, Council,who are awaiting a reply from the Runcorn Urban District Council respecting the query of that body regarding the cleansing of the reservoir. From the tenure of the footnote which accompanies the formal acknowledgement of the receipt of the letter it would appear that the clerk to the Runcorn Urban District Council finis it competent to call attention to an apparent analogy in the certificates of analysis submitted by Dr, Carter Ball, That may or may not be, but Mr. Thomas Wright's contention that the storage reser- voir should be cleaned out with greater frequency, is a matter the public Can comprehend far better than hypotheses on the percentage of chlorides and the conclusions to be drawn from the presence of such in water. If the statement tnat the reservoir has not been cleansed fnr five years is true, it is a great shame, that is all the public have to say.</p>
        <p>It is no good anyone trying to defend anything which is rotten to the core ; it is not economy, it is not even principle; but it might have some relation to martyrdom. Yet from choice I would not be the one to suffer martyrdoat in a rotten cause. Then in the name of conscience why do so many people devise schemes to gloss over the faet that the Run- corn water supply is a decidedly inferior one. In our town at the present moment of writing, there are more cases of typhoid fever than I care to tell— and if these are not directly traceable to the defec- tive water supply; if, I say, they are not the out- come of bad wa7;er and bid sanitation, ta what are they due. It is a subject we nme of us can treat lightly, or We may be soon face to face with a con- dition of affairs that will be as bad if not worse than was the eve at Maidstone.</p>
        <p>analyses already show that the water is none of the best; there is even a note of doubt in the tone of all the certificates submitted by the analyst for some time past ; and I believe I am right in saying that never has Runcorn wster been pronounced as a " first-class " water. Teat there is something unsatisfactory about it is amply verified by experi- ment, for we are seldom if ever free from disease— when there is any disease at all, Runcorn somehow or other manages to get its share.</p>
        <p>To Runcorn there has fallen two very great honours within the last few months. It is not so long since we boasted of the appointment of, a Run- corn captain to the coma:lan:l of the largest ship afloat, the "Oceanic." Now another honour comes to us, inasmuch as our respected townsman Captain H. Rigby will navigate the vessel—the " Dunotter Castle"—to the Cape, having on board the hero of the hour. Sir Redvers Buller, commander of the British forces in South Africa. The "Dunotter Castle" sails from Southampton on Saturday, and takes with it the "God speed" of every English heart.</p>
        <p>I learn that the Runcorn Liberal Club I; about to undergo extensive alterations, which have been in contemplation for some time. The alterations are said to be sub as to provide greater accommodation for readers, as it has been felt that latterly the accommoiation has been simply for players—in other words that the Club has been more of the nature of a billiard-saloon. Thanks, however, to the generosity of Sir John Brunner, for I hear he is at the back of it, this state of affairs is about to be altered and as much aecommolation given for the "old politician—reader and thinker" as for the " young politician—player and talker." The pro- posed alterations will be submitted to a general meeting of members to-night (Friday).</p>
        <p>The Theatre Royal, Runcorn, is threatened with opposition, and naw we are promised a regular ser. vice of plays in the Runcorn Public Hall. It is obvious to the public that bath these institutions cannot succeed, and they must decide whether they will " off with the o'd love and on with the new. Myself, I hope they will support the favourite of the past. Mr. Caddie has alwAys done his best to entertain.</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER" SERIE9. THE WARRINGTON Pc MID-ORESHIRE EXAMINER, THE "ST. HELENS EXAMINER. AND PRESCOT WEEKLY NEWS." THE " EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON EXAMINER AND ASHTON. GOLBORNE, AND HAYDOCS REPORTER." THE" RUNCORN EXAMINER." AND</p>
        <p>THE "WIDNES EXAMINER."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FASHIONABLE WEDDING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>FASHIONABLE WEDDING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>CHALLINOR-TI 111 I NS.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Parish Church was the scene of an interesting and happy spectacle on Wednesday afternoon last when the nuptials of Mr. John Jeffreys Holden Challinor, son of the late Mr. Alfred Challinor, of Bolton, and Miss Ada Timmins, only daughter of the late Mr. Ebenezer Timmins. of Mill House, Runcorn, was celebrated in the presence of a large concourse of friends and well-wishers. The bridegroom entered the Church at 2.15, and shortly after the bride, leaning on the arm of her brother, (Mr. W. Timmins), who gave her away, enterPd the building accompanied by her three bridesmaids: Miss Florry Timmins (niece), Miss Challinor, and Miss Patty Timmins (niece.) The bride was prettily attired in a dress of wnite glace3l silk with polo- naise of grey crepe, and wore a white :chiffon hat trimmed with white feathers, ospreys, and jewel buckles; she also carried a large bouquet of white roses, and a shower bouquet of roses, lilies, and orchids ; the gift of the bridegroom. The brides- maids' dresses were of heliotrope cloth trimmed with silk and guipure lace,and beneath black velvet picture hats they looked charming. They carried bouquets of yellow and white chrysanthemums,and wore ropes of pearls with pearl and diamond hearts, the gifts of the bride and bridegroom. As the bridal party proceeded up the aisle,n sweet music from the organ resounded through the sacred building. They were met at the chancel by the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood (vicar), and the Rev. H. B. Firth (curate), whe performed the solemn cere- mony. After its conclusion the party left for the home of the bride, Stanley Villas, where the wedding breakfast Awes partaken of, and subse- quently the happy couple drove to War- rington en route for Harrogate where the honey- moon will be spent. The bride's going-away dress was a heli )trope costume with hat to match. The groomsman was Mr. Challinor (uncle of the bridegroom). The guests included :—Major and Mrs. Booth, (Turtou), Mr and Mrs W Timmins, Mr and Mrs C Timmins, Sir Charles Timmins,(junior) and Miss Ada Timmins, Mr and Mrs A Timmins, Mr l 4 Timmins, and Master Launce• '1 immins, Mr William Challinor (Bolton),Aiss Addison (Lytham) Mr Poole (Bolton), Mr A Challinor (Bolton), Mr J Liptrett and Miss Liptrott (Bolton), Mr J Jeffreys, (Liverpool), Mrs Norman Martin and Mr F W Timmins,—The music at the church was played by Mr. A. C. Timmins (nephew) and included "Andantino," "Six Offertoires "1 (Lefebure-Welby), and "The Wedding March " (Mendelsshon. The presents were many and hand- some. Prominent among them was a massive marble timepiece the gift of the employees of Messrs. E. Timmins and Sons, Limited, engineers and contractors, Bridgewater Foundry, Runcorn, in which concern the bride is one of the largest share- holders, being the only daughter of the late Mr Ebenezer Timmins, the founder of the firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the above Board, was bold at the WorkhousP, Dutton, on Tuesday. Mr. J. Moseley-Williama presided, and there were also present : —Messrs. A. R. Norman, T. Lea, T. Reynolds, J. Meadowcroft, the Rural District Councillors, with Messrs. G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Haugh (deputy clerk, and James Cunnah (Master.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RESIGNATION OF THE MASTER'S ASSISTANT.</head>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF THE MASTER'S ASSISTANT.</p>
        <p>Philip B. Hindmarsh wrote resigning his appointment of boys' caretaker and masters' assistant. His sole reason for leaving was that he had got an appointment under the Ship Canal Company, which he thought would suit him better than remaining in the Poor Law Service. —The Master intimated that the vacancy had been advertised.—A Committee was appointed to c3nsider the applications and report.</p>
        <p>THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward suornitted the finances of the Board. There was a rather large account, but still he did not think there was anything that called for special men- tion. Out-relief was granted as follows:—Runcorn £80; Frodsharn, £22 • and Great Budworth, £24. The pay- ments also included the usual amount for salaries. They paid to the Pantaiaph Orphanage in respect of the children from the Union there, £26. There were some large payments in vaccination fees, and they would, he felt sure, appreciate the difference which the vaccina- tion rules of the Local Government Board brought about. Toes, had paid to Dr. Bower, of Great Bod- worth, £l9 103 61 made up of one case at 53 ; and 40 at 7s 6d. The difference in price was all on account of distance.—Several guardians asked what the cost had been before.—Mr. Woodward replying said the avelage payment to the out stations was something like £9 per quarter, now they had a total average of about £l9 103 61 for Dr. Bower. The average for Dr. Mc Dougall bad been about £l5, while that quarter they had to pay about £25. Mr. C. E. Linaker : It is rather too early to have a statement of comparison with the previous year ? Mr. Woolward : No, you cannot take the annual accounts yet. It is too early. Mr. Norman asked was it not a fact that the fixing of the fees for vaccination was subject to revision at the end of the twelve months ? The Chairman : Teat is so. - - . - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward continuing slid the actual balance at the quarcers end was £719 63 611 payments that day amounted to £lOO3 53 71, which they would have been unable to meet, but for the navment, by the Local Government Board, of £4lB 16s 61 to their account in respeat of the A.yricultural Rating Act. The balance remaining was £134 183 7d. Mr. T. Wright seconded and the report was adopted.</p>
        <p>AN INCREASE IN INMATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Meadowcroft asked whether the master could answer him a question respecting the returns of the inmates. Ho noticed that the number of inmates (men) for the last week ending the Michaelmas quarter, 1899, was 79 as compared with 60 for the sorresporAing period of last year; with reference to the women and children (ordinary ablr-bodied) he had 172 for the week ending Michaelmas as against 159 for the corresponding period, and for the first week of the Christmas quarter the figures were 170 as against 158 for the corresponding period. He required to know whether they had aiven less out relief that they were having mare indoor in- mates than last year._ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman. said he could scarcely answer that question and did not know whether the master was abe to do so. Re thought, however, that Mr. Meadowcroft could take it fro:n the general statement submitted by Mr. Danzie, the poor favv inspector, that the out-relief was on the increase chroughout the country. Ic was rather favoured by Guardians and with the smaller cases there were more applications for out-relief. Mr. T. Wright observed that for Runcorn the out- relief was if anything rather less. Mr. A. R. Norman thought that Mr. Meadowcroft's idea lavas that one governed the other, but he (Mr. Nor- man) did not think so. They were, in fact, quite dis- tinct, and did not effect one another. Mr. Wadowcroft stated in explanation that he was rather questioning the policy the Board had advocated in one or two cases from which it would appear that they had decided it was better for the Board if these people came in to the house, and it occurred to him that the increase of in,nates might have arisen in that manner. The matter dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ADIVIRAL AND THE TELEPHON'E.</head>
        <p>THE ADIVIRAL AND THE TELEPHON'E.</p>
        <p>Admiral Jones. Parry asknqi the Cr:airman whether he would ask the Chairman of the House Committee, what had become of the telephone? The Chairman (indicating the telephone to the p3rters room) i is there. (Laughter.). Mr. Norman , He does not refer to that one. Admiral Jones PArry : Oh, yes. It is all right; but I thought I saw someone go out with a message just now. (Renewed laughter.)</p>
        <p>.........40-........ GREAT SANKEY PARISII COUNCIL</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in Great San- key Board Schools on Tuesday evening. Mr T Hal- ton presided, and there were also present,:— Messrs W Taylor. Joseph Kilsbaw, Cooke, J Wright, Thomas Kilshaw, with J. Smith (clerk.) The Clerk said he attended at Preston in connection with the county rate basis, and the figures arrived at were as follows:—Gross, £10,649; rateable £9,272; and assessable £7,988. They appeared to have taken different calculations this time in arriving at the basis. Since Sir. Hulton's decease it was pro- bable that the new clerk had arrived at the basis of assessment by different calculations altogether to those which Mr. Hulton adopted. There was a difference of £243 between their present assessment and that fixed by the County Raze Basis Committee. —lt was decided to walk the boundaries of the parish on the last Friday in the month, starting at nine o'clock in the morning from Smallshaw-lane. —The Chairman thought that the Council should meet. more frequently daring the next six months las several important matters affecting the district would be brought before the Rural District Council. —lt was left with the Chairman to call a special Imeeting should important business render it neces- sary.—This was all the business.</p>
