        <head>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Mr G S</p>
        <p>Davies. Kee wick ;</p>
        <p>Mr GW Hurs C</p>
        <p>Hextable ; Mr H Walker, Sheffield; Mr P White Poplar; Mr C 0 Fiske, London ; Mr H Proctor Haslingden ; Mr A. &amp;sties, Manchester; Mr A Brad' bury, Oldham ; Mr R Johnson, Leicester; Miss Pear- son, Catford ; Mr F Arkwright, Leeds; Mr L Adamson. Newport. Mon. ; Mr W Shute, Newcastle ; Mr Jeffrey, Biggleswade ; Mr R Williams, Coventry ; Mr J Braithwaite. Horncastle ;Mr B Oxley, Reading; Mr P Phipps. Melton. Mowbray; Mr L A. Courtenay, Coventry; Mr A Darnell. London: Mr J MacDon...ld, Scarborough ; Mr E Reed Darlaston ; Mr J Fisher. Market Harborough ; Mr A Wilson, Salisbury ; Mr B Edwards, Ilfracombe; Mr S Foster, Nottingham Mr L Rae, Wolverhamp- ton ; Mr T Cave. London ; Mr L Pilkington, Dewsbury ; Mr 8 Matthew, Dover; Mr Herbertson Glasgow ' • Mrs Harrneworth, Birmingham ; Mr L Piggott, Whitehaven ; Mr R Piggott, Whitehaven ; Mr T Miles, Bradford; Mr A Shakespeare, Roade ; Mr B Noble, Leicester ; Mr J Hart, Turvey ; Mrs M Lancaster, Leeds ; Mr P Douglas, York ' • Mr A. Blake, Lancaster ; Mr A James, Fenny Stratford ; Mr G Manning, Leicester; Mr D Lavane, Swansea ; Mr J MacLeod. Edinburgh ; Mr T Lewis, Market Harborougb ; Mrs G Irvin, Grove Park ; Mr D Spencer, Chester; Mr L Harper, Doncaster ; Miss G Marshall, Blackpool; Mr S Deway,Canter- bury ; Mr F Blackbouse, Tring ; Mr. J Arnold, York; Mr 11 Levy, Kentish Town ; Mr E Sampson, Lewes ; Mr M Barlona, Carlisle • Mr, A Smith, Gateshead ; Mr R Sims. Gateshead ; Pir G A N Alliason, London; Mr Wifialfour, Daventry ; Mr P Markham. Northampton; Mr W Illiott, Maidstone; Mr F Mahe, Brighton ; Mr Henry English, Doncaster; Mr R Ingot, Melton Mowbray ; Mr T Ernmerson. Kettering ; Mr S Duckenfield, Deal; Mr W Scott, Gateshead, Mr J H Pearson, Warrington.</p>
        <p>£63 WON AGAIN. The following were the result of the matches played on October 7th :—Notts Forest beat West Bromwich Albion, Stoke beat Wolverhampton, Newcastle United beat Notts County. Sheffield United beat Manchester City, Burslem Port Vale beat Luton, Gravesend beat Reading. Chesterfield beat Woolwich Arsenal, Warrington beat Wigan, Bradford beat Manninghana, Leicester beat Exeter. The following competitors gave ten correct results, and therefore divide the £5O prize : Mr W Macey, 1, Plainmoor ICottages, St. Mary's Church, Devon. Mr A Chaplin, 7. Grosvenor-road, Tunbridge. Mr J North, Lennox Villas. Highfield-sireet, Mr S Price. Weedon, near Daventry. Mr C Crichton. 415, Sauchiehall-street, Glasgow. Mr E Maple, Oaklands. Bushmead-avenue, Bedford. Mr Gerald Adams, The Limes, Bentley (two correct coupons). Mr C Wood, the Grange, Seaforth, Sussex. Mr Arthur, 52, Beulah-road, Tunbridge Wells. TEN LOCAL WINNERS The following competitors gave nine correct results, and therefore divide the £lO prize :—Mr S Baylis. 3, Keen-street Warrington ; Mr S Clayton, 2, Keen-street, Warrington ; Mir W Littlefair, 6, Florence-street, Latchford ; Mr C Turner, Fearnheari, Warrington; Mr W T Kips, Spring View, Near Walton, Warrington ;Mr J H Renton. 23, Bold-street, War- rington •Mr G Makin. 63 Wakefield-street, Warrington ; Mr Albert Butterworth, 146 Knutsford-rood, Latchford. Warring- ITIE ton ;Mr Arthur Lawton, 63, Priestley-street. Warrington; ACCRECATE PRIZE. Mr J Tucker. Exeter; Mr H Bond, Colne :Mr 3 Hodges. The Coupon prize of £3 has been won by Mr E Pridgeoli, Coalcille. East; Mr S Price, Walsall; Mr E Pace, Blakenhan 3, West Park Grove. Granville-street. Heath, Near Walsall ;Mr 0 Miller, Spllsby,- Mr W H Bull, The winners of the £59 prize have had four points sada Bath ; Mr H 3 Johnson. Woadchester ;Mr 0 W Stanley, placed to their credit for theaggregate prize, and the winners Stroud; Mr W Powell, Bowbriclo, ; Mr H Twinitn;, The of the £lO wiz, one point each. Thrupp, near Stroud ;Mr T H Harris. ulymouth ; Mr H E ANOTHER SCRUTINY.—WARRINCTON WINNER. Helmar, Devonport ;Mr T H Gilpin. St. Budeaux. Devon ; One claim for a scrutiny in the competition decided last Mr W Foster, Plymouth; Mr G Bathman, Rotherham ;Mr week having proved correct, the name of Mr. J. Warburton. J r Ancock, Waingrove, Denby ;Mr B Morris, Alfreton ;Mr 6, Eldon-street, Warrington, has been added to the list of J Randall, Harland ; Mr D Jackson. Penishaw ; Mr E competitors who gave nine correct results, This competiton Pridgeon, Hull; Mr W H T Wood, Truro.; Mr liii thertfore share the £lO consolation prize with there B W Ingram, Uppingharn ; Mr B C Bruce. London; I winners whose names appeared last week. LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH,. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Ithe ably° mission were continued on Sunday. The I pulpit at both services was occupied by the Rev. H. A. ! Lester, of the Warrington Traiaing College. In the at ternooa a children's service was held, when the Rev. E. R. Tarbuck, vicar of St. Paul's, delivered an address which was listened 0 with ereat interest.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with this church was begun on Friday evening, when the special preacher was the Rev. George Paters, M.A.. vicar of St. Thomas', Liverpool. The church had been beautified with the flowers and fruits of the earth, the decorations, as in other years, being of considerable merit from the artistic point of view. On Sunday there was a celebration of the Holy Commuaion at eight o'clock. The preacher at the fbrenoon service was the Rev. C. R. Killick. M.A., vicar, while in the afternoon the Rev. N. J. Poole - (curate), gave an instructive address t the younger members of the church. Toe preacher in the evening was the Rev. Canon Wood, k1.9...wh0 founded a foreitee sermon on the text " Do men gather grapes of therne,or figs of thistles." The choir gave an impressive rendering of the anthem" Blessed be the name of the Lord." Mr. Timmins' services at the organ were an indispensable element in the proceedings. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCEI, RUNCORN. The harvest festival was observed on Sunday. Special sermons on the subject of the season were Preached morning and evening by the Rev. J. D. Evans, of Garston. In the afternoon a service specially for scholars was held, at which the Rev. J. D. Evans gave a thoughtful and instructive address. The choir rendered a programme of festive music, and their treatment of several specially-selected anthems NW the subject of favourable comment on all sides. The church was appropriately decorated in keeping with the rrz c4sion it he ladies of the congregation having bestowed much pains and care to make the chapel as bright and attractive as possible. The buildinsr was crowded in the evening. ST. AMBROSE CHURCH, WIDNES. In connection with the St. Ambrose Church. Halton View, the harvest thanksg:ving services were held on Sunday, for which the sacred edifice had been most tastefully decorated. The pulpit was occupied in the morning by the Rev T S Lea, and in the evening by the Rev Samuel Gasking,chaplain of Walton Workhouse. In the afternoon a special service was held for the children. Appropriate harvest hymns and music were sung, and, by special request, at the evening service, Miss J. Wil- liams and Miss Lewis sang the duet "Oh lovely peace,' MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with the Mersey Mission to Seamen was held on Thursday. October 5,1 h, and was continued on the following Sunday. The church was beautifully decorated with an abundance of grain, flowers, fruits, and vegetables, and presented an unusually bright appearance. The lady decorators were Mrs R Shaw, Mrs Evans, Mrs Mack, Mrs fluetws. Mrs Locket, Mrs Leach, Mrs Cliff, Miss Coward, Miss Dyer, Miss Bowyer, and Miss Ga.rvie. The preacher on Thurs- day evening was the Rev. F: E. Hicks, hon. chaplain to the Mersey Mission Rock Ferry, who gave an appro- priate sermon. The Rev. Canon Maitland Wood. M.A., vicar of Runcorn, occupied the pulpit on Sunday morn- ing, and also administered the Holy Communion. The service in the afternoon, specially for children, was con- ducted by Mr. J. W. Davies, hon, licensed lay reader, Farnworth. The evening sermon was preached by Mr. J. Stokes, hon, licensed lay reader. All the services were well attended. Special hymns and anthems were sung by the choir, and the proceedings were alto; ether a distinct success. CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHURCH. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday and Monday last, the pretty chap I of Camden. which readily lends itself to anything in the nature of decoration, was most tastefully and profusely set out with fruit, corn, grasses, Ate., arranged artistic- ally here, there, and everywhere. The occasion was the holding of the first harvest home, and it may be said that the work of the decorators was of so nigh a standard that the palm for the season's festivities mutt go to Camden easily. The serviees of Sunday were con- ducted by the Rev. J. A• B. Malvern, and on monday were continued by the Rev. H. Mnrrison, and at each service large congregations listened attentively to the utterances of both eloquent preachers. Mr. Malvern's discourses being especially eloquent and telling. On Monday night there was a sale of fruit, after which a large number of friends of the chapel stayed and par- took of a supper. The collections and proceeds of the gathering amounted to over £l2 which was considered I highly satisfactory. ST. GEORGE'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BOLD-STREE I" WESLEYAN CEIURCH.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREE I" WESLEYAN CEIURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services were held in this place of worship on Sunday, the pulpit in the morning being occupied by Mr. H. Thorne (London), who preached from St. Mark iv. 21. In the evening the pulpit Was occupied by the Re v.G.H. Robrrts,M.A.,who spoke upon the words'* Not chat I speak in respect of want, for I have learned in whatsoever state I am, therefore, to be content." There were very large congrogations,especially in the evening. The chapel this year was most taste- fully decorated and called forth the praise of the large congregationF, great praise being due to the foltowing ladies :—Mesdamcs Harrison, G Weaver, Davey, Holford W Isaac, and the Misses Weaver, Barlow, Collin, g Jones. Special music and anthems were ably renders&amp; by the choir, the solos being most praisawortbily taker* by Miss Taylor, Miss Rawling and Mr. L. Richardson. The anthems were " 0 clap your hands" and" The eyes of all." Mr. Harrison ably presided at the organ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ALL HAIM'S' CHURCH, THELWALL.</head>
        <p>ALL HAIM'S' CHURCH, THELWALL.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival was celebrated on Sunday in the ab3ve church and both services were well attended. The decorations had been very tastefully executed by a number of the ladies of the parish. The pulpit at the morning service was occupied by the Rev. Johnson. Barker, curical secretary of the Waifs and Strays Society and at the evening service by the Rev. F. H. Powell, organizing secretary of the Additional Curates Society. At each service sr ecial hymns and anthems were sung, and collections werl, made in aid of the Waifs and Strays Society. LATCHFORD PRIMITIVE MEPHODIST CHAPEL. Harvest serviva were held on Sunday,and were largely attended. The chapel was tastefully decorated with flowers, fruit and corn, contributed by the members of the congregation. Mr. Morton of Warrington, preached at both services. Special anthems and hymns were ren- dered by the choir. Mr. J. Massey presided at the organ. On Mmiday evening a fruit banquet was held, when the chair was tsheo by Mr. Massey. An excellent pro- gramme was pr3vidsd, and a very pleasant evening was spent.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SERIFS OF</p>
