      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A NEGLIGENT FATHER. SENT TO PRISON. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday, Frederick Cooper, a joiner, residing at 106, Hardy-street, was charged with neglecting to maintain his wife and four children whereby they became chargeable to the Com- mon fund of the Warrington Union.—He pleaded not guilty.—Mr. B. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Guardians. He said that prisoner's wife and four child- ren 2ged 12, 9,9, and 6 respectively, were admitted to the Workhouse on the 29th September, through his neg- lied. Be was a joiner and could earn close up•in £2 a week but he had not given his wife any money for seven weeks. He had not qiven her above £5 or £6 during the last twelve months. A month ago he told his wife that he had got work but could not get his tools out of pawn as he could not raise sufficient money. She worked and got him the money, but instead of going to work,he again pawned the tools and spent the money in drink. Re had get himself into such a state that he could not work, and he (Mr. Shearer) had been repeatedly told about the case by outsiders.—Mary Cooper gave evid- ence in support of this statement, and prisoner who had previously been convicted for neglect of family was cymmitted to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>THEFT IN A iEBLIC-HOUSE.-9. WARRINGTON WOMAN</p>
        <p>SENT TO GAOL.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Coart on We3nes- day, b'fore Mr. Geddes (in the chair), Alderman James Fairclough and Mr. Birch&amp;ll, Mary Moran, or Molloy a middle aged woman. was charged with stealing from a table at the Cross Keys Inn, Buttermarket-street, at 9.45 on Monday night 305., the money of Alfred Booth, a fitter, residing at 186, Winwick-road. She pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that shortly before eleven o'clock on Monday night Booth, who was slightly under the influence of liquor, went into the Cross Keys Inn, with the prisoner, and asked for two drinks, but the landlady, seeing his and the woman's condition, refused to serve them. Booth then put his hand into his pocket and pulled out two sovereigns and one half-sovereign, and said" Do you think I have not the money to pay for it." Whilst placing the money on the counter, one of the sovereigns dropped en the floor, and when he stooped to pick it up the prisoner pounced upon the 303. and put it into her stocking. The police were sent for, and Sergeant Wil- liamson came upon the scene. Booth being somewhat excited, refused to give his name and address and the prisoner made off,bat she was apprehended in a lodging- house, at Friars'-green, on Tuesday night. The prisoner it appeared, went to a lodging-house in Dial- street, and sent one of the lodgers out for a bottle of whiskey, giving him a sovereign. Evidence in support of this statement having been given a charge was preferred against Peter Woods, with whom the woman lived. Toe case having been proved, the Chief Constable said the man had been before the magis- trates no less than forty times. —The woman was sent to prison for a rronth with hard labour and the man was committed forltwo months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Hepherd (in the chair), Alderman Roberts, Councillor Smethurst and Mr. S. Wright.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING CRUELTY TO A WlPE.—George Holmes was charged with pi'sistent cruelty to his wife Mary Holmes, 61, Wilderspool-road.—Defendant, who appeared in court in a state of intoxication, pleaded not guilty.-111r. A. Br owns, who appeared on behalf of codtplainant, said that defendant was a carter. For some weeks past he had only given his Wife 4d., Bd., and Is. a week. They were married at St. Paul's Church on the 26th September. 1883, and resided at SI, Wilderspool-road. Mrs. Holmes took in professionals from the theatre in order to make a living. On Sep tember 23rd defendant came home drunk and offered his wife Sd as a weekly allowance. She refused to take it and he commenced to cause a disturbance, but she took no notice cf him, Oa Monday he again came home drunk and asked for some supper. She would not give him any be- cause he had not given her any money. whereupon he rushed at bet% knocked her backwards over a chair and struck her several times about the face and head miking her a terrible black eye which was there still although it was done a fort- night ago. His daughter interfered and he gave her a good flushing, He then commenced to throw the crockery about and broke a lot of things. He repeated his conduct on Saturday night and on Monday night. Previous to when the assault took place he bad thrashed his daughter and torn her clothing. He had done everything possible to agravate her to do something she ought not to do. Com- plainant put him out on Monday night and she now asked for a separation order to be made with an allowance of at least 85 or 10s a week.—Eyidence in support of the above statement was given by the complainant and her daughter.— In answer to the Chairman defendant said he g,t Li Is Od per week.—A. separation order was granted, defendant to contribute 85 a week towards the maintenance of his wife and children. AN APPRENTICE SUMNIONED.—Kate Price. 16, Stephenson. street. an apprentice to Messrs. Platt, Wilkinson and Co , Limited, was summoned for leaviny, her work on the Ist September, thereby putting the firm to a loss of Wilkinson proved the case, and an order was made for de- fendant to return to her work and pay the amount together with the costs. WIPE ASSAULTS—Dennis Dolan, 24, Ellen-street. was sum moned for assaulting his wife. Ann Dolan, on the 2nd Octa- ber.—He pleaded not guilty.—Complainant odd that on the date in question defendant kicked her and thumped her on the bed. Defendant who denied the oft?,nc,t said his wife was a dangerous woman airi he was afraid of her.—Defen• dant was bound over to keep tho peace and ordered to pay the costs (105.)—John Gillighan, 3, Farmers Brow was fined be. and costs for assaulting his wife. Mien (itillighan on the 23rd September. - - -</p>
        <p>- MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alderman Blaekhurst, Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst, and</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Birchall. LIRE A RAVING MlNlAC.—Bridget Curley. Friars Green, was charged with being drunk and dis,,rderlv in Cairo-st., at 7.20 on Saturday night. —P.O. Monks said that at the time named he was on duty in Sankey-street when he received information that prisoner was lying in an nalapit off Cairo-street. He proceeded to the place and found her lying full length in the ashpit fast asleep. He awakened her and found she was very drunk. She becam • very disorderly and used filthy language, and he was obliged to take her to the Pollee Statien.—The Chief Constable said he saw the Constable taking the woman to the Police Station and she did not seem to be very drunk. When she got to the station, however, she became like a raving maniac and her language was frightful. She only came out of gaol on Saturday morn- ing after serving a month for drunkenness. She had been before the court nine times for being drunk and disorderly, four times since June. He did not think her condition was altogether due to drink. She seemed to be partially de- mented. Superintendent Monaghan who had known her for some time said she was formerly a very respectable woman, and was hard working and of cleanly habits. She had bad Children and they had knocked her about till she was only half-witted. He asked the magistrates to extend their Clemency to her that morning ; she had never been let off before.—Prisoner was discharge lon promising to go to the Workhouse. A LOCAL TERROR —Mary Grimes, of no fixed address, was charged with being drunk in Cockhedge-lane at 1.15 on Sun- day morning.—P.S. Bell proved the case.—The Chief Con- stable said the woman was one of their local terrors. She did not really belong to Warrington, but she would persist In coming to the town when she was discharged from gaol. She had been there 19 times since October, 1894, and had 14 days on September 12th.—Fined 5s and casts or seven days. A NEGLECTFUL HUSBAND.—Joseph Turner, last from Scot- land, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Liver- pool-roadat 8 o'clock on Sunday night,and also with not paying arrears in maintenance due to his wife amounting with the ,costs to £2 4s 6d.—P C. Woods said that he saw prisoner In Liverpool-road at the time named. He was drunk and said</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>he would kill his wife. Knowing that there was a warrant out for the arrest of the 'nen for arrears in mainten- once he took him to :the Police Station.— Mary Ann Turner said her husband had not paid her any money since the order was made.—The magistrates decided to send prisoner to gaol for a month for not paying the arrears, and a month for being drunk and disorderly. DISGRACEFUL SCENT.EI IN DIAL•STREET—FIGHTING WITH KNIVES AND RATCHETS.. -13-iciget O'Brien 7, silver-street and Ca oline Coyne. 11, Dial-street, were charged with being drunk and disorderly •in Dial-street at 10.55 on Sunday night. —P.S. Williams said that at the time in question he and P.S Evans were on duty at the corner of Whittle-street when they received a comotaint of a disturbance taking place in Dial-street On proceediig there they found the prisoner Coyne standing in the middle of the road. She was drunk and creating a disturbance by shouti.ig. They endeavoured to ,'et her into the house but she became wares, The second prisoner then came out screaming and alleged that Coyne had struck her on th- head with a knife. They took Coyne into custody and the other prisoraer followed lath/Lying in a disorderly manner, so she was also locked up. 011rien was taken to the Infirmary to have the wound on her head attended to. —The Chief Constable said the wound was found to be of a trivial nature, and migtr have been caused with a knife. P.S. Williamson had appended to his report a state- ment to the effect that the prisoners and O'Brien's husband had for some considerable time been giving an almost Inca!- culable amount of trouble to the police. On Saturday night the house at Dial-street was visited half-it-dozen Liaise. Coyne and the husband of the other prisoner quarrel- ling. A great deal of drinking went on in the house after closing time, the place being sometimes crowded. One of the curses of Warrington was drin!cing in private houses. Prisoners would hit one another with hatchets and knives but as tne affrays always took rola oe ii doers the police could not interfere. O'Brien had been b.-2fore the court 14 times and Coyne. nine times previously.—Prisoners were committed to gaol for a month. FIGHTING IN CHURCH.STREET.--/SHISS Highans, 7, Roger- street. and George Thorlev,l, Gilbert-street,were sum mooed lor committing a breach of the peace lay fighting in Church- street o'i the 2nd inst.—P,S. Beans proved the case, and de- fendants were bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs (7s).</p>
        <p>TUESDLY—Before Mr. Birchen and Councillor Smethurst. LIcENIIING,—James Spencer Earle applied for temporary authority to sell at the " Green Dragon " Inn, in lieu of Sarah Forel. and Robert Walker applied for temporary authority to sell at the Talbot Inn, in lieu of Pater Holt Lake. The ap- plication. were granted. WIVE AS9AULT—SEPARAIION ORDER GRANTED.—The ad- journed case in which Jolla Garnett was charged with assaulting his wife, Rachel Garnett, on the 10th September, again came on for hearing.—The Magistrates Clerk explained that the case was adjourned on defeadant promising to sign the pledge and behave differently towards his wits—Com- plainant stated that her husband had kept having drink just the same but was quleter.—Clefendant said that he had had no beer. He had beel very ill and had taken whisky to make him better.—Complainant said she wanted a separation order as defendant was not safe to live with when he had drink.—Defendant.—lt is spite and nothing else—Complain- ant restated the facts of the case which were to the effect that her husband hit her on the back with a shovel.—Defendant was fined 10s. for the assault, and a separation order was granted elefen4ant to pay 10s. a week towards the mainten- ance of his wife and family. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For being drunk and dis- orderly in Bridge-street at 640 on Mond L 7 evening. Edwin Andrews, Friars Green, was fined 58. and costs.—P.C. Taylor proved the case. IRREGULAR ATTENDANCE AT SCKOOL.—The following per- sons were dealt with tor neglecting to Bead their children to school regularly. Mr. W. Jones, chief school atteudance officer, proved the eases :—John Mills, Dyehouses, child sent to the Hightown Truant School, Liverpool; John Birchall, 3, Wakefield-street, fined 28 6d Charles Packer, 37, Gari- baldi-street, fined 5s ; Adam Lester, 14, Admiral-street, fined 28 6d ; John Knight, 9, Sparling-street, fined 28 ; John Hans- bury, 6 Admiral-street. fined is ; Thomas Caldwell, 166, Church-street, order for child to attend school ' • William Owen. Old London-road, case adjourned; Edward Wellings, 40, Pierpoint-street. fined 2s 6d ; Joseph Wright. Price-st., order made for child to attend school; Thomas Griffin, 2,old- gardens. fined is ; Edward Smith, 72, Cartwright-etreet,tined 28 6d ; Thomas Wright, fined Sc; and Henry Newton, fined 2s 6d.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDA.Y.—Before Mr. Geddes (in the chair). Alderman Fakelough and Mr. Birchall .</p>
        <p>ARREARS IN Si AANT-ENANCE.—William If en ry Skidmore was chargellwith not paying arrears in maintenance due to his wife, amounting with the coats to £lO 63 6d.—liis wife hay- ing given evidence, prisoner was ordered to pay the money Immediately or go to gaol for two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN PET BE33IONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PET BE33IONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before Hems. Henry Rey aolde (chairman) and</p>
        <p>John Ockieston.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS CisE9.- -John Rollinson, of Ashton, sum- moned by the police, for being drunk. on the 29th of Sep- tember. pleaded guilty, and was fined ss. The case was proved by ice Sergeant Bennett. -William Sutton. and Benjami Kv. both ot Weston, pleaded guilty to being drunk *slid disorderly, in Heath-roa I, Weston, on the 30th of September.—Fined ss. and Bs. 6d. costs each, or 14 days in default.</p>
        <p>SCHOoL BOARD CASES--The following parents were pre- cee 'ed against by the Runcern School Board for neglecting to e,anse their children to attend school regularly ; and were fined 6d. and 4s. 6d. costs :—Tho earflughes. Boscowe-street; John Harding, Sbawestreet ; George Colecrofte. boats, (Z cases) ; James Ashcroft, boats (2 cases). —Bvidence was given Ly Sehool Attendance Officers Bowyer and Howard. AN UNHAPPY ALLEGATION AGAINST A WlFlL—Peter Noon, earthenware dealer and hawker, of Church-street Run- corn, was summoned by his wife, under the Married Woman,s Act, 1895, for wilfully neglecting her and refissing her reasonable maintenance, and by such neglect causing her to live apart from him. A separation and maintenance order was asked for. Mr. H. N. Linaker (Messrs. Linaker and I Linaker. solicitors). Runcorn, appeared for the complainant ; and Mr. F. A. Lake (Messrs. Day and Lake), Runcorn, for the defence.—Mr. Linaker said that on the 25th of September Noon, the defendant, ordered his wife out of the house. She refused to go, and he said if she did not go he would make her. A little later she went into the yard, and he rushee at her there. Being afraid that some bodily harm would be dens to her she went home to her mother. Her mother spoke to her and advised her to go back, end late that night she did go. When she did go back, being afraid, to go alone, she got a police-constable to go with her. She knocked at the door and her husband came. She asked him to take her in. Ile refused. She again asked him to take her in, a second time, in the preseace of the constable. but be replied on that occasion that he would not, but that he would take the child. She had been to the house several times subsequently, but was unable to get admission. Her husband had not contributed anything to her maintenance since the 25th ult.—Margaret Noon, the complainant, on oath, corroborated her solicitor's statement. Previous to the birth of the child May, her husband had assaulted her by striking her on the body and on the face. She could not tell exactly what her husband made out of his business but would think about £2 a week.—By Mr. Lake: Her married life had not been a very happy one. The unhappiness commenced before the child was born. —Mr. F. A. Lake for the defence, admitted the facts of the case ; they were agreed as to them. But he contended that Noon finding that his wife was unfaithful was justified in not maintaining her.—Peter Noon gave evidence supoorting his solicitor's statement.— fhe Chairm in observed it was a very unheppy case, could not some settlement be arranged between the parties ?.-Defendant: It is quite impossible with me, sir, I cannot undertake to live with her again.— The Chairman ; Well, seeing that there is not the slightest proof that this woman has committed adultery, we cannot believe your tale. We, therefore, make the separation order, and you have the costs to pay (21 12s. 6d ),and you will also have to contribute 10s. a week towards the maintenance of ycar wife. Seven PHySI HIS FoLLIES.—John Avias, a French seaman, was charged, in custody, with being drunk in High-street, on Saturday. the 7th of October, an offence he admitted.— P.C. Nicholls proved the ease —The Superintendent added that the man had been paid off,and was in the possesaioa of a large sum: —Fineel 55, DANGEROUS TO No ONE BUT HIM3ELY.—John Burke. of Mersey-3treet. Runcorn, wae charged in custody, with being drunk and disorderly in Granville-street, on the 7th of October. —He pleaded guilty.—Constable Edwards give evidence in support of the charge. —The Superintendent said prisoner had been before the Court on about 43 previous occasions, but did no hares to anyone but himself.—Fiaed 51 end 55 costs, or 14 days. —Burke went to prison. AN UNIQUE "PLAY " AT THE THEATRE.—John Welker, William Garvey, Leonard Sergeant. ana John Bowdon, four Runcorn boys, of respectable connection, were sumrnoaed for stealing a number of brass cheques, six pieces of chocolate, and a quantity of pears, the goods of William Henry Caddie, from the Rovaity Theatre on the 6th of October.—Mr. W. H. Caddie said hel was lessee and manager of the Royalty Theatre. On Friday the 6th of October. he was informed by the police that the Theatre had been broken into by the boys and on going there he missed a number of brass cheqees off a side shelf inside, and also a quantity of chocolate and pears the property of Mrs. Shalleross. He knew the defendants by sight. -There was a sliding door in the " what " which was fastened by a hasp, and he believed that the boys had obtained an entrance by using a knife to force the hasp. The door opened into Byron-street.—Mary Shaileross, wife of Mr. Thomas .ehalleross, resident in Dike-street, near to the Theatre, said, she heard a noise in the Theatre and in conse- cetence went to the Theatre door. She told a eine about her suspicions that some boys were in the theatre, and he to:d her to get a knife and op-n the door. She did so. On geine inside she saw Garvey and Saegeant. When the boys saw her they ran off and ultimately made their escape by the roof. She missed the articles mentioned.—Police Constable Thomas S Bowyer stated he received information of the affair and apprehended the defendants. They all made statements ter him.—ln answer to the charge Walker and Garvey pleaded not guilty, and Sargeint and Bowden guilty. They all elected to be summarily dealt with.— Mr. Caddie stated he had no desire to press the ease ; he was only desirous of it being a warning to the boys.—The Chairman (addressing the bey 3) : This sort of thing yon know will bring yen to jail e—You know that we'l ?—De- fendants : We do, str.—The Chairman : It is a very serious matter, but as Mr. Caddie does not wish to press the ease, and 99 we do not want to record a conviction against you. who are so young, we will discharge you if you promise not to do so again, simply binding you over to appear when called upon for judgment within the next 6 months.—The boys promised, and were bound over, in £1 to come epee desired. HoODWINICING A RUNCORN SHOEMAKER.—AN INGEN'OUS DODGE.—Mary Ellen Woo beard, aged 1.1 years, an intelligent little girl, who for some years has resided with Mrs. Amelia Clays, at Overton, Frodsham,was charged with the larceny of</p>
        <p>3 odd boots, the property of Mr. Thomas Cooke, shoemaker, of Church-street. Runcorn. on tees 4th of October, valued at tts —Thomas Cooke, said he was a shoemaker, and carried on business at 2), Church-street, Runt:am. About 10 oVock on the morning cf the 4th of October, the girl Woodward, 'acme to hia shop, and asked whether she could have some boots for Mrs. fileive to see. He as .ed her which Ira. &amp;leave. She replied in that street up there. He said "Vicar's-street "and she replied" yes." He :liked her name, and she said it was Woodward He got three pairs of boots, and cut- ting them apart gave her the three odd ones (all right fo-st), and she went out of the shop. He watched her and saw her go past Mrs. 0-leave's He afterwards made inquiries, and went to qrs. Gleave and to several Woodwerds' in different parts of the towe,bet could fled no tree., of the girl or boots. He then gave informa- tion to the police, and was t uld that the b»ts were in the Police Station. -Alfred Dodd, manager of a pawnshop at 53, prineess-street. stated about a quarter past ten o 4 the same date the girl came to his shod with the boots (produce I) and offered thews in pledge. Noticing t hey were odd ones he asked her where the others were, and how she became possessed of them. She repli3 I that she " got them fr tm home ; where else would she get them ?" and that she had left the others in a neighbour's named Mrs. Ball. He telci her that he did not think that she had corns by them honestly as they were new ones, and it was Isis in- tention of detaining her. She replied to him that they were her boots. and asked for them back, or she would fetch estuteone who would make him return them. Hventually she ran out of the seep before he could stop her, and as the youth who assisted him was out at the time, he was unable to go after her.—P.C. Bowyer said he :apprehended the girl and on charging her with the offence she made no reply The little girl in answer to Mr. Johnson (magistrates clerk) pleaded guilty, and decided to be tried by the nie.istrates.— She had nothing to 'say, beyond the fact that she resided at Frodsham ; lived with Mrs. Clays; her father did not work at all, but her mother was dead ; and she came herself to Runcorn that morning and that nobody had sent her. —Miss Wright, of Chester, was present In court and ex- pressed a desire to provide a suitable place for the girl where she would be brought up under better surroundings,provided a provision could be made that the father could not interfere and take the girl away,—.lers. Clays in answer to the Bench said that the father knew all about the girl's doings, but thomen she had seen him that very morning he had said nothing at all —The Chairman having received a promise from the girl, that, if she was allowed to go with Miss Wright she would be a better girl, dismissed the charge against her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
