      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13 1899</p>
        <p>"THE SECOND MOODY."</p>
        <p>EVcINGELIS T TELLS A SENIITIONSL STORY.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. l'oreEns, the well-known Eiglsh evangelist, often called "the second Moody," has (says a Glasgow paper), been telling a startling aril tru; story to the press. Mr. Powers is practical if he is anything, and his story is worth reprinting. B ira in 185$, at Hull, it was not till 1879 that Mr. Power; commenced nis special life work. Cumberland was hl3 first sphere, and there he Wad successful in evangelising the masses. He had fixed upon one of the p-,orest parts of the town tor operations. and often for fully an hour and a half before the opening of the doors a thousand persons were waiting to gain a imi•tancs. lit Sc)tland he is wall known as a to pular sea-coast preacher. His home, however, is at Earby, near Colne, Yorkshire. The addresses of Mr. Powers are earnest, full of pathos, and at times lightened by genuine humour, and he also enhacc s the service by rendering solos and accompanying himself on the melodeon. His daughter, too, is a thorough musician. But it is not of this highly interesting work we would spec Ally write (says the rep )rt quoted), but of the thrilling story which he gave to the journalist who waited upon him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RESCUE FROM DEATH,</head>
        <p>A RESCUE FROM DEATH,</p>
        <p>"In my time„' said Mr. Powers, " I have had four- teen severe attacks of influenz I, the illness usually, seizing me about the month of April. I remember in 1889 I was preaching in Ireland when I fell a viltim to it, and I might say, un the very point of death. Whea the influ- enza wore off, I was left as weak as water, and the after- effects were severe. I tried every rrm-dy poasible to cure me, but none had a permanent effect. Happening. most luckily, to hear of the wonderful cures which Dr. Williams' pink rills for pale people had wrought, I decided to give them a trial, with the happy result that Chi atter-effucte of the influenza from which I was suffer- ing entirely left me. My last attack was in March of this year, in the midst of my work in the Baptist Church at Earby. I had to be brought OUT OF BED VICE NIGHT TO THE PULPIT,</p>
        <p>and after the service go to bed again. It was at that time I began to take Dr. Williams' pink pilla. Before I had consumed the contents of two boxes I was as well as you see me now, and not since that time have I had the slightest recurrenc3 of the trouble, nor co I fear another attack."</p>
        <p>"And you owe to this your return to buoyant health?"</p>
        <p>"Certainly, and believing firmly in them, I also persuaded the people who were ill to use Dr. Williams' pink pills. I could cite numerous instances whore those who, acting on my snggestion, have taken them, and found good heaPh. I have made it a task of duty to inveatigate cases where,according to advertisements,Dr. Williams' pink pills had been taken, and I'll tell you of one.. A lady for three yeara suffered from a bad leg. In fact, the pain and condition of it made her think that she would have to undergo an operation, if not corn. pieta amputation. Everything was tried to mend that limb, but the disease obtained a firmer hold. She was persuaded to try Dr. Williams' pink pill 3, hardly be- lieving at first that they would do her good, but, to her amazemt‘nt, before she had got throu4h thre3 boxes all pain in her limb had gone, the swelling had abated, and to-Jay she is completely cured, able to walk long distances and do a whole day's work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HEARD IN- A TRAIN.</head>
        <p>HEARD IN- A TRAIN.</p>
        <p>"I was in a train a few weeks ago, when I met a Mrs. Holton, of Castle-street, Brierfield, Lancashire,and in the course of conversation learned Irma her that she had suffered for a long period from impoverished blood. S) far was her system down that a cmplica.tien of dis- eases set in. However, finding het looking remarkably healthy, I said. But you are now remarkably well, if I'm to judge by your face." Yes,' said she, and do you know t 3 what I attribute my return to good health? I tosk Dr. Williams' Pins Pills, and they cured me al- most at once !' Another case I might mention. It was at Carlisle. A Miss Lord, of Shadiongate, suff.red so much Ir3m poorness of blooi Etat she seemed to have on energy left in her, but she was recommended to take the pills, and in her case the result was the same. Sne was blcopletely restoret So gratified was she tbat she asked me to visit another lady in the same town who had been similarly affected with bad blnol, and who had been re- commended to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and who, after consuming the contents of several boxes, was per- manently cured." THE EVANGELIST'S WARNING.</p>
        <p>" In going about the three kingdoms I get to know of the wonderful cures which these pills of Dr. Williams' effect. Bat I must tell you further, sir, that as the re- sult of inviries, I have discoved that there has some- times been apparent failure with the pills ; hut the failure has, in my experience, always been due to the sufferers taking substitutes instead of the genuine Dr. Williams pink pills for pale people. Persons hearing of the value of Dr. Williams pink pills would enter a shop and say—"A box of pink pills," but they did not always get those that they meant to secure, and I wish to em- phasise the fact that when sufferers from any trouble go into a shop for a lenc of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people they should see that they get them, and not something made up as a substitute." Mr. Powers, whose testimony is perfectly voluntary and unbiassed, is the author of two books of poems, "Bright Rays of Light," and "The Garland of Praise," the former losing dedicated to Viscount Wolseley, by kis lordship's special permission. Mr. H. Powers' unstinted praise of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people will be easily comprehended when it is remembered that there is hardly a town or village in the kingdom but contains people whona they have cared of disease arising from an impoverished state of the blood, such as anmasia, pale and sallow complexion, general weakness, depression, loss of appetite, palpita- tion, shortn,ss of breath, pains in the back, nervous headache, early decay, all forms of female weakness. They have also cured numerous cases of rheumatism, Paralysis, locomotor ataxy, satica, and influenza. Taos., pills are not a purgative; take care that the pack- age hears the full name, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and in case of doubt send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Flolbora Viaduct, London, E.O , as the pills can no hi post free at 2 i 91, or six b mes for 135 9d. Pals sold loose, or from glass jars, are not Dr. Williams', but a useless imitation.</p>
        <p>WAILItINGTO'N INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY.- CONCERT AT PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>The annual concert in aid of the above Institu- tion was held in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening, there being a very large audience. Some first class artistes appeared and their contributions gave great, satisfaction. Mr. T. J. Down's well-known orchestra played selections of music with much ability, Mr. Down conducting. The pieces ren- dered by them were, march "Le Regiment Favori,' valse, " Mondnacht am Rhein" selection "Remini- scences of all nations," overture "Semiram incidental music to Shakespeares Henry VII. (Sir Arthur Sullivan), and galop (descriptive), "Cham- pagne." Miss Jennie Williams, contralto, sang a couple of solos with good effect, being encored for her interpretation of " When the heart is young" and singing in response " The Better Land" with feeling. Mr. A. F. Thornborough possesses a fine tenor voice which was heard to advantage in the songs "The last watch" and "The Irish Emigrant." He was awarded an encore for the last named contri- bution and in response sang "When other lips" for which he was loudly applauded. The humorous portion of the entertainment was in the hands of Mr. Harry Lawton, who kept the audience in roars of laughter throughout his performance. His Impersonations were particularly good. The play- ing of the National Anthem by the orchestra con- cluded the concert.</p>
        <p>- TO THD DHAF. —A gentlemln who cured himself of Deafness and Noises ;la the Head after fourteen years suffer- ing will gladly send full particulars of the remedy, on receipt et stamped addressed envelope.—F. CLIFTON, Asalberky lrbtae., Sbrand, London, W.C.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WIDNES V. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>WIDNES V. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest was evoked in this match at Widnes, the visitors proving successful by one goal and four tries to one try. Lawton (2), Davies, and Ellis crossed the line for Oldham, while Batey scored for Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ROCHDALE HORNETS v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ROCHDALE HORNETS v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The Hornets entertained the "Saints," whom they defeated by two goals and one trylto nil. Field and Allen each dropped a goal. and Boothroyd gained the try.</p>
        <p>SALFORD v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>Nearly 20,000 people visited the Salford ground to see this match, which was stubbornly contested, Swinton winning by one goal and three tries to two tries. Lewis, Goodman, and Hampson obtained the winners' tries, while Pearson and T. Williams crossed the line for Sal- ford.</p>
        <p>LEIGH v. MILLOM.</p>
        <p>On their own ground, Leigh were much superior to their opponents, and won easily by two goals and four tries to two tries, obtained by Eger A and Latham. Pear3ona Caldecot, Greenhalgh, and Ganley gained ths victors' tries, and Molyneux dropped a goal.</p>
        <p>STOCKPORT v. BROUGFITON RA.NGERS.</p>
        <p>The meeting of these clubs at Stockport resulted in a most excellent struggle, Stockport, though playing only fourteen men in the second half, winning by one goal and one try to one goal. Oram for Stockport, and James for the Rangers, scored.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENEOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENEOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>LWERSEDGE v. HULL.</p>
        <p>The home team completely out p'a yed their opponents at Hull, the final result being in their favour by one goal and three tries to nil. Savage, Meek and Jacques each secured tries, the last-named player clnverting one. HUDDERSFIELD v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Leeds were strongly represented at Huddersfield, but suffered a crushing defeit to the extent of four goals and five tries to nil. Cowley. Readyhough, Dewburst, Taylor and Dickenson gained the tries. Lorriman kick- ing the goals, one being frorn a mark.</p>
        <p>iIOL-BECK v. 'WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</p>
        <p>A close contest was witnessed on the Holbeck ground, the locals winning by two goals and two tries to three goals and one try, or ten points to nine. Metcalfe (2) and Kerstaw scored for Wakefield, while McCarthy obtained two tries for Holbeck, Heinslev converting. MANNINGHAM v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Fully 16,000 people viewed this contest at Bradford, where the home team were returned the winners by four goals and two tries to nil. Prole and Pollard gained the tries, and Calvert dropped two goals, while Cooper pl4ced two more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>BRAMLEY v. BA.TLEY.</head>
        <p>BRAMLEY v. BA.TLEY.</p>
        <p>The score at half-time in this ms.tch, at Bramley, was a try (Clarke) to nil in favour of the home side. After- wards Spurr and Goodall obtained tries, one of which Davies converted, and Garner dropped a'goal for Batley, who won by two goals and two tries to one try. _ LEEDS PARISH CHURCH v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>Leeds was the venue of this match, which was won by Hunslet by three goals and two tries to two goals. Wilson, A. Golttvarpe, s.nd W. Golthorpe were among the scorers for Hunslet, while Mosley dropped two goals for Leeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CASTLEFORD v. HALIFAX</head>
        <p>CASTLEFORD v. HALIFAX</p>
        <p>The Halifax team paid a visit to Castletord, when the local representatives won a good game by three goals and one try to nil. Brady obtained a try, Townsend converting, and the same players each dropped a goal. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>In this match at Hull the home fifteen were left easy winners by two goale and six tries to nil. Debney and Morita largely costributed to the winners' total.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" v. WIGAN "A."</p>
        <p>This match was played on the Wfiderspool enclosure on Saturday before a good attendance. Swift kicked off for Warrington who, from the start, held the upper hand, and play had not been in progress many minutes before Hockenhull scored a try, which, however was not improved upon. From a scrimmage Fish go away and scorecl the second try for Warrington, which was con- verted. After xcitia.c., play, Mason scored another try, and Fish almost kicked a goal.the ball strikina the cross bar. During the remainder of the first half, Fish, who showed capital form scored two more tries, and when the interval arrived Warrington was leading by 1 goal 5 tries to nil. On resuming, Wigan showed up more prominently, and for a time play was of an even char- acter, until Hallam scored a try for tile home team. Further tries were scored by Camnbell and Moores. Wil- cox placing' a goal. Final : Warrington, two goals eight triea (28 points) ; Wigan, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RANGERS V. RUNCORN RECREATION.—At Latchford. During the first half the play was even,and though the Rangers attacked thr3ugh clever play by Dagnall, nothing accrued, and half-time arrived with no score. During the second half, Latchford early attacked, but were driven back,and in quick succession the Recre- ation scored two tries, neither of which was converted. Final : Runcorn Recreation, two tries ; Latchford Ran- gers, nil. FLETCHER RUSSELL'S V. BIRICENHE&amp;D WANDERERS "A."—Played at liovely-lane on Saturday. The home team were without their half backs, their places being filled by T Antrnbus mad Lawless, whilst the visitcrs were strongly represented. Birkenhead won the toss and the game was at once taken to the home quarters, where after a few minutes Ingana was given an open- ing, which he failed to take. Lawless eventually clear- ing. The result was that Fletcher's paid a visit to the other end, but their stay was brief, as the full back pun- ted to centre, where Jolley was tackled. From the scrimmage which ensued Forster got possession and passed to Nelson, who was put in touch at the corner. Birkenhead attacked and T Knight effected a capital save. Not to be denied. Birkenhead took play on the Warrington line, but Lawless was responsible for a speedy clearance, and following up tackled the Birken- head full hack, before he could return. From the scrimmage which followed T Antrobus got poss,ssion and passing by the three-quarters J Antrobus was enabled to open the scoring for the house team. The goal kick was a failure. The rest,.rt claw the visitors CLD2B away with detnmination and T Knight was called</p>
        <p>" 51, Harrow Road, Llndon, W., " February 9th, 1899. _</p>
        <p>operations."</p>
        <p>trrom a photograph.)</p>
        <p>It is an undeniable fact that cod-livir oil, when properly administered, has a favourable effect upon digestion, and, in fact, upon the entire alimentary canal. This fact has been demonstrated repeatedly by scientific experiments, and it has an important bearing upon the care of children. Many of the ills of children come from weakness of digestion, and are especially noticeable in those children who have begun the battle of health in a state of low vitality- The way coi-liver oil can be administered with confidence in the results, is told in the following letter:--</p>
        <p>. , "Dear Sirs,—When my son—JohnChurchill Nash —was a baby be was a very delicate child, and seemed to be subjcct to various ailments. He had gastricjdisturbancos at two years old, which be— earns very serious, and he had to go to a hospital for operations, until we did not think he could live through his difficulties. When in this weak ema- ciated condition a friend advised us to try Scott's Emulsion. We did so, with the result that he gained strength, and was eventually restored to health by this preparation. He is now eight years old and is</p>
        <p>upon repeatedly. The Wanderers maintained the pre; sure, and after a lengthy attack the full bick dropped a goal. Ingam was hurt in endeavouring to save, with the result that he had to be carried off the field. At half-time the score was.—Fletcher Russell's one try, Bir- kenhead Wanderers one goal. The second half was un- interesting throughout. Phial :—Fletcher Russell's, one try (three points) ; Birkenhead Wanderers A," one goal,(two points).</p>
        <p>which became very serious, and he had to go to a hospital for</p>
        <p>a healthy child. I have found Scott's Emulsion o great benefit to children when cutting their teeth. In fact, I think that this is the best remedy which could be employed for children's maladies. —Yours very truly, "(Signed) Edward John Nash."</p>
        <p>The advantages of Scott's Emulsion over other forms of cod-liver oil are made apparent to any— body who will take the trouble Co try this prepara— tion. Instead of taxing digestion in dealing with the oil. Scott's Emulsion requires almost no diges— tive effort, and rather than being a hard remedy to take, Scott's Emulsion is palatable and is looked upon with favour, especially by children. Scott's Emulsion presents cod-liver oil in its best form and it also supplies hypophosphites and glycerine,both of which are of advantage in all cases where cod- liver oil can be employed with benefit. This is merely a statement of facts,as experience will clear- ly_sbow.</p>
        <p>- You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by Fend ing threspence to covnr postige to Scott and Bowne, Limited, Manufacturing Cbemists, 95,Great Saffron Hill, London, E. C., and mentioning this, paper. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>Woodward cleared the danger. Give-and-'ake play then followed, and after good passing between Heins and Murray, the latter had a long shot which just went wide. Immediately after a goal-kick Warrington were granted a free kick, and Graham sent the ball into the net, but the ball had not been touched in transit. Warrington still played up well, and Mather was conspicuous by a fine bit of play. Warrington con- tinued to have the best of the play, and D. Jones had an ineffectual shot at goal. Prescot now began to press,and a fine combined movement between Lyon, Henry, and Chorley ended in the latter defeating Aspinall with a difficult shot. After the restart D. Jones served his for- wards well, and the ball was centred to Connor, who, however, centred too high, and thus lost an almost cer- tain chance of equalising. Preston bore down on the Warrington goal, and Aspinall had great difficulty in negotiating a hot shot. He did so, hewever, and from a free kick Warrington obtained a corner. A dangerous movement, led bY Murray and Cannor, almost led to Prescot's downfall, the ball bobbing about the goal in a tantalizing fashion. Riley now had a fine chance, for he had almost an un- opposed run in, but passed instead of shooting near the goal. Warrington tried hard to equalize before the in- terval, and one shot by Riley hit the upright. At the interval Warrington were pressing. but Prescot were leading by one goal to nil. In the second half Warring- ton commenced in earnest, and brilliant play by the for wards was responsible for the gang° hovering near Grange's charge. Twice in ae many minutes the Prescot goal had narrow escapes, and it was not until ten minutes had elapsed that Murray served Connor well, and that player after trick- ing Alcock steadied himself, and a lightning shot put the sides on level terms, Graege having no chance. The Warringtonlans maintained the aggressive and fine work by Rains, Riley and Sergearat Jones was duly applauded. Connor obliged with several smart sprints and a fine centre was intercepted by Cardin, who was checked at half-way by Mather. Another attempt to cross the half- way line was prevented by Downie, and eventually Grange was defeated by Being. A minute later a long shot by Murray took effect, and though Present made frequent incursions into Warrington territory it was all to no purpose, Aspinall showing fine work in goal. The leaders attacked in the closing 'bees without result,and at the close Warrington were winners by three goals to one.</p>
