      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <p>LN view of the approaching Football settioa. it may be ill- teresting to state that the world-famed manuf ,cturers. Geo. G. BUSSEY a Co., of London. are preparing a stock of Balls, which for quality. aniformity, ant duribility, will be unique. These bWs will be distinguished by the appearance thereon of their well known trade mark *NW. Ask the local Sports Dealer for their Catalogue. If not procurable, apply to 36 et 38, Queen Victoria-street London. Factories —Peckham, London; and Innis well, Suffolk.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CC Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the Head Office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, n3t later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. --- LANCASHIRE SENIOR. COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. --- LANCASHIRE SENIOR. COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGtON WIN AT WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Warrington's fixture on Saturday waswith Wiran,the wooden-epoonists of the Lancashire Senior Competition, and they naturally viewed the event from a hopeful standpoint. Moreover they were not doomed to dis- appintment as at Rochdale. but emerged triumphant at Wigan's expense. The Wiganers were not very strongly repreernte I even for them, Smith and Straughan being two prs ninent absentees from the three-quarter line. Warrington took a strong team away with them, the same fifteen as that which defeated Widnes, turning out. The beet of weather —bright and cool—favoured the matah, and the ground also being in fair order, play was of a fast charawer. Even yet the Warrington match is a popular one at Wigan, in memory of the old cup-fight- inr days no doubt, and there was a good ring of about 4.000 spectators present. Mr. RancJck, of Ruacorn, was referee and the teams turned out in the following order.</p>
        <p>Warrington : Ashurst, full-back; Wood, Jones, Isher- wood. and Harris, three-quarters ; J T Bate and Duck- worth, half backs ; faylor, Naylor, Morrison, Fairhurst, Boardman, Scholtze, Eden and Sudlow. forwards. Wigan : Lewis, full-back ; Ball, Anderton, White snd Fairelouc,,h, three-quarters ; Dixon and Owen, half- b mks ; Ellison, Yates, Hilton, Blackburn, Turner. Pres- cott, Rigby and Brown, forwards. Warrington won the toss, and Bate elected to face the sun in the first half, but had a slight slope in his favour. Briwn started and Harris returned, but the initial movements were in favour of Wigan, who gained a good position in the Warrington half by strong forward play. They were soon sent back however by the Warrington front rank, who dribbled to centre in fine style. Ashurst was early called upon but made a good catch and kicked to midfield, where Fair- dough lost the ball in touch. Isherwood and Wood put in punts which carried play into the enemy's qualters, Ashurst stopping a rush by the home forwards. Dixon nhanged the venue with a nice break away and pass to Ball, who ran down to the Warrington 25 line, where Harris barred the way. After a useless penalty against Warrington, Dxon was again to the fore, and beating Bate and Duckworth should have led to a score had he only been supportsd. The home backs had only a poor notion of combination, however, and the movement was checked. Up to this stage Warrington had only shaped moderately, but they cow rallied and Wigan were soon driven back. Wood and Harris both made good runs, the latter hav- ing hard lines in not getting clear away with an inter- cepted pass. The Warrington forwards dashed along from a peaalty and registered a dead ball. Wigan were kept busy defending and hereabouts had what they thought bard lines. Harris punted, and Taylor who was standing offside picked up and made a nice opening for Eden who romped over near touch. The try looked fia-. grantly offside, but a Wigan man having touched the ball in transit made Taylor's action quite legitimate. The point was allowed. but Jones failed to majorise. The Warrington backs now kept up a bombardment of the home line, their passing quite bewildering the Wiganers. Isherwood and Jones both nearly got in under the ;posts, but it was left to Harris to increase the lead. The speedy right-winger took the ball from Duckworth near centre and raced in splendidly despite Lewis's attempts to stop him. Jones's kick for goal was well- direc'ed but fell short. Half-time arrived with Warring- ton two tries ahead.</p>
        <p>Taylor resumed, and Duckworth was prominent with a clever run through the Wigan backs. Play settled at centre for a spell until Isherwood broke through and contributed a brilliant run half the length of the field. He might have gone on but chose to pass to Jones and the ball went wrong. Anderton responded for Wigan but was upset by Isherwood, who seemed to be playing the whole Wigan fifteen at this stage. Warrington slackened off for a time and play be- came slow until about three-quarter time. Wigan had defended slabbprnly, but they were no match for the not three movements which were started by the War- rington halves. The ball in each instance went to Wood who had a clear run in but he failed to take the oval every time, much to his own and Warrington's chagrin. He should easily have added three tries. The attack was still maintained and Harris dodged over at the corner but was ruled touch- in-goal. Time arrived with the score still reading: War- rington, 2 tries (6 points) ; Wigan, nil.</p>
        <p>AN EASY VICTORY FOR RUNCORN. MAKING AN EXHIBMON OF TYLDESLEY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last, Tyldeiley visited the Irwell-lane enclosute, where about 2,000 spectators had assembled to see them play. The day was beautifully fine and cool, but a nasty glare from the declining sun made catching unc -.dein and Tyldesley who faced it were sadly handicapped, during the first ball, though that by no means detracted from the credit due to Runcorn for the thrashing they administered to them. The fol- lowing composed the teams : Runoorn :—S Houghtoe. back; T Wilkinson, T Warder, J Butterworth, and Jones, three-quarters; J Jolley and J Faulkner, halves; 0 Hughes, Tomlinson, S Walker, H Farmer, Laegley, F ki ay ter, Lightfoot, and Darlington, forwards. Tyldesley :—Dickiason, full back ; Aldred, Eckersley, Malkin. and Holt, three-quarters ; Berry and Bates- man, halves ; Clewes. R Berry, Halliwell,Tinker,A.shton, Bankes, and Worthington, forwards. Tyldesley opened the game with a man short, Worth- ington not having put in an appearance. Warder had ea opening, bet failed to pass when one would be use- ful. Jolley gave to Warder and he to Butterworth who scored a beautiful try quite at the commencement. Farmer failed to convert from an easy position. Dickin- son saved shortly afterwards, and again an opening that Wilkinson had was lost. Jolley hazarded a drop which missed. Houghton received and indulged in a piece of good gallery play which carried the ball well into the Tyldesley territory. A. melee ensued and a lot of poor foot work resulted in a try being scored by Langley, who fell on the ball just over the line. Scrums took place in the Runcpra qtzartlr and Runeorn again gained ground. The Tyldesley half backs received from the heels of his forwards, but his kick was charged down Faulkner and Jolley followed up quickly. The latter vowed and passing to Walker, that player just scored on the line as Dickinson brought him down. The next interesting feature was an attempt at breaking through by Farmer. A five yards scrum resulted which Tyldesley cleared. Tomlinson was good. Wilkinson tried a drop at goal hut it was very much off. The Tyldesley alf was beseiged for a time. Jolley got his head hurt by one of the Tyldesley forwards falling on him. The ball was badly put into the scrums several times, and a penalty was awarded Runcorn. Farmer tried to drop but failed. Gayter was grassed but got up smiling. Dickinson saved with a huge kink. Aldred was then coespicuous with a good run on the top wing, but Houghton brought him up by receiving his kick and dogging grandly. Some keen tackling was next visible Jones was grandly Bailing in, but his pass which he re- ceived from Butterworth being a forward one, he, had to come back again. Runcorn were good all round. Warder, Faulkner, Wilkinson, and Jolley passed well. Butterworth tried to improve and got collared. Another !toed bit followed, and a " marydlous " back-handed pass by Butterworth was as well received by Faulkner. The effort came to nought. H. Farmer was brought to grass amidst roars of laughter. Tyldesley then slightly improved things. Warder got something into his eye, and Houghton, changing his position, had a game on his</p>
        <p>own as three-quarter. At this stage the " fun " was abut at its height. Runcorn heeled out to Faulkner well in the Runcorn half, he ma.4e splendid way, send- ing two of the Tyldestey forwards on a " Catherine Wheel" expedition; he passed to Wilkinson, and he on to Jones. and finally to Buterworth. The latter suc- ceeded, after a splendid run of passing, in scoring a superb try. Farmer converted easily from a go-d posi- tion. Tyldesley, it was now notice 1, had the 15en man on the team. some fun followed in the f idesley 25. Malkin cleared well. From a mark by Butterworth, Farmer kicked widely. Owing to the sun two of the Tyldesley men ran into one another and both were slightly injured. They appeared to be Maikin and Beery: Resuming, Runcorn pressed. Several scrimmages took place on the visitors 25 line. Holt mulled, Malkin did likewise, Farmer rushed up and weuld have scored but for falling. Whilst on his knees he tried cr wling over, but was prevented amidst general hilarity by half the '1 Tyldesley team falling on top of him. Dickineon touched down, but as soon as he got his drop out the interval ar- rived : Runcorn,2 goals,4 tries,l6 points ; fyldesley, nil. When Farmer opened the second hal, it was noticed that the sun was not nearly so troublesome. The trio Faulkner, Jones, and Butterworth, were again con- spicuous for a good passing run, but the latter was held at a critical moment, Warder in marking got slightly hurt. The tackling of the Runcorn forwards was keen and good. A lively time ensued in fyldesley quarters. Berry passed strongly to Aldred's inside partner and he on to Aldred. Before he could make away however, Jones had him collared. Faulkner then had a chance and marked. The position was an easy one and a kick at goal was expected to follow, but did not cerne off. Later Jolley dropped, the ball going wide. The Tyldesley de- fence was now playing much better. Warder tried to back-heel the ball, and put all his opponents off the scent. Another opening was given Warder but be tried too much himself, and his ti elfishness cost him a fall. then followed a smart piece of work by Houghton, Jolley, Faulkner, Butterworth, and Jones all having a hand. The cheering was deafening. This was then re- peated, and the crowd was kept in a merry humour by the perplexing 'exhibition the Runcorn backs gave them. Open play followed and Tyldesley looked dangerous but Jones and Houghton saved. Farmer next stood for the Tyldesley men to try and push him over. The crowd laughed and it was really very funny. Magnificent passing followed, the ball eventually reaching Jones, he made off with Butterworth in attendauce. Just on the line Dickinson, Aldred and another snuffed poor Jones out by completely conveying him into touch, not, how- ever, before he had parted with the oval to Jimmy who was soon over and well behind the posts before the backs recovered from their bewilderment. Farmer kicked a goal. This point was disputed and a charge down claimed but the touch judge decided in favour of the major point. Some roughness was promptly dealt with by the referee. Runcorn pressed. Faulkner was particularly good. Malkin was again slightly hurt. Some good footwork was put in by Faulkner, Walker and Tot/Anson which gained ground. Tyldesley replied with a good effort and Holt got an open- ing which Aldred improved. Jolley saved. Dickinion kicked strongly and Warder replied. From a piece of loose work Tyldesley got a chance,Aldred having a clear field. Houghton cut across to stop him. Aldred hesi- tated, tried to dodge and as a result was grassed in fine style by the veteran full back. Tyldesley pressed. Run- corn returned and Aldred and Jones had a race in which Aldred won and saved by kicking dead. The serum was raised by Tyldesley, and Malkin was given a chance hy Aldred, but was heavily tackled by Wilkinson. the game quieted down somewhat. In getting clear Butterworth appeareilto be tripped and was slightly injured. He however, resumed. Tyldesley now showed better form and Aldred put in another run but finished in touch. Runcorn returned to midfield and were on the point of getting dangerous again when time was called. The final score being: Runcorn 3 goale, 5 tries, 21 points ; Tyldesley nil.</p>
      </div>
