      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LANCASHIRE v. CUMBERLAND</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE v. CUMBERLAND</p>
        <p>Following the trial game at Salford on Wednesday. the team to represent Lancashire against Cumberland at Oldham on October 21 was chosen as follows :—R L Thomas (Oldham). full back; T Fletcher (Oldham), left centre ; r Williams (Salford), right centre ; S Williams (Oldham), right wing ; U Hadwea (Salford), left wing; J R Lawton (Oldham) and H Ern:4er (Roch• dale Hornets), half backs; P Tunney (Salford), P Tay- lor (Leigh), G Frater (Oldham), D Morrison Warring- ton), W Briers (St. Helens), J !Williams (Salford), J Harrison (Rochdale Hornets), and E Vigors (Swinton), forwards. Reserves :—D Smith (Salford), full back ; D Traynor (St. Helens),F Hampson (Swinton), T D Davies (Oldham), three-quarter backi ; D Davies (Swinton), half back ; G Aspey (Widnes), G Whitehead (Brnughton Rangers), J Telford (Oldham), and G Jackson (Lancas- ter), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWI4 AMMONS V. GOLBORNE RESERVE. —Played on the ground of the former on Saturday. Tee opening stages were mostly confined to midfield neither side elaireing any material advantage. A nice combined movement on the pare of the Albions forwards resulted in Dodge scoring their first goal from close quarters after 15 minutes play. This was all the scoring up to the intervals which arrived with the Albions leading by a solitary goal. On resuming the hornesters were soon hovering around the Golborne goal and were with diffi- culty repulsed. The visitors next had a turn at attack- ing and eventually a fine shot from the right wing struck the crossbar and rolled tetween the posts. En- couraged by this success Golborne again compel!eJ the homesters to act on the defensive. A foul against the visitors transferred play and the Albians second goal was soon afterwards rushed through after an exciting scrimmage in the goal-mouth. From now to the finish the hoosesters monopolised almost the whole of the attacking but owing to the splendid defensive tactics showed by the visitors they were only able to score orice more, the ball rebounding off Dodge through the goal. Final, Earlestown Albions 3. Golborne Reserve 1. EARLESTOWN V. DARWEN.—LANCASHIRE LEAGUE.— Darwen had a rare gate for their snatch with Earles- town, and the game was played under ideal conditions. Fopping was absent from the home side, Dunn taking his place, while Earlestown had two of their recruits, Wilkinson and Whitehouse. In the first half the home side were the superiors in every department, their half back line espeoally playing a splendid game. Earles- town were kept in their quart-rs,and Bell early-on made a brace of beautiful goals with shots which gave Chew no chance wkatever. It was very largely duo to the efforts of the visitors' capable custodian that the score was kept down, and that Earlestown were only two goals behind at the interval. Earlestown obtained their opening score within five minutes of the restart, the re- sult of a lovely shot by Flampson, and Cooper scored the third goal for the home side, the next goal not being made until near the end. when Earlestown obtained their second point from close quart,rs. Darwen won by three goals to two, but the score by no nvans represents the difference between the two teams. The home side ought to have had several additional goals, but were not sufficiently determined near goal, though many of the movements by the forwards were of a beautiful character. Collinson was the pick of the defence, al- though Dunn also played well. The halves were a smart trio, and Barber was very prominent, the right wing pair playing rather loosely at times. Howarth gave a capital display in centre. Chew was great in the Earlestown goal. The backs gave a steady disci ay. and Cottam was the pick of the halves, Whitehouse giving the best exhibition in the front line. Darwen have now won every engagement since the opening of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>L EiNCASHIRE ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>L EiNCASHIRE ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>WARIISGTON V. PRESCOT.—These teams met On Saturday at the Warrington cricket field, in fine weather, before a lar/e gate. Warrington were without theiecentre half-back Hackitt,who was injured at work, D. Jones filling the vacancy. Jack Mather, a popular local full-back, who has played for Earlestown and Middleton, made his first appearance for Warrington. Caslin kicked off for Prescot and Green and Henry tolk pay to the Warrington goal, Mather and Dpwnie being called upon. Passing between Sergeant Jones and Balm, Murray and Connor restored the lost ground, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>ENGLAND v. SCOTLAND.</p>
        <p>The tenth meeting was the principal attraction at the Bon Accord Club competitions, Corporation Baths,Aber- deen, on Saturday evening: The event caused consider- able interest in the city. the Council of which enter- tained the teams and officials daring their stay. The teams were :--Scotland : Craig (Pollockshields), Mattis (Glasgow), Cornett (Inverness), Stirling (Glas- gow), Taggart (Glasgow). Russell (Glasgow), and Hamilton (Pollockshields). England : Robertson (Warrington), Marshall (London), Gray (Landon), Sharrock (St. Helens), Ripley (St. Helens), Derbyshire (Manchester), and Jarvis (Leicester). The baths were crowded. England began by playing to the shallow' end, and the first to reach the ball was Derbyshire in * race against Russell. Scotland tried long shots, and were on every occasion short. The English forwarde were strong on a rush up, but a foul against thent stopped the advance. Sharrock was playing strongly, and scored two goals. Half-time: England, two; Scotland, none. In the second halt the first point was taken by Stirling for Scotland. Jarvis followed, and Derbyshire shot two goals. Result : England, five; Scotland, one. Scotland's men wen too daring with their shots, and threw away several chances.</p>
