      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>BAND OF HOPE UNION.</head>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>UNITED RALLY IN PARR, lIA.LL.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Band of Hope Union recommenced their united rallies in Parr hail verb au•piciously last Thur!!day evening. The building Was crowded, and the children were greatly inter- ested in the proceedings. The united choir under the conductorship of Mr. Walter Brough was accommodated on the platform. The singing was exceptionally good,the several choruses being given with precision and feeling. The opening chorus "Now pray we for our Country" was a most appro- priate one and was greatly appreciated by all present. 1</p>
        <p>- After prayer had been offered The CHAIRMSN (Mr. R A Naylor), in the Course Of a few introductory remarks said that was the first rally of the winter season of the Warrington Band of Hope Union and he thought he was right in saying that duty had that night the largest audience that they had had on any eccasion since that Band of Hope Union was in- stituted. (Applause.) They were still in the possession of a charming choir and a very worthy conductor and if they heard no more than the one song to which they had just listened they had not come there in vain. (Hear, hear.) The song was entitled "Now pray we for our country ' and it must have touched the hearts of everyone. They were really proud to belong to such a great empire. the greatest the world had ever leen, and it was well that they should pray for their country. With those sentiments operat- ing in their souls a feeling of patriotism, everyone of them would be ready to do what they ceuld to drive away anything that was detrimental to the true interests of this mighty nation. The boys and girls were assembled there that evening in their bun dreds. They had a few ladies and gentlemen present, but they did not think about them. (Laughter.) It was the children tney wanted and they were met together to try to strike a blow at an enemy which was worse than war.woree than pestilence, worse than famine, though those were dreadful things. Peihaps war, famine and pestilence combined could not do more against the true interests of their country than strong drink was doing. (Hear. hear.) Therefore they were banded together in one bond of fellowship determined, come what would, to remain true and firtn.and do what true patriots ought to do for the glory of God and the good of their greae, country. (Applause.) Miss Naylor wss afterwards called upon to distribute prizes in cannection with the competition examination "Why abstain." Mr. E. LLOYD JONES said perhaps it was advisable that he should tell them 0: the circumstances which had led to the presentation of the priz,s. A sh..rt while ago a competition was held in connection with the Lanes- shire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union, and eighteen members of the Warrington Band of Elope Union eat for examination. Eleven were successful and the certifi- cates and prizes of the county union were distributed to the successful competitors at the open-sir rally held at Sankey Bridges. The prizes which were now to be pre- sented were given by the Warrington Union to the same competitors. (Applause.) The prize winners were as follows :-13t pens, Mr Harold Gray (W.M. Mission, Bank Quay), Mise Kitty C Whitfield (Wycliffe), Master Tnomas W Wright (thick- street Mi58101}); 2nd prizes,Mr A P Hoult (W.M. Mlssion, Bank Quay), Master Roby T Jones (Stockton Heath United), Miss Lucy Evans (Wycliffe) ; 3rd prizes, Mr Charles Morrall (Wycliffe), Miss Lucy Travis (Wycliffe), Master W S Whitfiel i (Wycliffe); workers division, Mr F Morrall (Wycliffe). Mr A J Pemberton (W.M. Mission). A feature of the evening's programme was the singing of Miss Esther Williams. Her first contribution was "Songs the children sing" (Moir), which was given in ,a manner that greatly pleased the audience. An encore was demanded and complied with. Miss Williams was qually successful in herinterpretation of" Daddy " and pa response to the repeated applause of the audience she gave as an encore " Ora Pro Nobis." Miss Amy Kinsey recited "The hand of Providence." The choir also efficiently sang several other choruses including " Dashing on before the gale."'• Wake ! Wake ! Wake!' and "Home, S vsee Home." Mr. Wilkes ably discharged the duties of accompanist.</p>
        <p>The major portion of the second half of the programme was devoted to a lecture to the children by Madame Care Goslett on " Habit's that make the battle cf life hard." It was illustrated by lantern views which were Ethown Igy Mr H Taylor, Police Court Missionary. She clearly demonstrated in a simple way the mischief caused by alcohol, and characterised the assertions put forward in its favour as untruths nhe showed that instead of alcohol strengthen ing the muscles it weakened them ; that instead of giving clearer vision it did the exact opposite as was shown in the results of shooting competitions; and that instead of" keepir.g out the cold" it let in the cold and this was proved by Arctic explorers. Pictures of the brain and tbe heart were thrown on the screen, and the ill effect of alcohol on these organs was clearly shewn. Alcohol killed its victims, and its power to kiln was evidenced by the abnormal death rate amongst publichouse and hotel keepers. The services of all those who bad taken part in the vening's entertainment were acknowledged on the pro ition of Mr. E LLOYD JONES, and a pleasant eve ning eluded with the singing of the Doxology.</p>
        <p>FATAL SCAFFOLD ACCIDENT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>COMPLIMENT TO THE nousz SURGEONS</head>
        <p>A COMPLIMENT TO THE nousz SURGEONS</p>
        <p>AND NURSES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse conducted an inquiry in the Parade Room at the Police 6tation,Warrington, last Thurs- day morning, respecting the death of Frederick Joseph Cooper, a plumber, 34 years of age, of Allen- street.—Mr. Joseph liedgcock was foreman of the jury. Mr. Browne represented the relatives of the deceased, and Mr. Tullock, of Manchester, appeared for Mr. William %%instanlev, the contractor for the plumbing work. Photographs of the scaffolding were placed in the hands of the Coroner and jury. Henry James Cooper, brother of the deceased, said the deceased was 34 years of ago last birthday. He bad been in the employ ot Mr. Winstanley five weeks only, but he was an experienced plumber. The accident happened on the Thursday, and wit- ness heard about it the same day. He saw his brother several times at the Infirmary after the accident. He (witness) wished to express his grati- tude to the doctors and nurses for the great kind- ness and attention shown to his brother.</p>
        <p>- The Coroner: 1 can testify to their kindness. - -</p>
        <p>Witness, continuing, said his brother never made a s'atement to him about the accident as he never sufficiently recovered consciousne.n to do so. He was once conscious, but he made no remark about the accident.—By Mr Tullock:The deceased had been engaged on similar work before the accident.—He had worked for some of the best firms in London. Edwin Howkins said he had been in the service of Mr Winstanley for about eight months, and he was on the scaffolding when it gave way. Be did not fall, as he happened to catch hold of the ladder, but the deceased fell to the ground. The scaffolding had been erected about tix days previously by eceased, witnesq, and another man named Moss. Four of them were on it at the time it fell. Witness had not the blightest buspicion that it was dangerous. It was examined each day. There was no leading hand in its erection ; all had the same responsibility in it. They were all eati4ied with the manner in which it had been put up. A very strong wind blew in the early part of the week.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: There was no leading nand ; they divided the responsibility between them, and the men were on the scaffolding at the time of the ac- cident. Toe responsibility was evenly divided. Mr. Erowne said le was not going to attempt to show any criminal liability. He believed it was a pure accident. Miss Wiliiams.one of the nurses at the Infirmary, said the deceased was admitted to the ingtitution last Thurgday suffering from a fracture of the base of th(, skull, and he died on the following Tuesday morning at 11 30. After a short private consultation the jury re- turned a verdict of " Accidental death," but sug- gested that in the future some other method for fixing the scaffold should be adopted. Mr. Tullock : There is no blame to Mr Winstan- ley. The Coroner: Quite to. The jury don't blame they only suggest. Mr Browne said the relatives did not actachblarn to anyone.</p>
      </div>
