        <p>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE BRIGADE, WARRING- TON. THE ROYAL ,MIILSH LADIES CHOIR WILL GIVE A</p>
        <p>GRAND CONCERT AT THE PARR WILL, WARRINGTON, ON MONDAY, OCTOBER 16TH, IN AID OF THE WARRINGTON CORPS, ST. JOHN AMBULANCE BRIGADE. This is their First Appearan'e in Warringtan. THIS ROYAL WELSH I:ADM,' CHOIR is under the SPECIAL PATRONAGE of the QUEEN They :,re the Win- ners of the First Prize at the World's Fair, Chicago, and have had the honour of appearing before Her Majesty the Queen and Royal Family; of the Patti Concerts. Royal Albert Hall; London Ballad 0 'ncerts; Harrison Concerts;and also the honour of appearing before Presi.tent sod Mr:. McKinley ; and at the Principal American Oon-_•erts. CONDUCTRESS: MADAME CLARA. NOVELL() DAVIES. PIANIST AND ACCOMPANIST MISS MARIA WILLIAMS. (Gold Medalist National Eisteddfod, 1393 and 1899), ASSISO,D BY R. MALYS DAVIS ,E.C.1.1 the Renowned Baritone MIS BACKSHEEN ,VOOD, Solo Violinist. Reserved Chairs (Ratty of Hall and Cantre Gallery), 3, Side 0 slleries and Second Seats (Body of Hall) Reserved, 21 Other Seats, is. Money will not b taken at the doors till 7.31 : hot Ticket- bolders-will be admi(ted at least 15 minutes earlier. Tickets may be had from Members of the St. John Ambalanc- Corps and others. Written applications (accompanied by remittance) will be promptly attended to, and Tickets for the best unappro- priated boats secured and forwarded. Plan of Hall may be seen and Seats booked at Mr. 11. H. Pearson's Bold-street, Warrington. AN BEARD GRAND PIANO WILL BE USED.</p>
        <p>keittrs to Me Mitor. NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intendea fur publication must in all cases send the,.r came, dad addresses to the alitor. Matters intended or pubtion must be written on one side only ot the piper. Correspondents ere s_rougly Advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>MR. J. L. TUNS PAL L AND THE ASSISTANT OV tuitSE EALSI:I IP, ,</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I be permittel to trespets Upon your space in order to protest against certain statements which are reported to have been made at tee meeting of the Town Ceuecd held on Tuesday lest, waen the transfer of the Assistant Overseership was under discussion ? Mr. Councillor Menks is reported to have said :—"But taking over the stall has given more trouble than all else. With regard to Mr. Smith, he has himself to blame. For a long time tie has been a stumbling block, and he said he would only come on such and such con- ditions, which the Finance Committee could not agree to for one moment ; his conditions here quite sufficient to prevent any further consideration of his claim." I hope and believe this statement is based upon a mis apprehension, for it is certainly contrary to fact and calculated seriously to prejudice Mr. Smith who, indeed, has never had a chance of formulating any conditions. The true faces of the case are as follow : I was approached by the Borough Treasurer on behalf of the Corporation, and asked to state on what terms I would he prepared to surrender my appointments, and I was at the same time assured that certain clauses, which had already been inserted in the Bill of the Cor- poration which had then been, or was about to be intro- duced into Parliameet, were Si inserted only with the view of an amicable arrangement with me, and that the clauses would be withdrawn if terms could not be arranged in a trienuiy way. On this understanding, I named my terms, wnien Included a proviso that the Corporation should take over my staff on certain con- ditions. A sub-committee of the Finance Committee who had the conduct of the matter, ,finally rejected my effer, and informed me that they had nothing turther to say to me, and not ouiy were the clauses retained, con- 4rary to the assurance given me, but the insertion of a clause for my protection was refused, and further clauses affecting me prejudicially were added. I petitioned against the But, out finally had to give way, ' the best terms I could (Again for my staff tieing en ,gree- ment by the Ceporation to take them ever and retain them until the 25th March next.</p>
        <p>At the same meeting, Mr. Councillor Green is re= ported to have said" they knew nothiog whatever of the staff of the clerks emp'oyed by Mr. Tunstall. They were his private servants, and as such the Corporation would be in a positkn to nuke overtures to such of them as tht,y found it necessary to take into their ser- vice. If they liked to accept their overtures they would Came into roe service of the Corporation, and those it was thought would not be serviceable to them, would have to look out for appointments elsewhere." It passes my comprehension how Mr. Green could publicly make this statement, for he was the Chairman of the Com- mittee which agreed with me to take over my staff, and they are now actually in the employ of the Corp Oration. If any of them are dismissed, the onus will have to fall in the proper quarter, and not upon ma. The suggestion that I ought to compensate the staff, constitutes a miserable attempt to shirk the discredit of a shabby transaction. Mr. Green is further reported as follows :—"They could not that night make any suggestions as to the staff to be taken over, the salaries to be paid, and what additional remuneration should be given to Mr. Woodcock...... They expected to make considerable savings in the management." Why, I should like to know, should I, after having been de- prived of office against my will, for the benefit of the Corporation, be asked to find the means to enable the Corporation to make a further saving, 'and at the same time to advance the salary of one of its officers ? It may be taken as a fact,that the boasted savings to be made by the concentration of duties will be trifling com- pared to the entire work of the staff,aod do. not warrant the dbmissal of its youngest member. Such savings i's are brought about will:be effected chiefly by the dismissal of tried officers, and the performance of their duties by others who havo had little or no experience of the work, and as regards the registration of voters, an exceed ingly intricate matter, the risk of inefficiency will be serious. If, however, savings are to be effected by such means. I would suggest that the opportunities are not yet exhausted. Why not, for instance, let the Town Clerkship by tender, and refill the Boroug h Treasurer- ehip P—l am, sir, your obedient servant, J. L. TUNSTALL. Warrington, 6th October. 1899.</p>
        <p>WARRTNOTON VOLUNTEERS AT ALTCAR.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PRIZE MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the prize meeting of the Ist V.B. South Lancashire Regiment was held at Altcar. The officers present were Lieutenant-Colonel J C Ridgway, in com- mand ; M4jor and Adjutant F Roome, Major Buckton, Captains J H Ridgway, B Fairclough. D Fairclough, F M Appleton, Quartermaster Potter, Lieutonants E G Medley, C P Gough, and Cl' R Crosfield. Several teams of sixtlen men from each company competed for the Battalion C'aallenge Shield and £lB, but after four and a half sections had gone through the long-range volley and field firing, the light became so bad that the com- petition had to be postponed. Result 3 of competitions concluded : NON-COMMISSIONED OFFICRRS. 200 500 To-</p>
