      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CHRIST CHURCH, LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>CHRIST CHURCH, LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Tho following is takon from "Christ, Church Parochial Magazine" :—" it i 3 now a matter known to all that Mr. Prescott, woo faithfully and zealously worked hare for 18 years, has resigned he living of Christ Church. The severance of so long a connection cannot but be attended with regret to many with whom Mr. Prescott was deservedly popular, tie was assisted for 16 years by his brother. the late Mr. James Prescott, wl,o during that time gave his services willinwly and voluntarily to the parish. Toe circumstances which resulted in both leaving the parish where they had laboured so long were such. as to call forth very considerable sympathy. Mr. Prescott has, however, deemed it advisable not to return, and the Patron has offered the benefice to Mr. Screeton,who bas been in charge since June, 1897. After careful consideration Mr. Screeton decided to accept an offer which he knew was made partly in deference to what the Rector of Grappenhall believed to be the wishes of the con- gregation. It is not to be expected that any appointment will be universally popular, but we hope that all will work well and loyally together for the sake of God and His Church, and the new Vicar ventures with confidence to ask for the prayers of the congregation and for that charitable consideration, sympathy and encouragement, with- out which a clergyman's work is impossible, even heart-breaking."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13. 1899. EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. I NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN. I WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICi COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 13. 1899. EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. I NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN. I WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICi COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>LONDON MISSIONARY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At the Congregational Chapel Newton,on Sunday, special services were held in connection with the London Mia-ionary Society. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev II Wilson of Ashton, and in the afternoon an address was given by Mrs. Marriott, from Samoa. In the evening the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. Mr Clarke of St. Helens. Collections were made at eaca service in aid of the funds.</p>
        <p>[By A CORILBSPONDBNT.] FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Earlestown still linger at the bottom ef the Lan- cashire League list, and unless the unexpected hap- pens, there they will remain for some little time longer. It was never expected that they would be able to take two points out of Darwen from home, when they were beaten by a goal by Darwen on the home ground. Still, had the Earlestown back play been anything at all what it should have been, it is quite on the cards that the match would have ended in a draw. Wilkinson was the defaulter and I think the committee will be well advised to drop him, that is, if they have a better an to replace him. His kicking on Saturday was feeble in the extreme. and he lacks that dash which is s) neces- sary for League form.</p>
        <p>Darwen only scored two goals in a legitimate manner by their own players. Three however were placed to their credit, two being obtained within a few minutes of the opening of the game. The first was due to a miskick by Wilkinsan, and Hayes rushing across with his accustomed dash, kicked strongly to clear the danger, but unfortunately the ball struck Cottam in theback and re bounded off him into the net. The second goal was also due to Wil- kinson's faulty play, and it is lucky for the team that Chew was in form between the sticks. The second half of the game was mostly in favour of Harlestown and Darwen had their work cut out ta prevent the visitors scoring.</p>
        <p>The meeting on Saturday is with Blackpool on the latter's ground, and the following will represent Earlestown :—Goal, Chew; backs, Hayes and Wil- kinson ; half-backs; Green, Parr and Johnson, for- wards, Whitehouse, Hampson, Harrison Leach and Settle.</p>
        <p>Newton added a couple of points in the Liver- pool and District Combination on Satarday, easily defeating Hudson's by 2 goals to 1, There was more combination among the forwards on Saturday, and doubtless this had something to do with the victory.</p>
        <p>The Shamrocks surprised St. Helens Rees Re- serves in their Sr. Helens and District league en- counter, the glassmen being vanquished by 3 goals to 1.</p>
        <p>The Rovers were also victorious over Frodsham in the Warrington, and District league by 2 goals to 1. 1 am afraid if the Revers wish to pose as a respect- able team,they will have to cultivate their manners a little, as I am inclined to think some of the Frodsham players will remember their visit to Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The Albions are bent on making a name for themelves. They hold an unbeaten record and on Saturday they ran round Golborne Reserves to the tune of 3 goals to 1.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire League table up to date is as follows : Goals.</p>
        <p>Played. Won. Lost. Drawn. For. Agst. Pts. Stockport County 5 ... 4 ... 0 ... 1 ... 16 ... 3 ... 9 Crewe Alexandra 6 ... 4 ... 1 ... 1 ... 15 ... 10 ... 9 Wigan County 5 .. 2 ... 0 ... 3 ... 6 ... 2 ... 7 Darwen</p>
        <p>Blackp00l Stalybridge Rovers— 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 7 ... 5 ... 6 Haydoek 4 2 1 1 5 4 5 Chorlev Middleton Southport Ceutral South Share White Star Wan'd Rochdale 4 1 3 0 4 15 .. 2 I-forwich Raciest() wn</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in Bewsey Cham- bers, Warrington. on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. Joshua Ball pm.ide], and there were alas) pre3eot :—Mesirs Forshaw, A. Brooks, P. Longton. William Houghton, J. W. Knowl3s, Thomas Haltcn, with J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), William Thompson (sur- veyor), Dr. Sephton (medical officer), and R. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>LIAYBOCK PRIMITIVE METHODISTS.</head>
        <p>LIAYBOCK PRIMITIVE METHODISTS.</p>
        <p>In connection with the harvest thanksgiving ser- vices held in the above chapel, a concert was given in the schoolroom. Haydock, on Monday evening, by a number of friends from Earlestown. Songs. recitations, and mandoline solos were rendered. the artistes including Miss Stronach, Messrs A. Banton, J. Baxter and R. A Robinson, of EArlestown ; Miss Dearden, the Misses Pilling, and Ar. A. Laites, of Haydock. Miss Toombs very efficiently accompanied. Daring the evening a nistribution of fruit took place. Poucit CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Saturday morning, before Mr J W Emmett, Andrew Murray and John Walters, were charged with being drunk and disorderly, and were each fined 2s 6d and costs. —At the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday morning before Mr T 3 Gillespie, Michael Mannion, was charged with being drunk on Sunday night, and was fined 5s and costs. P.C.'s Lenny and Benson supported the charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening there was a fairly large audience assembled in the Lecture Hail of the Wesleyan Chapel, on the occasion of an elocution entertainment by Mr. a P. Venn, in aid of a fund for the purchase of hymn books for the Sunday School. Tne Rev. P. R. Passmore presided,and the recitals of Mr. Venn, which were cleverly given,in- cludeu selections from the writings of G. R. Simms, Longfellow, Mark Twain, Burns and others. Inter- spersed with the pieces, several vocal items were contributed by Miss 0. Clare, Messrs. Webster, and Twemlow, and a piano duet by Mrs. Mather and Miss Chandley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT P.S.A.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT P.S.A.</p>
        <p>A very successful meeting of the above society was held in the Co-operatiye Hall, Earle-street, on Sunday afternoon last, the hall being well filled. Mr T Williamson presided. The following pro- gramme, (Mr Knight being responsible). was car- ried out in an excellent manner, the abilities shewn both vocal and instrumental being of a high order— Opening hymn and Lords prayer; quartette," God is a spirit," quartette party; solo, " The promise of the King," Miss Corlett ; pianoforte duet. Miss Williamson and Mr Knight; solo, " The Raft," Mr Hitchen ; duet," 0 Lovely Peace." Misses Corlett and Mather ; violin solo, Miss Farrimond ; solo, 1 Abide with me," Miss Mather; quartette, "Ora pro Nobis," quartette party; solo," When I survey the Wondrous Cross," Miss Corlett; solo, " Honour and Arms," Mr Hitchan ; violin solo, Miss Farrinond ; solo, "God shall wipe away all t3ars," Miss Itlather; pianoforte duet, Miss Williamson and Mr Knight. In the evening a service of song entitled " No sur- render," was given by the P,S.A. choir, the connec- tive readings being given by Mr T Williamson.</p>
        <p>THE DISAPPEARANCE OF AN OLD WOMAN FROM</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that last week we pub- lished the disappearance of a woman named Mary Darbyshire, 70 years of age, from Lumbar-lane, Burtonwood. No tidings could be gained of her whereabouts, although the canal was frequenty searched, until Saturday last when her body was found in the Sankey Brook. An inquest was held at the Chapel House Inn, Burtonwood, on Tuesday, by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner.—Hannah Darbyshire, a daughter, stated that the deceased had been of rather weak intellect during the past six weeks, always imazining she was away from home. About half-past nine on the night of the Ist inst she went out, but was brought back to the house. About 10 o'clock, how- ever, she made her way out again unnoticed. On witness becoming aware of her absence, she and her brothers immeiiately went in search of her, remaining out all night. The night was rough and very dark. Witness heard nothing ot her until she was brought home on Saturday afternoon last, dead, having been found lying in the Sankey Brook, about a mile distant from her home.—A verdict of "Found drowned," was re- turned, no evidence being adduced as to how de- ceased got into the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The members of this association opened their winter session on Monday by a meeting in the Co- operative Hall, when there was a very good attend- ance. Mrs. Rawlins presided, and she was sup- ported by Mrs. Miller,Miss Allatt,and Miss Greenall. Miss Crawford was introduced as secretary. Mrs. Rawlins in her opening address gave a resume of the Women's Council Meetings in London, remark- ing that Dr. Spence Watson had, after many years, come over to the side of the women on the franchise question. She referred to various matters which were dealt with at the Council Meetings, amongst which was the half-timers question, upon which she was sorry to find the member for the Newton Division had voted against the increase in the age limit. On temperance matters, she said, Sweden, Norway, and Russia, would not serve drink to young children, yet England was very much behind these countries in that respect. She made reference to the Peace Conference at the Hague and said she believed in the Czar's honesty of purpose when be suggested the conference. She spoke at some length on the Transvaal question, and in order to make her remarks more lucid, snowed by means of maps the various districts connected with the dispute, and expressed her horror at the prospect of a war.—Miss Allatt moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Rawlins and, congratulated her upon her restoration to health, and said they were all glad to welcome her amongst them once again, hoping they would have a very good session. Mrs. Miller seconded, and the vote was heartily accorded.—Mrs. Rawlins briefly acknowledged the vote and said she expected that %Liss McLaren, Ramsey would give a lecture sometime in Novem- ber. Afternoon tea was afterwards partaken of, the opening meeting being voted entirely successful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr. O'Keefe, who is very well-known in the district of Parr, St. Helens. en Tuesday nieht consented to become the Liberal c‘ndidate for Parr Ward,to oppose Councillor R. Ryder (Conservative).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>SUPPOSED MAD DOG AT ST. HELENS .</head>
        <p>SUPPOSED MAD DOG AT ST. HELENS .</p>
        <p>The St. Helene Ha4ltll Committee have received a re- port from Professor Rose, Bradford Animal Institete, London, in respect to the dog belonging Co Mr. J. H. Nickson, which was killed at St. Helens early in August last, after havicer! bitten a man named John Thomas Reed, of flaydock. Mr. Dixon, the veterinary surgeon, b-li..ving the dog to be mai, sent tin dog's briin to Lon- don for ,!xamiriatior., and the prefeianr's 'won states that the dog was not uffering from rabies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TECHNICAL EDUCATION IN ST. HELENS</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL EDUCATION IN ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>The S. Helens Town Hall was on Wednesday night the scene of a large gathering, on the occasion of the annual distribution of prizes and certificates gained by the students attendine the clazises and evening schools arranged by the St. Flelns Technical Committee. The procF.elings attracted more than usual attention on ac- count of the ceremny of unveiling the bust of Sir David Gamble, But., C. 8., which the Corporation have had executed by ;qr. Onslow Ford, R.A., in recognition of Sir David's princely generosity in presenting to the t.mn the rnaignificent Technical S-hoot and Free Library known as the Gambie liistittre Tne institute ranks among the very best and mos: complete technical s Mnols of the country,and the total cost of building and equipp- ing it amounteu to ciose on £30,000. In addition,Sir David has for very many years taken a foremoit part in the management of public affaire, and has earned the warm.ist revcc: and aff c`ion ot the wholi-.1 corn.nunity by his many ec:s of generosity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CRICKET CLUB</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CRICKET CLUB</p>
        <p>A general meeting of this club was n4d on Mon- day evening at the li-mdrick-street schoolroom, the Vicar (the Rev. E. N. Tarbuck) presiding. The meeting was callad for the puraose of reviewing the past season's work, and considering the state of the Club's affaira in general. The Hon. Treasurer (Rev. R. A. Lester) submitted the balance sheet, which showed a small balance on the right side, The Hon. Secretary (Mr. Leach) in presenting his report,said that considering they had started rather late in the season, thereby having a difficulty in getting suitable finres, they had had a fairly suc- cessful time, both first and second teams. It was, decided to continue the club another year, and, so far as can be judged, a very prosperous season may be anticipated. Votes of thanks to the officers of the club for their services,and to the Chairman foi- owed by an interesting and instructive speech by hat rev, gentleman, concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>HURSDAT.—Before Messrs. T. J. Gillespi,i (in the chair), J Jackson. C. B. F. B ,rron, ant R. Sole</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—The following were fined for drunkenness: —Charles Ratcliffe, 28 6d and costs ; Robert Wilson, 2s 6d and costs ; James Ashen, be and costs ; John Gregory, 2s 61 and costs Thomas Duffey, 5s and costs ; Patrick Carney. 2s 6d and costs. °Lan( Vali WAGES—Three Irish harvesters named John Cain, James Lowe, and Peter Co iy, claimed 18s lid from Rdwari Gleave, farmer, 3:1 days wages due to them. —lt was stated that co nplainants worked for the defendant, wh s paid them 153 a week, and said he would give them 103 at the close of the harvest. They wanted him to put them ore piecework but he refused and they left work on Thursday morning. They did not reoeive 3 days wages due to them of the 10s h ervast, money.—Defendant said that he intended tp pay the men lOs at the close of the potato harvest. It had always been his practice to do so. It was also understood that a week's notice on either side should terminate the agreement. Compleinants did not give notice.—Complain- ants said that a week's notice was not required. --The mag- istrates decided that defendant should pay the 83 lid wages, but did not think the men were entitled to the lOs harvest money. unless they worked until the close of the potato harvest.</p>
        <p>DRUNK ON LICEN3ED PREWI3ES.—N3tIvaI Byrom WAS fined 28 6d auct costa for being drunk on th lloensi premises in the occupation of John Travis, Haydock, on the Z7th Sep- tember.</p>
        <p>BREACH OF THE PEACE —John Sankey and Peter ltladdi- son were summoned for committing a breach of the peace by fighting at Havdock on the 2nd October.—They were bound over to keep the peace for six months in the sum of £5.</p>
        <p>FAILING TO DIiSuEP. -Esmond Stabbs was summoned for having on the 16th inst caused to be moved from Crewe to Haydook 96 sheep and act;ng In controvention to Regula- tion 1 of the Diseases of Animals Aetaad Sheep Scab Order by failing to dip them twee within 14 days from the removal:— P. 6. Walker said that at noon on the sth inst be visitel the defendant's farm at Haydock. He found in a field 86 sheep and in a shippon 10 sheep. He asked the defendant how long he had hal them on his premises and he said from the 14th of September. He said he had not had them dipped as he was not aware that he should do so Witness found in another building the carease of a dead sheep. He called again the following morning and saw another carcase of a sheep. Most of the sheep were suff _ring from foot-rot. He made inquiries at the railway statioae and found that defendant received 93 sheep altogether. Copies of the order were posted near the farm.—The Clerk said tbe defendant wa,iiaie to a penalty of £Bo.—Superintendent Brassing- ton said that was the first case of the kind that had been brought into that Court- The order only came into force in July.—The Chairman said that as that was the first case that had been brought before the Bench they thought it was very possible that the defendant was not aware of the order. They were disposed to treat the case leniently although they could fine him between £430 and £5OO. They would let him off 01 payment of 10e.ineludin4 cost.a. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>FACTORY GIRLS' DISPUTE.—John Littler was charged with assaulting Mary Ann Horrigan. at Golborne on the 3rd inst. —Complainant stated that on the date In question she and some other girls were c 'ming home from work, when they met some girls from another mill who commenced to quarrel with them. Defendant jumped from behind the hedge and hit her in the lace and kicked ker.—Evidence In support of this statement having been given, Kate Horrigan was eharged with assaulting Edith Speakman on the 2nd October aid Mary Ann Horrigan was charged with assaulting Mary Ellen Speakman.—Complainants gave evidence to the offset that they were coming home from work, when defendants who worked at another mill met them. They had some words together and it:ended in defendant's hitting them on the head with their cans.—Suaerlatendent Brassington said that two policemen had had to be put on duty at the Om where the disturbance oe iurred, owing to the conduct of girls who worked at Golborne and Ashton. The girls had now commenced bringing men with them to help them to tight.— The magistrates decided tu bind both parties over to keep the peace for na months in the sum of £5 each. TERPTIOF:A. SPADE AND MACKINTOSH—WM(BM Carman was charged with stealing from Princes coal pit, Haydock, on September 22nd. a coal spade value is 10d, the property of Edward Waring ; and also with stealing a mackintoso value 2s 6,1, the property of Thomas Jones.—Bdward Waring, a collier, residing at 165. Cross-lane. Barlestown. said that he worked at Princes pit. Havdock. On the 22nd Septemher he used the spade, and when ne had done with it he left it in a coal box. He did not see it again until Thursday last when it was in the hands of the police.—P.C. midterm said that on the 14th last, he went to ths house of Rachael Jones, Athe'-stre t, and the spade was handed to him. He charged prisoner with the theft, and he made no reply.— Prisoner said that another man told him that he could have the spade or he world not have taken it.—The case in which the prisoner was charged with stealing a mackintosh was then proceetied with—Thomas Jones, collier, 65, Athol-street, Bariestown, said the mackintosh produced was his property. He saw it safe in the house on the 29th September. and missed it on he f4liowin4 Saturday. Prisoner, who lolzed at his house disappeared on Saturday and ho did not see him again Mil he was in cast° iy. —Prisoner said he did take the mackintosh, but did not in'en,l to steel it It was raining when he went ont, so he put it on.—The magistrates com- mitted him to gaol for a month.</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL EVIIIITTION AT ST. JAMES'S HALL, AIANCEIESTER.</p>
        <p>The latest exhibit'oa arranged bv Mr. Albert P. , Baker, is being held in St. James's Hall, Oxford-street, Manchester. Fourteen exhibitions, represeutalive of a van tv of trades, have been held from tim t tima dur.n! the last five years, and the ia the third devo".ed to British and Glonial industries. Many prominent busine,,,s firms in various parts of the country have contributed to toe present collection of articles, which include, in addition to food- stuff', condiments, and sweetmeats, the latest specimens of gas stoves and ceokers, kitchen ranges, dravviug-room firtgrates and overmantels, pianos and other musical instruments, building materials, and elec- trical and other appliances. l'he Manchester Liners Limited have on exhibition beautiful models of veseels built to their special order, and there is an interesting section devoted to samples of honey. Prizes have been offered for excellence of exhibits in seven classes corn- in this department, the Lancashire Beekeepers' Association having the competitions under their care. The music given at the exhibitions is a great attract on to visitors, the sorvices of some of the finest brass bands in the country being invariably secured- The present ex- hibition will remain open till October 21st,and this week the inosic, afternoon and evening, has been provided by he Itlyal Artillery (Mounted) Baud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPHTON presented a report on the health of the district ter tho past two months. For the month ending the 11th inst. , the 'Viatica! Officer stated that there were 18 births, 11 males and 7 females, and 6 deaths, 4 males and 2 f emales. The excess of births over deaths was 12. The death-rate from all cases was 7 per thousand against 15 for the previous three months, and 21-7 in the corresponding period of last year. The zymotic death-rate was 1 per thousand against 4 in the corresponding month of last year. Two cases of infec- tious diseases had been reported, one of diptheria and one of typhoid.</p>
        <p>VISITATION OF DELEGATES TO THE HEALTH CDNORESS. Tne ASSISTANT CLERIC (Mr. S. Sutton) reported that in company with Mr. Knowles he attended the Congress of the Royal Institute of Public Health at Blackpool. He presented a brief report on the subject. Mr. J. W. KNOWLES also added a few remarks upon the recent Congress. Referring to a paper on cremation, be said the delegates seemed to think that in the near future they would all have to be burned instead of being buried. A resolution was passed that nuisance inspec- tors in rural districts ought to pass an examination to qualify them for the position as was already the case in towns. Some very stringent measures were advocated with reference to the treatment of tuberculosis, but he and others voted against tbe resolution which he was glad to say was lost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TRE INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>TRE INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. KERFOOT in his report stated that the Medical Officer of Health had reported to him a case of diptheria at Woolston. He had examined the premises but failed to find anything to account for the disease, although in his opinion, they wore not in a good sanitary condition. There was also a case of typhoid fever reported from Penketh but these premises were in a good sanitary con- dition. He had to report two farm houses as being with- out a pr, per supply of wholesome water sufficient for the use of the occupiers, one in the township of Great Sankey and the other in the township of Burtonwood. In both cases he thought a supply could ba obtained by boring and sinking deeper. The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE SURVEYOR'S REPORT.—THE WORKMEN'S HOURS. Mr. THOMPSON in his report stated that with regard to the question of weekly payment of wages, he had en- quire from the surveyors of two largo rural districts, and found that they paid fortnightly,but inideferenne to Mr. Halton's vEolution and the rEquest of the Council he had considered the proposal, and now had pleasur, in submitting the following sehems :—That the Council's workmen be paid weekly, tha week to end on the Thurs• day at 530 p in., the foremen to make up their pay- sheets according by, and certify as to time worked and amount due to each workman, and the wages to be paid to,the foremen:every Friday. It would be unnecessary for toe Councillors to sign the paysheets as their signatures were not now required by the auditor, but they would heve every facility for inspection at the office. With reference to the payment for team labour, be considered the present system was the most satisfactory that could be devised. Referring to the workmen working on Saturday after- noons he begged to say that they had a very great ob- jection to doing so, and would be pleased if the Council would continue the hours as at present. They were very agreeable to work the threalhours extra per week according to the new regulations and he felt sure the Council would do well to make them this slight conces- sion. They were a class of respectable strong, able- bodied men, with years of experierlise in every kind of road work aad he should be sorry to lose them after the great pains he had bestowed ors their training. Two had already left and he was afraid there was a large amount of unrest among the others.</p>
        <p>THE SUGGESTED CONTRACT SYSTEM. The SURVEYOR in adverting to Mr. Tharnton's pro- posal teat all the Council's work should be done by con- tract said it woald be most unwise to de so. At present the Council's work was done in the best manner and at the least coat by a highly efficient staff of workmen, whom he should be sorry to see disbanded, as it would take years to replace them. The condition of the Coun- cil's roads spoke for itself, toe main roads ranked high and their cost of maintenance was below the average in this part of the county, and he very much doub:Ed whether the County:Council would agree to their contract being sub-let. To lOC off their maintenance to contractors would be a retrogressive and co4!y step, the condition of the roads would deteriorate from want of that unremitting attention, so essential to good mainten- ance. and he felt sure the experiment would end in dis- appointment and loss, caiosing increased rates, and an unsatisfactory condition of the roads. Tnere were few contractors who were really conversant with road main- tenance, and it WAS entirety oot of the question to think that use madmen could undertake such work, as they were unacquainted with the financial part of the bust- neos, and, again it would be undesirable to have work- men masters, therefore be hoped that the Council would see the advantages of retaining the present system. The CLERK said Mr. Thornton had written him that he was unable to be present. It was decided to defer the matter. THE SUGGESTED IMPROVEMENTS AT VULCAN AND BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>The SURVEYOR reported that on the 26th inst. he called upon Mr. Langton to drive him over the Vulcan private road, and also to call upon Mr. Collingwood.ttc manager of the Vulcan Foundry. Mr. Longton being away he did the business alone. Mr. Collingwood went over the nad with him and he (the Surveyor) pointed out several places that might be considerably improved Mr. Cnllingwood informed him that his Company would bear half the cost of widening the bridge across the brook, subject to his directors' approval of plans and estimates, which he would like to have early. If Mr. E. W.Sankey would give the necessary land, between the brook and the Foundry Company's bermdary, to widen the approach road on their side, they would set back the fence, and after he had had an interview with Mr. Sankey be was to let Mr. Collingwood know the result. The Company were ales agreeable to the road being widened at the point where the bank prejeoted out, by the siding, providing that the Radway Company would continue their retain log wall as far as necessary and they hoped to hear that the Railway Company would do. Mr. Collingwood also informed Shim that his Company were about to do with• out the pair of rails next to the road at the siding , which would be a great improvement. Whilst on the ground he examined the railway briage, and he thought it couli be widened and more headway given, if the Railway Company could be prevailed upon to do the work. He proposed to see Mr. Andrews, the divisional engineer, with regard to both this mattv and the re- taining wall before mentioned. He considered that it would be very desirable to make application,next April to the main roads committee of the County Council to have the Conneirs portion of the road made a secondary road. He also called on Mr. Shirley, at the Town Flail Newtoc, and informed him that the Council thought it would be unwise to spend a large ram of money on the private road from Newton Common Lock to Hal Lane, Burton wood. as it would only be tem- perary, and that the matter had better stand over for two or three years to see whether the land-owners and the Main Roads Committee would co-onerate in making a main road from Hall Lane to near Earlestown Station. Mr. Shirley promised to report the matter to his commit- tee _ _</p>
        <p>Thn report on new buildings was adopted and the Conacil rose.</p>
        <p>FRODSFIAII PAROCHIAL COMIIITT EE.</p>
        <p>The Parish Council of Frodsham, acting as a Parochial Committee, held their monthly meeting in the Court Rom, and by adjournment in the Overseers' Office, on Friday evenieg, there being present Messrs Charles E Linaker (chairman), Eirlam (vice-chairman), Barrow, Shsre, Illidge, P Booth, Rothivell, Wilkinsou, Kydd, Rolgers, Williams, and Davies; Ashton (clerk), Farring- ton (inPpector), and Diggle (eurveyor.) The Clerk reported that he had written to Sir G F Mares. vice-admiral and acting conservator of the river Mersey, with respect t) the washing away of the em- binkment on the quay, Frodsham Bridge, and had re- ceived a reply inquiring what legal powers the Coun- cil supplies the c mservators possess to prevent such en- croachment. The matter was allowed to drop.—The C ark reported that it had been found that it was not customary for Water to be Taken from the mains of one township-to extinguish a fire in another township, and in all probability the township of Frodsham would b, paid for the water consumed by the Runcorn Brigade at the farm fire in Netherton. This was considered satis- factory.—fhe question of a water supply to cottagers Iveping cows wai agaiu discus.ied, and the matter was referred to the Water Committee to fix the rate to be levied upon all persons who were not assess, d at a rateable value of 15.-011 the proposition of Mr. Kydd, seconded by Mr. Badge, it was resolved that application be made to the Local Government Board t ) put in fore() the Towns Police Clauses of 1847. A long discussion took place with re- ference to a modified clause as to the width of a street which would be a S coridary means of access to new cottage property, and on ten votes to two it was agreed that the modification of 1:4 law 9, allowing the street to to) of the width of 10 feet be adopted.—The Inseector reported that he hid submitted samples of water from the Ttodiharn mains and the Frodsham Bridge to the county analyst, and both were certified as good water and fit for domestic use.</p>
        <p>_../,,_.. A DisAvowAi..</p>
        <p>We are requested to state by Mr. Thomas Dain- tith, farmer, Woolston, that he is not the Thomas Daintith, charged with being drunk at Poul!;on, as reported in our last issue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL HARD LINES TOR THE VISITORS</p>
        <p>Warring*on j ,uraeyed to Rua on Wednesday for the purpose of playing c.he club which sports the black jer- seys, is a friendly match. The journey to the Third Port is a very long and tedious one, occupying about four hours and a quieter, and as the game which was played only consisted of two thirties, there was a total of eight hours and a• half travelling for one hour's play. Moreover as Warrington had one three-quarter injured and annther ordered off the field, the visit was not of unalloyed pleasure. The weather was, however, very fine, a circurnrance which has not been usually the case in Warrington's previous visits to Hull, and as the fair—one of the largest of its kind in England—was in progress, the visit shun as it was, was very enjoyable notwithstanding the above mentioned events. Warrington took their selected team viz., Ashurst, full back ; Wood, Jones, Isherwood, and Harris, three-quarters ; Bate and Duckworth, half- backs ; Naylor, Sualow, Taylor, Eden. Swift, Fairhurst, Scholtze, and Boardman, forwards. The Hull club only chose a moderate team, in view of a csmpetition match with Wakefield Trinity to-day. It was composed as fol- lows :—Sillis, fug back ; Meek, Tanner. Mahoney, and Jacques, three-quarters; Savage and Burrell. half Vacks; Cornish 130111165, Migee' Forskaw,Rippon, Hamer, For- syth, and Forbes, forwards. Mr. Sedman of Liversedge vas the referee.</p>
        <p>Jones kicked off for Warrington, and toc eager follow- ing up ofiSillis' return, caused Hull to be ruled off-side, and the first : scrimmage was on the home quarter line. The Warringten forwards were in floe form, and won every pack. The ball was Quickly transferred to the backs, and Hull were driven back. A round of passing between Bate, Duckworth. Isherwood, and Harris, almost ended in the wing man getting over, Meek just barring his progress in the nick of time. Still keeping up strong pressure, Bate whipped out the ball to Isherwood, who, four minutes from the start,put the ball safely over the fine. Jones failed at the place. The taking of the tall to the half-way line was the first time Hull had been in that portion of the field since the commencement A smart follow up was made of For- sbaw's kick-off, with the result that Isherwood was bowled over and lost possession, but Ashuest kept the ball from getting into dangerous quarters, and Bate and Duckworth getting the hall, it was transferred to Isher- wood, who made a fine run to half-way, and Jones gained still further ground. Meek, however, got pos- session, and made a grand run along touch. It locked odds on him scoring, but Harris finally settled him. The referee was acting in a very peculiar manner, and for some mysterious reason, a free kick was awarded Hull in a tolerably easy position. Jacquss however failed to kick a goal, and the ball went dead. From the drop out Wood made a fine run along touch, but he was finally knocked out of field of play. Bate got possession from the touch kick, and made a splendid run almost half length of the field nut no one was ready to receive his pass, and thus a fine ebance went a bogging. A few minutes later, passing between Bate, Duckworth, Isherwood and Harris, ended in the latter scoring an unconverted try. A misfortune now betel Warrington, for Jones was injured in the thigh P.nd had to leave the field. He came on again for a few minutes, but bad to leave again. Eden then went wing and Wood centre. With their weakened forces,Warring- ton were driven back,and much astonishment was occa- sioned by Bate being penalised for following up his own kick, and an he did not rush back at once to take his place behind the scrimmage, he was penalised for being off-side. Jacques again failed at goal. Bate again broke away and kicked across and Duckwortn unfortunately stumbled when only a yard from the line. From the resulting scrimmage Meek broke away and passed across to Tanner. The latter passed to Jacques who knocked forward fully six yards. He was however allowed to pick up again and throw hack to Meek who dashed over the line. Jacques kicked a goal. Immediately after the restart, Cornish charged down a kick by Ashurst, and following up scored another try which Jacques con- verted. Half time thus arrived with Hull leading by two goals, two tries, to two tries. Forshaw restarted, and Mahoney almost dropped a goal the first minute, Hull with a man more on the field pressed hotly, and Taylor made his mark on the line, which saved a try. The play became very uninteresting, free kicks being awarded by the referee with too great frequency. Fre.HTl a free kick by Taylor, Fairhurst rushed up and got possession, and had a clear field be- fore him. Lack of speed, however, lost a try. Isher- wood made a dashing run but was unsupported, and nothing tangible resulted. Another misfortune now be- fel Warrington, for Harris following up a kick, tackled the full-back just as the latter kicked the ball, and though there was no roughness, he was ordered off the field, and Fairhurst had to be taken out of the scrim- mage to fill his place. With only thirteen men,Warring- ton pluckily maintained the struggle, and a fine passing run by Bate end Duckworth took play to-the Hull line. A desperate scrimmage took place, and the six Warring- ton forwards beat the eight Hull men for possession,with the result that Bate got the ball and unfortunately grounded a foot from the line. Again the ball came out on the Warrington side, and Duckworth grounded it over the line, but the referee refused to grant a per- fectly legitimate try, much to the evident disgust of Dunkwortb. A third scrimmage took place in the same place, and again the ball came out on the Warrington side. Bate got possession and forced himself over, the try this time being allowed. The place was not a difficult one, and as there was only a minute to play, victory or defeat depended on the goal kick. Isherwood was deputed to do duty, but to the joy of the Hull spectators, the ball grazed the underside of the cross har, aid thus Hull won luckily by two goals, two tries (10 points) to three tries (9 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>RUNCORN DEFEAT HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN DEFEAT HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>In a guarantee match played at Hull, on Wednesday last, Runcorn maintained their undefeated record, and beat their opponents by a try to nil. The try was Ecored after a magnificent display by J Faulkner. The fine ex- hibition of the game given by the •'Linnets" quite de- lighted the 6,000 spectators or so, who assembled to witness it, and nothing but praise is being bestowed upon what c.in now be described the finest rugby com- bination in England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>017R2LOCAL CaIIRCHES.</head>
        <p>017R2LOCAL CaIIRCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Y.mge presided at the meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. There was an aver- age attendance. The speaker for the occasion was Mr W Beecher Smith, of Manchester, who drew some useful lessons from the name Gideon—a des- troyer of iniquity. Mr. A. Bennett contributed a violin solo and an effective rendering of "The Sailors Grave" was given by Mr. Horton, of Leeds. Mr. F. Hickman presided at the organ. The lesson was read by Mr. Whetnall, and Mr. F. Brown offered prayer. It was announced that the prizes would be distributed at the open meeting to be held to•morrow( Sunday.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>In the absence of the Rev. W. Bracecamp, the napn's service was c,anducted on Sunday afternoon in Sc. Peter's Church, by Mr. Thomas S. Steel, who also delivered an address on "The Lif a of Christ." There was a go.)d attendance, and two solos were creditably rendered by Mr. Thomas Reddish. Mr. Winterbotham also Rave a recitation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>GOSPEL HALL, FORSTER-STRETT.</head>
        <p>GOSPEL HALL, FORSTER-STRETT.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night, Messrs. W, Roden and J. Green preached the G )spel illustrating it by the stor s of Cain and Abel, who are two representative men, types of two classes of people who live to-day. One class, like Cain, seek to please God and gain Heaven by their own good works, Cain brought the fruit of a cursed earth and'G.A could not accept it. Mn t!-day presented t 3 God their good works fsreettine that all our riehtenusness are as filthy rags in the sight of God. Abel brraight a slain Lamb, signifying that his misdeeds deserved death but the Lamb dies in We plac,, and theref -,re he is ac,,opted because his punishment is borne by another—a clear picture of the Lamb of God. Jesus Christ, who died as our substitute and now givks acceptance with Goi and Eternßl Life to all who accept him. The quistion is now asked " What think ye of Ct,rist P"a question wor:h the consideration of all, for upon the answer depends eternal destiny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of the winter session was held last Friday and Dr White delivered a lecture on "A walk in Skye." Prefacing his discourse with a sketch of the general nature and probable origin of the old rocks of 511 rye and of their relation to similar formations in otter parts of the world. Dr. White. in imagination, successfully conducted a field club walk from Bradford Bay across to the shore of Loch Eishart, thence by a hill path to Loch Slapin, and so by road back to Broadford. He des- cribed the various geological strata, whose uptilted edges were crossed en route or whose sections were exposed in sea cliff or river gorge ; especially dilating on the many volcanic remains which ti!l reveal to the seeing eye somewhat of the subterraner.n forces which during successive epochs, from fissure or crater, buried under thick lava floods great tracts of country, or bidden below the surface rocks, distorted and thrust up these into mighty mountain bosses. He enliv- ened the strictly geological portion of his lecture with notes on the botanical and zoological rarities to be seen in the ramble, and at the close was warmly congratulated on the readiness with which he had placed at the service of the club the know- ledge acquired and observations made during his holidays. The subject was illustrated most effec- tively with maps, diagfems and a series of photo- graphs. The next lecture will he on "Some effects of environment," by Mr. A. J. Jolley.</p>
        <p>A-7,1i for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATSRS.—Advt.</p>
