        <p>LATEST NE W 4 .</p>
        <p>THE WAR. RECALL CF VIE TRA NS'v'A AL AGENT IN LONDON. The Central News says that Mr. Mentagu White, the representative in Londcn of the South African Republic, has been recalled by his Government. BRITISH AGENT TAKES LEAVE OF PRESIDENT KRUGER. PRETORIA, WEDNESDAY, 5.45 P.M. Mr. Conyng,ham Greene, the Bridal Agent, took leave of President Kruger and the State officials this afternoon. He leave-, I y special train to-night on his way to Cape Colony. THE BOERS AND THEIR PLANS. NEWCASTLE, WEDNESDAY. 4 P.M. Native spies report that the Boers' camp at Volkernat was struck at dawn to-day. The commando, with 3,000 men, marched in the direction of Pogwani Mountain, on the summit of which numberless teats can be seen. Trenches have ben dug in places, and the mount.in has been strongly fortified in ether ways. One large gun can be seen in position there. CABLEGRAM FROM KRUGER. "A PRICE WHICH WILL STAGGER HUMANITY." Naw YORK, THURSDAY. President Kruger has sent the following, cablegram to the " World" : Through the " World " I thank Americans most sincerely for their sympathy. Last Monday the Republic gave England forty-eight hours notice within which to give the Republic an assurance that the present dispute would be settled by arbitration or other Peaceful means, and that troops would be re- moved from the borders:. This expires at five to-day. The British Agent has been recalled. War is certain. The Republics are determined if they must belong to England that a price will have to be paid which will stagger humanity. They have, however, full faith that the sun of liberty will arise in South Africa as it arose in North America. (Signed) President KRUGER. STOCK EXCHANGE CHEEK. The " irrepressibles " of the Loudon Stcck Exchange on Wednesday sent the following message to President Kruger :—" From Buller and Co., London, to Kruger. Pretoria. Fcr what you are about to receive may the Lord make you truly thankful." BRITISH ADVANCE FROM MAFEKING. MAFEKING, THURSDAY. A erong force has just moved out ot the town towards the border, taking big guns and an ambulance equip- ment. LADY CYCLIST KIL;..ED. A young lady named Lewis, residing at Snodland, Kent, while cycling on Wednesday at Rochester, collided with a baker's cart. Her skull was fractured, and she died yestei day. LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST The following forecast of the weather for the ensuing twenty-four hours in the north-west of England (includ- ing Manchester. Liverpool, and Holyhead) was issued at the Meteorolog'cal Office at three o'clock yesterday after- noon : Westerly to scuth westerly or southerly winds. Fair at first, some rain later. THE AMERICA CUP. Yesterday's race was like the three previous one's, abandoned through lack of wind. Thi ro was a consider- able amount of fog. LORD C. BERESFORD PREVENTS A PANIC. Tha steamer Fletcher, bearing Sir I'. Lipton's guests collided with a ferryboat in the fog. Lord C. Bezesford took command and prevented a panic. DEATH OF LORD FA.RRER. The Press Asoocietion learns that Lord Farrer died at Dorking from heart disease on Wednesday. HEAVY FINE FOR BETTING. faßobert Crornarty. a cooper, was fined £5O at Berwick yesterday, tor u4itig his cooperage for betting put- poses,</p>
